parts:
- text: "Submitting a parsimonious universal baseline with mean + length-normalized LSE pooling and a single 1D Conv embed.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Mean+LSE (length-normalized) pooling + 1D Conv embed — parsimonious baseline\"\ntags: \"baseline, pooling, logsumexp, mean, conv1d, parsimony\"\nabstract: \"A minimal, dataset-agnostic architecture: a single 1D Conv embedding over one-hot RNA, followed by concatenated mean and length-normalized log-sum-exp pooling (temperature τ=0.5). A small two-layer head produces logits/outputs. No positional encoding or dataset-specific logic. Intended as a strong, simple baseline across all 7 tasks.\"\ncontent: |\n  \"\"\"\n  Parsimonious universal RNA model:\n  - Single 1D Conv embed over one-hot RNA\n  - Global pooling via concat(mean, length-normalized log-sum-exp) with temperature tau\n  - Small 2-layer head to task outputs (logits for classification; linear for regression)\n  - No positional encodings; no dataset-specific branches\n\
    \  \"\"\"\n  from typing import Dict, Any\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  from jax.scipy.special import logsumexp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n\n  # Constants (used by the Station)\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'hidden_dim': 256,\n          'kernel_size': 7,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.10,\n          'tau': 0.5\n      }\n\n  class ConvMeanLSEPool(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int\n      task_type: str = \"regression\"\n      hidden_dim: int = 256\n      kernel_size: int = 7\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.10\n      tau: float = 0.5\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic: bool = True):\n          # x: (batch, seq_len, 4)\n          # 1D Conv embed over sequence (SAME padding preserves length)\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.hidden_dim, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding=\"SAME\")(x)\n          h\
    \ = jax.nn.silu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Global pooling\n          mean_pool = jnp.mean(h, axis=1)  # (batch, hidden_dim)\n\n          # Length-normalized log-sum-exp pooling with temperature tau\n          # v_lse = tau * (logsumexp(h/tau, axis=1) - log(L))\n          L = h.shape[1]\n          L_const = jnp.array(L, dtype=h.dtype)\n          lse_pool = self.tau * (logsumexp(h / self.tau, axis=1) - jnp.log(L_const))  # (batch, hidden_dim)\n\n          pooled = jnp.concatenate([mean_pool, lse_pool], axis=-1)  # (batch, 2*hidden_dim)\n\n          # Small two-layer head\n          z = nn.Dense(self.hidden_dim)(pooled)\n          z = jax.nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(z)  # logits for classification; linear for regression\n\n          # Shape handling\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\" and self.d_output\
    \ == 1:\n              return z.squeeze(-1)  # (batch,)\n          else:\n              return z  # (batch, d_output)\n\n  class WrappedRNANetwork:\n      \"\"\"Wrapper exposing init/apply API required by the Station.\"\"\"\n      def __init__(self, d_output=1, task_type=\"regression\", hidden_dim=256, kernel_size=7, dropout_rate=0.10, tau=0.5):\n          self.network = ConvMeanLSEPool(\n              d_output=d_output,\n              task_type=task_type,\n              hidden_dim=hidden_dim,\n              kernel_size=kernel_size,\n              dropout_rate=dropout_rate,\n              tau=tau\n          )\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key},\n                                        dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n \
    \         if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      \"\"\"Create the minimal universal RNA model with mean + length-norm LSE pooling.\"\"\"\n      d_output = hparams.get(\"d_output\", 1)\n      task_type = hparams.get(\"task_type\", \"regression\")\n      hidden_dim = int(hparams.get(\"hidden_dim\", 256))\n      kernel_size = int(hparams.get(\"kernel_size\", 7))\n      dropout_rate = float(hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\", 0.10))\n      tau = float(hparams.get(\"tau\", 0.5))\n      return WrappedRNANetwork(\n          d_output=d_output,\n          task_type=task_type,\n          hidden_dim=hidden_dim,\n          kernel_size=kernel_size,\n          dropout_rate=dropout_rate,\n\
    \          tau=tau\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01)\n\n  def complete(params, opt_state, trial_data):\n      # Lightweight logging for lineage records\n      ds = trial_data.get('dataset')\n      vm = trial_data.get('val_metric')\n      seed = trial_data.get('seed')\n      print(f\"[Complete] Dataset={ds}  ValMetric={vm:.4f}  Seed={seed}\")\n```"
thinking_content: "**Planning next steps for Noema II**\n\nI need to outline our next steps for Noema II. We've reviewed the task, storage information, defaults, and a training template from the lineage. The leaderboard shows a state-of-the-art score of around 0.6866 from Noema I submissions. Our goal is to propose a new submission that emphasizes parsimony and insight. Starting with a simple improvement to the MLP baseline could be a good move, perhaps by adding global pooling techniques or variations.**Considering implementation options**\n\nThe system's default network is a straightforward MLP that uses mean pooling. I'm thinking it could be beneficial to introduce a \"Mean + LogSumExp\" dual aggregator with learned weights. This might improve predictions across tasks with minimal code. However, we have to be careful about the guidelines regarding collaboration and use from lineage storage. Although we can read and write in our lineage storage, I still need to confirm if we can use code\
  \ from our lineage overall.**Navigating code usage rules**\n\nThe Research Counter's \"Storage\" seems to allow read-only imports from any lineage, but low-level collaboration like importing code is forbidden. However, we can review and clone code through official channels. It’s clear that we can use our lineage's code. The baseline shows state-of-the-art methods using dsconv/resmix, but I don't want to rely too heavily on those. For our initial step, I could propose a small architecture like \"length-sensitive pooling using mean + LSE with a tau hyperparameter,\" which fits within our lineage templates. But the training code has a hardcoded loss function issue.**Implementing submission code**\n\nI need to get started on the submission code. This includes creating the network and defining hyperparameters, as well as setting up the optimizer. The state-of-the-art dsconv-based 'No-PE' with a pooling aggregator is currently scoring around 0.68, but I think we could simplify things with a\
  \ cleaner aggregator improvement. One option could be the \"PrePool Feature-Scored MeanMax\" aggregator, where I’d transform one-hot encodings using a 1D convolution to compute mean and max across time with a learned gate. I’ll have to ensure our network meets the expected output requirements.**Deciding on submission code**\n\nI need to decide on submission code content. We could use our lineage's 'submit_lse_template.py', which imports some heavy modules, but I have to ensure compatibility with the current system. It might be safer to define a \"test()\" function for validation, but since we want to run training for scoring, I’ll avoid that. I'm considering proposing a simpler aggregator improvement, like \"Mean+Max\" with a length-wise LSE pooling. I’m planning to implement a straightforward network in JAX/Flax that accepts variable dimensions and applies a depthwise convolution, dropout, and pooling aggregators, aiming for a minimal yet effective architecture.**Implementing simplified\
  \ architecture**\n\nI want to keep things simple, so I’m considering using an MLP with a hidden dimension of twice the hidden_dim. My plan is to create a head that outputs dimensions through a sequence of dense layers. However, since flax.linen doesn’t have nn.Sequential, I’ll code them sequentially. When dealing with multi-class classification, I’ll ensure the output logits match for training cross-entropy, sticking to a shape format. Plus, I’ll be implementing pooling strategies, including a length-normalized logsumexp to maintain scale invariance across diverse sequence lengths. \n\nNow, I also need to define optimizer and hyperparameters carefully. I want to focus on parsimony, aiming for an architecture that's effective but not overly complex. The final submission code will implement all of this, along with tags and an abstract. It’s exciting to see the potential outcome!**Finalizing the implementation**\n\nI'm moving forward with my model architecture! I’ve decided not to use dilations\
  \ or Res blocks to keep things simpler. I’ll incorporate a dropout layer and ensure I respect the max sequence length of 100 for the ncRNA dataset, despite previous versions using a different length. For multitask handling, I’ll be careful with shape changes and consistency during pooling. \n\nI’ll create optimizer functions with clear definitions and maintain the use of activation functions properly. As I structure the code, I’m also considering the importance of defining constants, like BATCH_SIZE, to fit the system expectations. After I've crafted the YAML for submission, I’ll double-check to ensure everything is correctly formatted before submitting!**Finalizing logsumexp implementation**\n\nI’m realizing that I need to use `jax.scipy.special.logsumexp` instead of `jax.nn.logsumexp`, which isn't standard. I’ll import `logsumexp` from `jax.scipy.special` for my calculations. For the length-normalized LSE, I’ll compute it using this function and ensure that I handle the shapes and types\
  \ properly to prevent dtype mismatches. After finalizing the optimizer function and crafting the YAML block for submission, I'll double-check to ensure everything is ready to go before hitting submit!"
