tick: 302
role: model
parts:
- text: "Great first result. Your minimal Conv + mean+LSE model is clearly stronger than the vanilla baseline and provides a clean foundation for ablations.\n\nPlan:\n- Ablate the pooling aggregator to isolate where gains come from:\n  1) mean+max vs mean+LSE (len-norm)\n  2) mean-only\n  3) mean+LSE without length normalization (optional third)\n\nI’ll write a reusable module to storage, then submit two ablations now.\n\n`/execute_action{storage write noema/submissions/conv_pool_baseline.py}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  \"\"\"\n  Minimal 1D Conv embedding with configurable global pooling for multi-dataset RNA tasks.\n  Aggregators:\n    - 'mean': mean pooling\n    - 'mean_max': concat(mean, max)\n    - 'mean_lse': concat(mean, LSE) with optional length normalization\n      hparams['length_normalize_lse']: bool (default True)\n      hparams['tau']: temperature for LSE (default 0.5)\n  \"\"\"\n  from typing import Dict, Any\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n\
    \  from jax.scipy.special import logsumexp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n\n  # Station-observed constants\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      # Default hyperparameters; submissions can override fields\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'hidden_dim': 256,\n          'kernel_size': 7,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.10,\n          'aggregator': 'mean_lse',\n          'length_normalize_lse': True,\n          'tau': 0.5,\n      }\n\n  class ConvEmbedPool(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int\n      task_type: str = \"regression\"\n      hidden_dim: int = 256\n      kernel_size: int = 7\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.10\n      aggregator: str = \"mean_lse\"\n      length_normalize_lse: bool = True\n      tau: float = 0.5\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic: bool = True):\n          # x: (batch, seq_len, 4)\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.hidden_dim, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,),\
    \ padding=\"SAME\")(x)\n          h = jax.nn.silu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Global pooling\n          mean_pool = jnp.mean(h, axis=1)  # (batch, hidden_dim)\n\n          if self.aggregator == 'mean':\n              pooled = mean_pool\n          elif self.aggregator == 'mean_max':\n              max_pool = jnp.max(h, axis=1)\n              pooled = jnp.concatenate([mean_pool, max_pool], axis=-1)\n          elif self.aggregator == 'mean_lse':\n              L = h.shape[1]\n              lse_core = logsumexp(h / self.tau, axis=1)\n              if self.length_normalize_lse:\n                  lse_core = lse_core - jnp.log(jnp.array(L, dtype=h.dtype))\n              lse_pool = self.tau * lse_core\n              pooled = jnp.concatenate([mean_pool, lse_pool], axis=-1)\n          else:\n              raise ValueError(f\"Unknown aggregator: {self.aggregator}\")\n\n          # Small two-layer head\n          z = nn.Dense(self.hidden_dim)(pooled)\n\
    \          z = jax.nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(z)\n\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\" and self.d_output == 1:\n              return z.squeeze(-1)\n          else:\n              return z\n\n  class WrappedRNANetwork:\n      def __init__(self, d_output=1, task_type=\"regression\", hparams: Dict[str, Any] = None):\n          hparams = hparams or {}\n          self.network = ConvEmbedPool(\n              d_output=d_output,\n              task_type=task_type,\n              hidden_dim=int(hparams.get('hidden_dim', 256)),\n              kernel_size=int(hparams.get('kernel_size', 7)),\n              dropout_rate=float(hparams.get('dropout_rate', 0.10)),\n              aggregator=str(hparams.get('aggregator', 'mean_lse')),\n              length_normalize_lse=bool(hparams.get('length_normalize_lse', True)),\n              tau=float(hparams.get('tau', 0.5)),\n          )\n\n  \
    \    def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key},\n                                        dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def build_network(d_output, task_type, hparams):\n      return WrappedRNANetwork(d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type, hparams=hparams)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01)\n```\n\nSubmitting Ablation\
    \ 1: mean+max pooling.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"1D Conv embed + Mean+Max pooling — parsimonious ablation\"\ntags: \"baseline, pooling, mean, max, conv1d, ablation\"\nabstract: \"Ablation of the minimal Conv baseline: replace length-normalized log-sum-exp with max pooling, using concat(mean, max). Tests whether max provides stronger nonlinearity than LSE under low-capacity convolution.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/noema/submissions')\n  from conv_pool_baseline import build_network, create_optimizer\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'hidden_dim': 256,\n          'kernel_size': 7,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.10,\n          'aggregator': 'mean_max'\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return build_network(hparams.get('d_output', 1), hparams.get('task_type', 'regression'), hparams)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate:\
    \ float = 0.001):\n      return create_optimizer(learning_rate)\n\n  def complete(params, opt_state, trial_data):\n      print(f\"[Complete] {trial_data.get('dataset')} val_metric={trial_data.get('val_metric')}\")\n```\n\nSubmitting Ablation 2: mean-only pooling.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"1D Conv embed + Mean pooling — ablation\"\ntags: \"baseline, pooling, mean, conv1d, ablation, parsimony\"\nabstract: \"Ablation of the minimal Conv baseline: remove the second pooling path and use mean-only global pooling. Quantifies the contribution of the second pooled statistic.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/noema/submissions')\n  from conv_pool_baseline import build_network, create_optimizer\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'hidden_dim': 256,\n          'kernel_size': 7,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.10,\n          'aggregator': 'mean'\n      }\n\
    \n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return build_network(hparams.get('d_output', 1), hparams.get('task_type', 'regression'), hparams)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return create_optimizer(learning_rate)\n\n  def complete(params, opt_state, trial_data):\n      print(f\"[Complete] {trial_data.get('dataset')} val_metric={trial_data.get('val_metric')}\")\n```"
thinking_content: "**Evaluating training submissions**\n\nThe training system involves 7 parallel trials, each taking about 150 seconds, and there’s a cooldown period mentioned, though it’s not clear how long it lasts. I want to submit a small grid and use a parameterized aggregator to test different aggregator options like 'mean', 'mean_max', 'mean_lse', and 'mean_lse_len'. I should consider using persistent storage for module code to avoid inlining everything, allowing for better organization in future submissions.**Modifying storage guidelines**\n\nI’m allowed to modify storage, and that's great. The \"collaboration rules\" permit reading other lineage code, so I’ll implement a module in storage/noema/submissions/conv_pool_baseline.py that will include the architecture and aggregator options for smoother processing. We can then submit small code that utilizes aggregator selection for tri-run seeds. I'm curious how the base random seed for training is chosen; it looks like in our log,\
  \ 'BASE_SEED: 42' is defined as the constant from our submission.**Planning seed and ablation strategy**\n\nIt looks like seeds for each dataset are created based on a combination of base and dataset index, like 'Seed=727'. We probably can’t control the tri-seed for the entire run easily. However, I can set the BASE_SEED to 7 or 123 to replicate seeds. Since it’s one evaluation per dataset, I think avoiding multi-seed due to cooldown makes sense. Now, for our ablation studies, I’m considering Variation 1: testing without length normalization in LSE, which could affect performance.**Evaluating ablation variations**\n\nIn each dataset, we have a constant length, which makes length normalization likely only shift the scale relative to tau. Without -log L, the logsumexp will grow with L, but the uniformity should keep the relative ranking stable, though it might affect classification and regression synergy.\n\nFor my variations:  \n- Variation 2 will use a mean+max aggregator, resembling the\
  \ previous SOTA baseline.  \n- Variation 3 will use just the mean as a baseline.  \n- Variation 4 will test different hidden dimensions: 128, 256, and 512.  \n- Variation 5 will consider adding a second conv block while keeping it simple.  \n- Variation 6 will explore various dropout rates.  \n- Variation 7 will compare gelu and silu activations.**Designing aggregator variations**\n\nInitially, I want to iterate quickly, focusing on aggregator changes, specifically testing mean+max against mean+lse. I noticed our current performance metrics show room for improvement. For our variations, I’ll first try switching to mean+max and then test variations like using mean only, and checking the effects of not normalizing LSE.\n\nI'm considering using dilated convolutions to increase receptive field without losing simplicity. I'll create a module for our submissions, implementing these variations while keeping the sequential submission strategy in mind to avoid saturating cooldown. Let's proceed\
  \ with the detailed plans for these tests!**Planning submission strategy**\n\nI need to carefully manage the token budget as writing large code multiple times increases usage. I plan to queue 2-3 submissions sequentially, using a smaller wrapper to reference shared code. The aggregator selection will be defined within hyperparameters, including options for mean, max, and lse pooling.\n\nI’ll implement the various aggregator configurations and ensure the module handles classification outputs correctly. The ultimate plan is to submit two ablations first and then address the effects of tau in further tests, keeping an eye on potential cooldown periods between submissions. Let's move forward with this approach!**Organizing results and submissions**\n\nI see there's a \"results\" directory with multiple files. I’m thinking about appending to those, but I need to create a new log file while keeping the headers intact. However, I can’t easily append due to the environment's limitations. Instead,\
  \ I'll create a fresh file named 'results/conv_pool_baseline_1.csv' for simplicity.\n\nNext up, I'll store the code and queue up two submissions, focusing on the 'mean_max' aggregator for improved metrics. I'll make sure to handle technical details properly, like importing necessary functions. Now, let’s move forward with the commands!"
