- text: "The LSE ablations confirmed: length normalization is crucial; τ=0.25 improves average vs τ=0.5 and lifts ncRNA/MRL, while mean+max still leads overall. Next, I’ll run tri-seed stability for mean+max and LSE τ=0.25.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"1D Conv embed + Mean+Max pooling — tri-seed replication (seed=7)\"\ntags: \"baseline, pooling, mean, max, conv1d, replication, stability\"\nabstract: \"Tri-seed replication for the best dual-path pooling variant: single 1D Conv + concat(mean, max). Tests stability across seeds; this run sets BASE_SEED=7.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/noema/submissions')\n  from conv_pool_baseline import build_network, create_optimizer\n\n  BASE_SEED = 7\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'hidden_dim': 256,\n          'kernel_size': 7,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.10,\n          'aggregator': 'mean_max'\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n\
    \      return build_network(hparams.get('d_output', 1), hparams.get('task_type', 'regression'), hparams)\n\n  # Use imported create_optimizer directly\n\n  def complete(params, opt_state, trial_data):\n      print(f\"[Complete] {trial_data.get('dataset')} val_metric={trial_data.get('val_metric')}\")\n```\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"1D Conv embed + Mean+LSE pooling (τ=0.25, len-normalized) — tri-seed replication (seed=7)\"\ntags: \"baseline, pooling, logsumexp, temperature, conv1d, replication, stability\"\nabstract: \"Tri-seed replication for the sharpened LSE variant (τ=0.25) with length normalization. Tests stability and whether gains on ncRNA/MRL persist across seeds; this run sets BASE_SEED=7.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/noema/submissions')\n  from conv_pool_baseline import build_network, create_optimizer\n\n  BASE_SEED = 7\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n\
    \          'hidden_dim': 256,\n          'kernel_size': 7,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.10,\n          'aggregator': 'mean_lse',\n          'length_normalize_lse': True,\n          'tau': 0.25\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return build_network(hparams.get('d_output', 1), hparams.get('task_type', 'regression'), hparams)\n\n  # Use imported create_optimizer directly\n\n  def complete(params, opt_state, trial_data):\n      print(f\"[Complete] {trial_data.get('dataset')} val_metric={trial_data.get('val_metric')}\")\n```\n\n`/execute_action{storage write noema/results/conv_pool_ablation_summary.md}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # Conv + Global Pooling Ablations (Noema II) — Update (Tick 305)\n\n  Model family: single 1D Conv (hidden=256, k=7, dropout=0.1) → global pooling → 2-layer head. No positional encoding. Dataset-agnostic.\n\n  Results (EvalID — Score | APA | CRI-Off | Modif | CRI-On | PRS | MRL | ncRNA):\n  - 442 — 0.5230 | 0.6808 | 0.0521 | 0.7125 | 0.4201 | 0.3893\
    \ | 0.6172 | 0.7892  (Mean+Max)\n  - 439 — 0.5224 | 0.6738 | 0.0815 | 0.7086 | 0.3665 | 0.3963 | 0.6132 | 0.8169  (Mean+Max)\n  - 444 — 0.5152 | 0.6709 | 0.0584 | 0.7042 | 0.3593 | 0.4044 | 0.6107 | 0.7985  (Mean+LSE, τ=0.25, len-norm)\n  - 438 — 0.4993 | 0.6728 | 0.0709 | 0.7099 | 0.3789 | 0.4059 | 0.5846 | 0.6723  (Mean+LSE, τ=0.5, len-norm)\n  - 443 — 0.4389 | 0.6328 | 0.0831 | 0.7009 | 0.3552 | 0.3216 | 0.5723 | 0.4062  (Mean+LSE, no len-norm)\n  - 440 — 0.4060 | 0.6630 | 0.0699 | 0.7035 | 0.3649 | 0.2929 | 0.5574 | 0.1908  (Mean-only)\n\n  Takeaways:\n  - Dual-path pooling is essential: mean-only collapses ncRNA.\n  - Length normalization for LSE is critical across variable-length datasets; removing it severely harms ncRNA and average score.\n  - Sharper LSE (τ=0.25) improves ncRNA and MRL vs τ=0.5, yielding a higher average than τ=0.5 and close to Mean+Max.\n  - Mean+Max currently leads average score; τ=0.25 LSE is competitive and may be more stable across tasks.\n\n  Next planned:\n\
    \  - Tri-seed stability: Mean+Max and LSE τ=0.25 (BASE_SEED ∈ {7,42,123}); currently running seed=7.\n  - After stability, light capacity sweep (hidden ∈ {128,256,384}) under both pooling choices.\n  - Optional: test tiny second conv block vs wider head to attribute gains to locality vs capacity with minimal params.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{rank author}`"
thinking_content: "**Considering normalization and paths**\n\nI’m thinking about the importance of maintaining simplicity in our approach, particularly with three paths. I need to address length-normalization, especially since data shows that a lack of it can hurt performance in ncRNA tasks. It seems like keeping consistent variable lengths is key, given the dataset specifics like max lengths. Also, pooling seems to vary based on dataset, which might lead to mixing issues. I’ll need to update the results summary files with some new findings!**Planning tri-seed experiments**\n\nI'm considering proposing a tri-seed approach and running two replicates with seeds 7 and 123 for a mean+max aggregator. I realize I can use the BASE_SEED constant in my submission code, which is set to 42. We need to ensure we’re only using allowed functions like _define_hyperparameters, create_network, and others. I’ll queue these tri-seed replicates for both mean+max and mean+LSE with the specified seeds. However,\
  \ there's a note about being limited to two concurrent evaluations. So, I’ll keep that in mind!**Submitting tri-seed experiments**\n\nI'm planning to submit two tasks at a time: one mean+max with seed 7 and another tau=0.25 LSE also with seed 7. After they finish, I'll submit the ones with seed 123. I’m a bit unsure about how quickly they'll complete, but I can definitely submit and then move on to ranking. \n\nFor the content scripts, the mean+max will have BASE_SEED set to 7 while using the conv_pool_baseline with the aggregator set to 'mean_max.' For the tau=0.25 LSE, I’ll set the aggregator to mean_lse, keep len_norm true, and also use BASE_SEED at 7. I need to set up the create_optimizer properly to avoid any issues. I'll make sure to note all of this in the results summary!**Considering additional experiments**\n\nI’m contemplating a third experiment that involves using a mean + max + LSE triple aggregator. However, I want to keep features minimal for this new method to maintain\
  \ parsimony. Ablating first before adding complexity seems wise, so I won’t introduce the triple path just yet. I’m also thinking about aligning our approach with prior DSConv methods, but we should stick with the minimal 1D conv for now. \n\nNext, I’ll compute performance differences across datasets 438, 442, and 444 to identify trade-offs. I’ll update the summary file with results for 443 and 444, reaffirm that length normalization had a positive impact, and also submit the two new tri-seed experiments. Lastly, I’ll consider setting 'rank author' to view.**Preparing for tri-seed submissions**\n\nI’m considering submitting two experiments: one for mean+max pooling with seed 7 and another for the mean+LSE pooling at tau=0.25, also with seed 7. I’ll need to create the YAML for these and write code that uses BASE_SEED and sets the appropriate aggregators. \n\nNext, I should update the summary file with results from experiments 443 and 444, highlighting key takeaways about length normalization\
  \ and the performance impact of tau. I can also prepare a formatted section for submission with updated metrics. Now, it’s all about crafting this neatly without complicating anything! Let's proceed with these actions."
