tick: 306
role: model
parts:
- text: "Tri-seed runs are in progress. I’ll stage extended pooling variants for the next wave, without touching the baseline file used by running jobs.\n\n`/execute_action{storage write noema/submissions/conv_pool_experimental.py}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  \"\"\"\n  Experimental variants for Conv + Global Pooling:\n    - mean_lse_max: concat(mean, LSE_tau, max) with length-normalized LSE\n    - mean_lse_mix: learned scalar mix of mean and LSE (w0, w1), returning hidden-dim\n  Keeps architecture universal and parsimonious.\n  \"\"\"\n  from typing import Dict, Any\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  from jax.scipy.special import logsumexp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'hidden_dim': 256,\n          'kernel_size': 7,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.10,\n          'aggregator': 'mean_lse_max',   # or 'mean_lse_mix'\n\
    \          'tau': 0.25,                    # default sharper LSE\n          'length_normalize_lse': True,\n      }\n\n  def _lse_pool(h, tau, length_normalize=True):\n      L = h.shape[1]\n      core = logsumexp(h / tau, axis=1)\n      if length_normalize:\n          core = core - jnp.log(jnp.array(L, dtype=h.dtype))\n      return tau * core\n\n  class ConvEmbedPoolX(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int\n      task_type: str = \"regression\"\n      hidden_dim: int = 256\n      kernel_size: int = 7\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.10\n      aggregator: str = \"mean_lse_max\"\n      tau: float = 0.25\n      length_normalize_lse: bool = True\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic: bool = True):\n          # x: (batch, seq_len, 4)\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.hidden_dim, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding=\"SAME\")(x)\n          h = jax.nn.silu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          mean_pool = jnp.mean(h,\
    \ axis=1)                     # (batch, hidden)\n          lse_pool = _lse_pool(h, self.tau, self.length_normalize_lse)  # (batch, hidden)\n          max_pool = jnp.max(h, axis=1)                       # (batch, hidden)\n\n          if self.aggregator == 'mean_lse_max':\n              pooled = jnp.concatenate([mean_pool, lse_pool, max_pool], axis=-1)  # (batch, 3*hidden)\n          elif self.aggregator == 'mean_lse_mix':\n              # Learned global scalar mix weights w0, w1 (shared across channels)\n              w = self.param('mix_weights', lambda rng, shp: jnp.array([0.5, 0.5], dtype=mean_pool.dtype), (2,))\n              pooled = w[0] * mean_pool + w[1] * lse_pool  # (batch, hidden)\n          else:\n              raise ValueError(f\"Unknown aggregator: {self.aggregator}\")\n\n          # Two-layer head\n          z = nn.Dense(self.hidden_dim)(pooled)\n          z = jax.nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          z =\
    \ nn.Dense(self.d_output)(z)\n\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\" and self.d_output == 1:\n              return z.squeeze(-1)\n          else:\n              return z\n\n  class WrappedRNANetwork:\n      def __init__(self, d_output=1, task_type=\"regression\", hparams: Dict[str, Any] = None):\n          h = hparams or {}\n          self.network = ConvEmbedPoolX(\n              d_output=d_output,\n              task_type=task_type,\n              hidden_dim=int(h.get('hidden_dim', 256)),\n              kernel_size=int(h.get('kernel_size', 7)),\n              dropout_rate=float(h.get('dropout_rate', 0.10)),\n              aggregator=str(h.get('aggregator', 'mean_lse_max')),\n              tau=float(h.get('tau', 0.25)),\n              length_normalize_lse=bool(h.get('length_normalize_lse', True)),\n          )\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key,\
    \ 'dropout': dropout_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def build_network(d_output, task_type, hparams):\n      return WrappedRNANetwork(d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type, hparams=hparams)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01)\n```\n\nI’ll wait for the two tri-seed evaluations to finish, then:\n- Submit mean_lse_max (τ=0.25) and mean_lse_mix to test if adding max (triple concat) or mixing (scalar weights) improves over current best."
thinking_content: "**Planning action steps**\n\nI need to determine our next actions. Right now, there are two evaluations running, and I might wait for them to finish or prepare additional code. The system limits us to two concurrent evaluations, so I can monitor their results as we wait for messages. I can also use the Research Counter to access various functions like ranking and previewing evaluations. While those are running, I could explore previous notes in private memory to align with earlier data conversations.**Considering memory usage**\n\nThe help message suggested looking into Private Memory for more insight on previous architectures like dsconv and prepool. However, I need to be cautious about token usage since I'm at 21% of my limit. There are many files in Private Memory—234, to be exact—so reading through them might use too many tokens. Instead, I could preview or list them. In the meantime, I'm also at the Research Counter, and I could add more results to our summary or\
  \ work on the paper draft stored there.**Planning micro-adjustments**\n\nWith the current ablations, I want to think about making small changes, like adjusting dropout rates, head dimensions, kernel sizes, and hidden dimensions. Maybe I could increase the hidden dimension from 256 to 384 to see how it affects accuracy. I could also consider adding channel-wise gating with a scale for the pooled statistics. I need to maintain a simple design while ensuring comparability in parameter counts. While focusing on pooling aggregators, I might want to calibrate LSE pooling, possibly making it signed or using logsumexp on absolute values, although I'm not entirely sure about that last part.**Evaluating results and planning**\n\nThe results we're seeing are decent, but they still fall short of the state-of-the-art (SOTA). The top SOTA by Noema is around 0.686, while our convolution mean and max scores are only about 0.523. There seems to be stagnation since the prepool DSConv reached 0.68, which\
  \ is closer to SOTA. Since our baseline is lower but serves as a reference, I should plan on writing a negative result analysis. I could start drafting a paper in Private Memory, but the Archive Room isn’t accessible until we meet certain maturity criteria.**Planning next steps**\n\nThe Archive Room is restricted for the first 50 ticks, so publishing isn't an option right now, but I can write a draft in private memory. Therefore, I’ll preview \"paper/draft.md\" to integrate our results. Once the tri-seed runs are finished, I can set up new seeds. I also need to consider various aggregator strategies to enhance classification performance while keeping an eye on parsimony. Overall, I’ll focus on analyzing results and keeping the workflow organized in storage for future updates. Let’s get started!**Implementing aggregator plans**\n\nI’m considering implementing an aggregator that combines \"mean,\" \"max,\" and \"lse_tau025” for efficient testing. However, I should be cautious about modifying\
  \ baseline code while two tests are running since that could compromise replicability. Instead, it makes sense to stage a new experimental file for the \"mean_lse_max\" aggregator without affecting the current evaluations. I’ll prepare the code, avoiding excessive storage writes for clarity. Meanwhile, keeping track of results from \"SOTA_MONITOR.md\" could guide adjustments, but I’ll lean towards conciseness in my actions. Let's get those preparations rolling!**Updating results and preparing code**\n\nTo ensure reproducibility, I'm going to append the new results 443 and 444 to the 'conv_pool_ablation_summary.md.' We already updated that summary, so that's good! Next, I'll take some actions: I'll write the aggregator code to 'noema/submissions/conv_pool_experimental.py' and prepare a template for submission referencing 'mean_lse_max.' \n\nFor the code structure, I'll import necessary classes, implement the new aggregator as 'ConvEmbedPoolX,' and focus on definitions for 'build_network'\
  \ and 'create_optimizer.' Once I have that written, I’ll run the commands to save it while keeping things brief!"
