parts:
- text: "Submitting two experimental pooling variants to test whether combining or mixing statistics improves over current best.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"1D Conv embed + Mean+LSE+Max pooling (τ=0.25, len-norm) — triple-stat\"\ntags: \"pooling, mean, logsumexp, max, conv1d, experimental\"\nabstract: \"Triple concatenation of mean, length-normalized LSE (τ=0.25), and max pooling atop a single 1D Conv embed. Tests whether combining robust mass (LSE), extreme evidence (max), and average (mean) improves cross-dataset performance while remaining parsimonious.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/noema/submissions')\n  from conv_pool_experimental import build_network, create_optimizer as make_optimizer\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'hidden_dim': 256,\n          'kernel_size': 7,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.10,\n          'aggregator': 'mean_lse_max',\n\
    \          'tau': 0.25,\n          'length_normalize_lse': True,\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return build_network(hparams.get('d_output', 1), hparams.get('task_type', 'regression'), hparams)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return make_optimizer(learning_rate)\n\n  def complete(params, opt_state, trial_data):\n      print(f\"[Complete] {trial_data.get('dataset')} val_metric={trial_data.get('val_metric')}\")\n```\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"1D Conv embed + Learned mix(mean, LSE) pooling (τ=0.25, len-norm)\"\ntags: \"pooling, logsumexp, mixing, conv1d, parsimony\"\nabstract: \"Learned scalar mixture of mean and length-normalized LSE (τ=0.25) as a single pooled statistic, preserving dimensionality and parsimony. Tests if a data-driven mix outperforms fixed concatenation.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/noema/submissions')\n  from conv_pool_experimental import build_network, create_optimizer\
    \ as make_optimizer\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'hidden_dim': 256,\n          'kernel_size': 7,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.10,\n          'aggregator': 'mean_lse_mix',\n          'tau': 0.25,\n          'length_normalize_lse': True,\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return build_network(hparams.get('d_output', 1), hparams.get('task_type', 'regression'), hparams)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return make_optimizer(learning_rate)\n\n  def complete(params, opt_state, trial_data):\n      print(f\"[Complete] {trial_data.get('dataset')} val_metric={trial_data.get('val_metric')}\")\n```\n\n`/execute_action{storage write noema/results/conv_pool_ablation_summary.md}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # Conv + Global Pooling Ablations (Noema II) — Update (Tick 307)\n\n  Family: single 1D Conv (hidden=256, k=7, dropout=0.1) → global pooling → 2-layer\
    \ head. No positional encoding. Universal across 7 datasets.\n\n  Latest tri-seed (seed=7):\n  - 447 — Mean+Max:     0.5194 | APA 0.6799 | CRI-Off 0.0761 | Modif 0.7103 | CRI-On 0.3116 | PRS 0.3842 | MRL 0.6184 | ncRNA 0.8554\n  - 448 — LSE τ=0.25:   0.5234 | APA 0.6764 | CRI-Off 0.0681 | Modif 0.7089 | CRI-On 0.3927 | PRS 0.3963 | MRL 0.6120 | ncRNA 0.8092\n\n  Summary so far:\n  - Dual-path pooling is essential (mean-only collapses ncRNA).\n  - Length-normalized LSE is critical; removing length norm hurts ncRNA and average.\n  - τ=0.25 improves over τ=0.5; LSE τ=0.25 is slightly ahead of Mean+Max on average, while Mean+Max excels on ncRNA.\n  - Task tradeoff: Mean+Max → strongest ncRNA; LSE τ=0.25 → stronger CRI-On and PRS; both comparable on APA/MRL/Modif.\n\n  New experiments (seed=42):\n  - Mean+LSE+Max (τ=0.25, len-norm): triple-stat concatenation to combine mass + extremes + average.\n  - Learned mix(mean, LSE) (τ=0.25, len-norm): scalar-mixed single-stat to preserve parsimony.\n\
    \n  Next:\n  - Complete tri-seed stability (BASE_SEED ∈ {7,42,123}) for best variants.\n  - Light capacity sweep (hidden ∈ {128,256,384}) under Mean+Max and LSE τ=0.25.\n  - Optional: add tiny 2nd conv block vs wider head to attribute gains to locality vs capacity.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{rank author}`"
thinking_content: '**Handling evaluation results**


  I need to respond as the AI agent from the Research Counter. We have evaluation results from a tri-seed replication (seed=7) with mean and max values around 0.519 and 0.523, respectively. Since the experimental module finished quickly, we can now submit experiments for the aggregator. We may also update the summary file with these results. For the next seeds, I could consider 42 and 123, ensuring we avoid the import overshadow bug. I''m proceeding with submitting two new experiments right now!**Proposing experiment submissions**


  I can propose two submissions for our evaluation. First, there''s the mean_lse_max with tau set to 0.25 and length normalization enabled, using the aggregator ''mean_lse_max'' while ensuring hyperparameters are consistent. The second submission could be the mean_lse_mix aggregator with initial weights at 0.5. I think I''ll want to stepwise test combinations and maybe log the results. The latest outputs are encouraging, especially the mean+max on ncRNA showing improvement. Therefore, testing the mean+LSE+max triple seems like a fair next step while keeping things simple.**Planning next steps for testing**


  We might run tri-seed for seeds 42 or 123 after completing the aggregator tests, keeping in mind the limit of two concurrent evaluations. I think we can submit two aggregator tests right now. Setting BASE_SEED to 42 would help maintain comparability with the earlier work since our baseline used that same seed. The prior aggregator tests also used BASE_SEED=42, which makes sense to stick with for consistency. So I''ll go ahead and choose BASE_SEED=42 for the new aggregator tests!**Finalizing aggregation tests and next steps**


  I''m confirming that for 442, we used "BASE_SEED = 42." The 444 analysis with tau=0.25 also had BASE_SEED set to 42. For our aggregator tests, I’ll use this base seed as well. I think it’ll be helpful to use the "test()" function for quicker validation, but we want thorough evaluation. I’ll also update the summary file with tri-seed results for 447 and 448. Finally, I can submit the ''mean_lse_max'' and ''mean_lse_mix'' aggregators with tau=0.25 and length normalization!**Computing early insights**


  I''m noticing some early insights regarding tri-seed results: the mean+max performed well for ncRNA, while LSE with tau=0.25 showed significant improvement on CRI-On, going from 0.3116 to 0.3927. It seems LSE is better for CRI-On, and mean+max is better for ncRNA, with both performing similarly for MRL. For PRS, LSE is 0.3963 versus 0.3842. The mean+LSE with tau 0.25 tri-seed has the highest overall score at 0.52337, slightly above mean+max at 0.519. Let''s implement the synergy of triple pooling!'
