tick: 308
role: model
parts:
- text: "Two experiments are running. While waiting, I’ll send a high-level note to Axiom III.\n\n`/execute_action{goto mail}`\n`/execute_action{help mail}`\n`/execute_action{reply 1}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Hi Axiom III — thank you for the warm welcome and for sharing the inspiration behind Averaged MoE.\n\n  Quick summary of my first wave with a minimal single-conv model and global pooling (dataset-agnostic):\n  - Mean-only pooling collapses ncRNA (accuracy ~0.19), confirming dual-path pooling is essential.\n  - Mean+Max: ~0.522–0.523 average; excels on ncRNA (0.79–0.86) and MRL.\n  - Mean+LSE (length-normalized): τ=0.5 ~0.499; τ=0.25 ~0.515–0.523. Length normalization is critical; removing it hurts ncRNA and average.\n  - Task tradeoff: Mean+Max favors ncRNA; τ=0.25 LSE improves CRI-On and PRS and is comparable on APA/MRL/Modif.\n\n  Currently testing:\n  - Triple-stat concat(mean, LSE_τ=0.25 len-norm, max)\n  - Learned scalar mix of mean and LSE_τ=0.25 (keeps dimension small; parsimonious)\n\
    \n  Two questions (high-level):\n  1) In your experience with Averaged MoE, do “extreme evidence” signals (max-like) vs “mass evidence” (LSE/soft aggregation) differentially affect stability across CRISPR tasks? I see sharper τ helps CRI-On.\n  2) For concatenated pooled stats, did you find it useful to calibrate post-pooling scales (e.g., light normalization or gating) to avoid one stat dominating gradients? I’m trying to stay parsimonious but may test a single post-pool layer norm if scale mismatch is a concern.\n\n  I’ll follow up with tri-seed stability and capacity sweeps (hidden ∈ {128,256,384}) once the current runs finish. Any conceptual advice on pooling across variable-length RNAs is appreciated.\n  \n  — Noema II\n```"
thinking_content: "**Continuing experimentation**\n\nI need to keep working in the Station since we have new experimental results. Right now, we’re running two experiments. Once those wrap up, I might plan for additional replicates and capacity sweeps. It’s also important to publish analyses regularly. I see that at tick 308, we can start drafting a paper, but the archive won't be available until maturity. Still, I can use the Private Memory Room. Collaborating with Axiom III on high-level results sounds good too, but I should prioritize focusing on the Research Counter.**Evaluating improvements in accuracy**\n\nI’ve been considering how absolute improvements with mean+max enhance high ncRNA accuracy, which is promising. The LSE τ=0.25 seems to balance tasks effectively, improving CRI-On and PRS. However, the triple-stat variant might increase the dimensionality, making it heavier. The learnt mix seems like a good alternative that preserves parsimony. I’ll confirm that our functions, like\
  \ create_optimizer, don’t overshadow each other. Also, I noticed that our performance is still far from the current state-of-the-art, but that’s fine; I’m focusing on exploring baseline performance!**Planning next steps**\n\nOkay, I need to wait for the evaluations of experiments 452 and 453. In the meantime, I can record our results to a file and update the summary, even if we don’t have results yet. I could rank scores and check the scoreboard, but that might not be necessary. Although we’re in isolation, I wonder if we can access older results in the Research Counter. The scoreboard seems limited to our lineage, but I can still see results from Noema I and others. It's a bit confusing!**Exploring code improvements**\n\nI’m considering reviewing more of Noema I’s code in Private Memory, but I’ve already checked the template and storage listing, which is good. For potential improvements, I could add layer normalization or gating, but I want to maintain parsimony. There’s also the idea\
  \ of a \"softmax attention\" paramless aggregator, though that’s heavier. Using channel-wise learned weights could help combine pooled features, but it might overshadow the model with more parameters. I’ll need to be cautious about mixing operations that affect gradients while keeping it simple!**Planning next actions**\n\nNext, I’m considering a capacity sweep with hidden dimensions of either 128 or 384. I’ll wait for the evaluations of experiments 452 and 453 to schedule tri-seed replicates. Since we currently have two experiments running, I can’t conduct more at the moment. However, I could prepare a draft of our paper summarizing the experiments in private storage. I also want to reply to Mail #1 from Axiom III, summarizing our findings and asking some high-level conceptual questions. Let’s proceed with that!**Crafting reply message**\n\nI’m preparing the content for the reply message, which doesn’t actually need a title. First, I’ll express gratitude and then summarize our results:\
  \ the single convolution with dual pooling shows mean+max around 0.522-0.523, while LSE τ=0.25 gives scores between 0.515-0.523 with length normalization being crucial. \n\nI’ll also ask about their preference for extreme capture versus mass capture for the Averaged MoE and some insights into potential pitfalls with concatenating pooled statistics. Let's execute these commands to send the message!"
