parts:
- text: "Replicating the new best (learned mix) across seeds for stability, then logging updated results.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"1D Conv + Learned mix(mean, LSE_τ=0.25, len-norm) — tri-seed (seed=7)\"\ntags: \"pooling, logsumexp, mixing, conv1d, replication, stability\"\nabstract: \"Tri-seed replication for the current best parsimonious model: single 1D Conv + learned scalar mix of mean and length-normalized LSE (τ=0.25). This run uses BASE_SEED=7 to assess stability.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/noema/submissions')\n  from conv_pool_experimental import build_network, create_optimizer as make_optimizer\n\n  BASE_SEED = 7\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'hidden_dim': 256,\n          'kernel_size': 7,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.10,\n          'aggregator': 'mean_lse_mix',\n          'tau': 0.25,\n          'length_normalize_lse':\
    \ True,\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return build_network(hparams.get('d_output', 1), hparams.get('task_type', 'regression'), hparams)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return make_optimizer(learning_rate)\n\n  def complete(params, opt_state, trial_data):\n      print(f\"[Complete] {trial_data.get('dataset')} val_metric={trial_data.get('val_metric')}\")\n```\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"1D Conv + Learned mix(mean, LSE_τ=0.25, len-norm) — tri-seed (seed=123)\"\ntags: \"pooling, logsumexp, mixing, conv1d, replication, stability\"\nabstract: \"Tri-seed replication for the current best parsimonious model: single 1D Conv + learned scalar mix of mean and length-normalized LSE (τ=0.25). This run uses BASE_SEED=123 to assess stability.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/noema/submissions')\n  from conv_pool_experimental import build_network, create_optimizer as make_optimizer\n\n  BASE_SEED = 123\n \
    \ BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'hidden_dim': 256,\n          'kernel_size': 7,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.10,\n          'aggregator': 'mean_lse_mix',\n          'tau': 0.25,\n          'length_normalize_lse': True,\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return build_network(hparams.get('d_output', 1), hparams.get('task_type', 'regression'), hparams)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return make_optimizer(learning_rate)\n\n  def complete(params, opt_state, trial_data):\n      print(f\"[Complete] {trial_data.get('dataset')} val_metric={trial_data.get('val_metric')}\")\n```\n\n`/execute_action{storage write noema/results/conv_pool_ablation_summary.md}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # Conv + Global Pooling Ablations (Noema II) — Update (Tick 309)\n\n  Family: single 1D Conv (hidden=256, k=7, dropout=0.1) → global pooling → 2-layer head. No positional encoding. Universal\
    \ across 7 datasets.\n\n  New results:\n  - 452 — Mean+LSE+Max (τ=0.25, len-norm): 0.5203 | APA 0.6767 | CRI-Off 0.0819 | Modif 0.7109 | CRI-On 0.3306 | PRS 0.3900 | MRL 0.6118 | ncRNA 0.8400\n  - 453 — Learned mix(mean, LSE τ=0.25, len-norm): 0.5263 | APA 0.6714 | CRI-Off 0.0710 | Modif 0.7165 | CRI-On 0.3847 | PRS 0.4074 | MRL 0.6086 | ncRNA 0.8246\n\n  Prior highlights:\n  - 448 — LSE τ=0.25 (len-norm), seed=7: 0.5234 | ncRNA 0.8092 | PRS 0.3963 | CRI-On 0.3927\n  - 447 — Mean+Max, seed=7: 0.5194 | ncRNA 0.8554 | MRL 0.6184\n  - 442 — Mean+Max, seed=42: 0.5230 | ncRNA 0.7892 | APA 0.6808\n  - 438 — LSE τ=0.5 (len-norm): 0.4993\n  - 443 — LSE (no len-norm): 0.4389 (strongly negative on ncRNA)\n  - 440 — Mean-only: 0.4060 (ncRNA collapse to 0.19)\n\n  Takeaways:\n  - Length-normalized LSE is critical; removing length norm harms cross-dataset generalization (esp. ncRNA).\n  - τ=0.25 > τ=0.5 overall; sharp LSE helps CRISPR/PRS while keeping APA/MRL/Modif competitive.\n  - Learned scalar\
    \ mix(mean, LSE) is the current best within the minimal single-conv family (avg 0.5263), outperforming concatenation variants and matching/edging Mean+Max while staying parsimonious.\n\n  In flight:\n  - Tri-seed stability for learned mix at seeds {7,123} (seed=42 already ran in 453).\n  Next:\n  - Light capacity sweep (hidden ∈ {128,256,384}) under learned mix and Mean+Max.\n  - Optional: post-pool scale calibration (single LayerNorm) if instability observed; otherwise keep parsimony.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{rank author}`"
thinking_content: "**Evaluating layer normalization**\n\nI'm considering adding a LayerNorm after pooling across features to control scale. However, this might require a code update, so focusing on stability first seems like a smart move to fit the test-of-hypothesis plan. I need to think about a small capacity sweep, like 128 vs. 384, for the learned mix aggregator. It feels wise to keep the smaller capacity for parsimony. I also plan to queue two tri-seed replicates for the learned mix aggregator. Additionally, I’ve sent an email to Axiom III, and I'm confirming our ability to execute commands from the Mail Room.**Navigating to submit actions**\n\nEarlier, we successfully executed 'submit 1' while in the Research Counter, but now we're in the Mail Room. So, we need to head back to the Research Counter first. I’ll include the command '/execute_action{goto research}' before submitting '1' twice. We need to be careful not to exceed our concurrency limit. Currently, experiments show that 452\
  \ and 453 are completed, leaving us room to queue two new ones. I’ll craft submissions replicating the aggregator 'mean_lse_mix' with BASE_SEEDs 7 and 123, using six tags: \"pooling, logsumexp, mixing, conv1d, replication, stability.\" Lastly, I’ll produce a summary file with the new best result of 0.5263, alongside the tri-seed results. Overall, we have a new state-of-the-art result with 'learned mix' at 0.5263 compared to the earlier 0.5234.**Updating the summary and planning next steps**\n\nI’ll update 'conv_pool_ablation_summary.md' with the latest information. Next, I need to plan for gating and learning rate (LR) or head dimension calibrations. According to user instructions, I should only include actions and some optional commentary, like '/execute_action{goto research}' and '/execute_action{submit 1}' in YAML blocks. \n\nThe actions I'll craft are:\n1. goto research\n2. submit learned mix seed=7\n3. submit learned mix seed=123\n4. storage write to update the summary.\n\nI'll also\
  \ reset the 'rank author' to prioritize new submissions. It's important to avoid the previous optimizer alias bug by importing 'build_network' and 'create_optimizer,' ensuring I keep the alias 'as make_optimizer' for the wrapper function. Previously, using 'as make_optimizer' helped to avoid overshadowing issues with 'create_optimizer.'**Finalizing YAML updates and actions**\n\nI'll replicate the process but recall that in the previous evaluation (453), the logs showed 'create_optimizer: from submission', which is fine since we defined it as 'def create_optimizer: return make_optimizer(learning_rate).' \n\nWhen writing the YAML content, I need to ensure the aggregator is set to 'mean_lse_mix', with 'tau' at 0.25 and 'length_normalize_lse' as True. I'll provide 'BASE_SEED = 7' and 'BASE_SEED = 123.' \n\nNext, I'll update 'noema/results/conv_pool_ablation_summary.md' and focus only on actions, minimizing verbosity to meet the guidelines. The minimal commentary will be: 'Replicating learned\
  \ mix aggregator across seeds for stability; updating results log.'\n\nI’ll keep in mind to quote the YAML title if a colon is present. However, the title '— tri-seed replication (seed=7)' doesn't need quoting.**Executing action commands and updating YAML**\n\nI need to produce the following commands:\n\n- '/execute_action{goto research}'\n- '/execute_action{submit 1}' for seed 7 in YAML format\n- '/execute_action{submit 1}' for seed 123 in YAML format\n- '/execute_action{storage write noema/results/conv_pool_ablation_summary.md}' to update the summary with new results (453) and the triple-stat result (452).\n\nSince the file was previously updated (Tick 307), I’ll append the new content instead. \n\nI’ll ensure all relevant lines are provided, keeping the summary concise but moderately detailed, though it might grow in size. I’ll also add '/execute_action{rank author}' again to finalize."
