thinking_content: "**Considering submission constraints**\n\nI see that we can use \"rank score\" to assess our global ranking and note our relative improvement. Our best score of 0.526 is noticeably below the top score of around 0.68, indicating we're not at the state-of-the-art (SOTA) yet. As a new agent, our focus is on establishing a baseline. I might need to adjust the configuration to use \"page_size 5.\" Right now, we shouldn't submit while having more than two pending runs. It looks like both experiments are still running, so waiting is the best option for now.**Scheduling actions and updates**\n\nI realize we can schedule actions and set up a mental plan, but in the multi-turn API, everything needs to be executed immediately, which limits us. I can only write code to storage to prepare things, not submit. It might be a good idea to write results updates with deltas to include the recent results of 452 and 453, which we've already processed at tick 309. I could also read previous\
  \ notes in my Private Memory Room, but I need to minimize my token usage. Lastly, I might explore ranking again to see if our score of 0.526 can reach the top.**Drafting and planning actions**\n\nThe score of 0.68 still overshadows our best effort of 0.526, so we're not there yet. We can propose an early draft for the paper in the lineage storage and update \"paper/draft.md\" with sections on our experiments, aggregator options, and a results table. I want to ensure the Private Memory is synchronized. Another task is to review entry 452 for submission code and logs, although we’ve already reviewed the logs. \n\nImplementing GRU pooling could align with Axiom's suggestions. I might explore options like \"Conv+GRU final pooling\" or \"Conv with a BiLSTM aggregator.\" This aligns with a variant I noted in previous storage. While our current minimal setup with 1D conv yields a score of 0.526, adding GRU could push us closer to 0.54. Yet, I want to focus on finishing tri-seed first and will\
  \ aim for succinct actions. My plan is to write a capacity sweep file at \"noema/submissions/conv_pool_learnmix_capacity.py\" that employs conv_pool_experimental, using a parametrizable 'hidden_dim.'**Planning submission details**\n\nI can submit later with the hidden_dim set to 384, but I need to be careful since our conv_pool_experimental's default hidden_dim is 256. When submitting, I can override this in _define_hyperparameters. Instead of creating a new module, I think it’s best to create small submission wrappers that set hidden_dim values to 128 and 384, along with the aggregator as 'mean_lse_mix'.\n\nAlso, we can param-sweep \"kernel_size\" options like 5 vs 9, but it’s a small difference, so let’s focus on hidden_dim for now. I’m considering another ablation with a dropout rate of 0.1 vs 0.0 for future tests.\n\nAdditionally, testing LayerNorm after the pooling aggregator on the concatenated vector could help with scaling. I might implement an optional flag for that, and I’ll\
  \ have to make sure to keep the code concise to avoid adding too many unnecessary bytes.**Finalizing action plans**\n\nWe’ve gleaned a new insight from the updated results: the learned mix appears to be the best option, while the triple-stat method isn't beneficial. It seems the synergy between mass and extremes is better captured through learned mixing rather than simply adding max values.\n\nI’ll prepare capacity sweep wrappers for storage without submitting them yet. We can also update our results documentation. I can write four wrapper file submissions to storage, focusing on hidden dimensions of 128 and 384 with appropriate aggregators, and I’ll consider indicating capacity sweeps in our summary file. I’ll ensure to follow concise YAML guidelines while preparing to deliver."
