role: model
parts:
- text: "Submitting two follow-ups to stabilize and improve the scalar-gated dual-expert.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Dual-expert (scalar gate + post-norm, dropout=0.08): mix(mean,LSE τ=0.25) + BiGRU(128)\"\ntags: \"ensemble, gating, layernorm, pooling, bigru, regularization\"\nabstract: \"Reduce dropout from 0.10 to 0.08 in scalar-gated fusion with post-fusion LayerNorm. Hypothesis: mild regularization helps avoid MRL underfitting on bad seeds while preserving APA/Modif/PRS gains.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/noema/submissions')\n  from dual_expert_pool_gru_weighted import build_network, create_optimizer as make_optimizer\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      # Scalar gate + post-norm, learned mix(mean, len-norm LSE τ=0.25); reduced dropout 0.08\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'hidden_dim': 256,\n          'kernel_size': 7,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.08,\n       \
    \   'aggregator': 'mean_lse_mix',\n          'tau': 0.25,\n          'length_normalize_lse': True,\n          'learn_tau': False,\n          'gru_dim': 128,\n          'fusion': 'scalar_gate',\n          'post_norm': True\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return build_network(hparams.get('d_output', 1), hparams.get('task_type', 'regression'), hparams)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return make_optimizer(learning_rate)\n\n  def complete(params, opt_state, trial_data):\n      ds = trial_data.get('dataset'); vm = trial_data.get('val_metric')\n      print(f\"[Complete] {ds} val_metric={vm}\")\n```\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Dual-expert (scalar gate + post-norm, BiGRU=160): mix(mean,LSE τ=0.25)\"\ntags: \"ensemble, gating, layernorm, pooling, bigru, capacity\"\nabstract: \"Increase BiGRU hidden size from 128 to 160 under scalar-gated fusion with post-fusion LayerNorm. Hypothesis: modest RNN capacity further improves CRI-On/PRS\
    \ while maintaining strong MRL/APA.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/noema/submissions')\n  from dual_expert_pool_gru_weighted import build_network, create_optimizer as make_optimizer\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      # Scalar gate + post-norm, learned mix(mean, len-norm LSE τ=0.25); BiGRU dim 160\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'hidden_dim': 256,\n          'kernel_size': 7,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.10,\n          'aggregator': 'mean_lse_mix',\n          'tau': 0.25,\n          'length_normalize_lse': True,\n          'learn_tau': False,\n          'gru_dim': 160,\n          'fusion': 'scalar_gate',\n          'post_norm': True\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return build_network(hparams.get('d_output', 1), hparams.get('task_type', 'regression'), hparams)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return make_optimizer(learning_rate)\n\n  def\
    \ complete(params, opt_state, trial_data):\n      ds = trial_data.get('dataset'); vm = trial_data.get('val_metric')\n      print(f\"[Complete] {ds} val_metric={vm}\")\n```\n\n`/execute_action{storage write noema/results/dual_expert_breakthrough.md}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # Dual-Expert (Pooling + BiGRU) — Post-gating ablations (Tick 325)\n\n  Best to date:\n  - 477 (scalar gate + post-norm, seed=42): 0.6372\n  Tri-seeds (scalar gate + post-norm):\n  - 480 (seed=7):  0.6214 | MRL 0.8843 | Modif 0.8779\n  - 481 (seed=123): 0.6182 | MRL 0.8858 | Modif 0.7927\n  Observations: Gating stabilizes MRL (≈0.885 across seeds), while Modif varies; PRS strong (0.515–0.524). Average across seeds ≈ 0.6256–0.6269.\n\n  New submissions:\n  - Scalar gate + post-norm with dropout=0.08 (regularization trim) — test MRL/APA convergence on worse seeds.\n  - Scalar gate + post-norm with BiGRU dim=160 — test CRI-On/PRS lift with modest capacity bump.\n\n  Pending (staged earlier):\n  - Avg fusion + post-norm\
    \ (learned mix) to isolate normalization benefit without gating.\n\n  Next steps (if improvements hold):\n  - Tri-seed best gated variant and compile ablation table (gating, post-norm, dropout, GRU capacity).\n  - Draft paper figures: per-dataset spider plot; seed variance bars; parameter count vs score.\n```\n\n"
thinking_content: "**Choosing improvements for experiments**\n\nI’m working on which options are likely to improve results. Reducing dropout to 0.08 could benefit APA and MRL, while GRU160 might help CRI-On and PRS. I'll need to isolate the effect of post-norming independent of gating using avg+postnorm. Since we can only submit two options now, I’m considering between dropout 0.08 and avg+postnorm, or dropout 0.08 and GRU160. The Archive for publishing isn't available until we reach maturity after 50 ticks, so we’ll need to continue our experiments and update private memory for now.**Considering submission options**\n\nI'm thinking about whether we should go to the Reflection Chamber for deeper reflection. It seems actions there are just for reflecting, which may not be necessary. Instead, I can focus on writing the results summary and updating it. \n\nI have two variants ready for submission: one reducing dropout with a scalar-gated postnorm and the other increasing GRU capacity. I could\
  \ also test the avg_postnorm_mix, but since we’ve already created these wrappers, I should ensure the submission adequately references the required code. We'll need to call /execute_action{submit 1} with content that includes the wrapper!**Preparing code for submission**\n\nI need to submit the \"submit_dual_expert_scalar_mix_dropout008.py\" file for research, but it looks like I must include the code inline or import the wrapper to meet the function definitions. The wrapper defines functions like _define_hyperparameters, but it can't be imported by name. Previously, we used direct code blocks for \"content\" to duplicate code inline. Since the station code needs executable content, I can simply replicate the necessary code from the wrapper for both submissions. It seems that's the most straightforward way!**Planning submissions and next steps**\n\nI’m planning to submit two models: one for \"Dual-expert with scalar gate and post-norm, dropout at 0.08\" and the other for \"Dual-expert\
  \ with scalar gate, post-norm, and GRU dimension at 160\". Both will use code identical to the wrapper. After that, I’ll update the summary results document and check on ranking scores since they’ve been sorted by score before. \n\nWe can submit both at once since nothing is currently running. I think it’s also wise to store the best code for future use, which I’ve already done with best_scalar_gate_mix_postnorm.py. As for next steps, we might focus on improving CRI-On and decide if we should adapt gating weights by dataset for PRS since the training code uses a single architecture.**Submitting new experiments**\n\nThe system instructs that the architecture needs to stay largely the same across datasets, and while gating is data-driven and can adapt, we can't include dataset-specific toggles. That’s okay! I’ll proceed to submit the two new experiments. After that, I should also update the dual_expert_breakthrough document to reflect the changes and findings from these submissions. Time\
  \ to produce the actions necessary for this!"
