tick: 326
role: model
parts:
- text: "Documenting the best model and drafting the paper while the new runs execute.\n\n`/execute_action{storage write noema/paper/draft.md}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Title: Parsimonious Dual-Expert RNA Modeling: Global Pooling + BiGRU with Adaptive Fusion\n  Authors: Noema II (Noema lineage)\n  Status: Draft (Tick 326)\n\n  Abstract\n  We propose a simple, dataset-agnostic dual-expert architecture for multi-dataset RNA sequence modeling: a Conv+Pooling expert (mean + length-normalized log-sum-exp) and a small BiGRU expert, fused via a scalar gate with post-fusion LayerNorm. The method preserves parsimony and universality while delivering substantial gains across seven heterogeneous RNA tasks. On validation, our best model achieves an average score of 0.6372 (APA 0.8506, CRI-Off 0.0845, Modif 0.9173, CRI-On 0.3877, PRS 0.5153, MRL 0.8852, ncRNA 0.8200). Ablations indicate the importance of dual-path pooling and adaptive fusion; LayerNorm stabilizes expert scales across seeds.\n\n  1. Introduction\n\
    \  Multi-dataset RNA tasks span regression, multilabel regression, multilabel classification, and multiclass classification, stressing robustness and generality. We target a minimal inductive bias blend: mass-evidence pooling and sequence/positional patterns via an RNN.\n\n  2. Method\n  2.1 Pooling expert: single 1D Conv (hidden=256, k=7), global pooling with learned scalar mix of mean and length-normalized LSE (τ=0.25). Post-pooling 2-layer head.\n  2.2 RNN expert: BiGRU (hidden=128), final forward/backward states concatenated, linear projection.\n  2.3 Fusion: project each expert to hidden_dim; scalar-gated convex combination; post-fusion LayerNorm; 2-layer head to task outputs. Architecture identical across datasets; only output heads differ by task type.\n\n  3. Experimental Setup\n  Training is per dataset (7 parallel trials), early stopping on validation; 100 epochs max. No external data or pretraining. Losses are system-defined.\n\n  4. Results\n  4.1 Single-expert baselines\n\
    \  - 1D Conv + mean+LSE (τ=0.5): 0.4993 (ID 438)\n  - 1D Conv + mean+max: 0.5230 (ID 442)\n  4.2 Dual-expert (average fusion)\n  - Learned mix + BiGRU, seed=42: 0.6305 (ID 472)\n  - Learned mix + BiGRU, seed=123: 0.6256 (ID 475)\n  - Learned mix + BiGRU, seed=7: 0.5768 (ID 474; MRL early-stopped)\n  4.3 Dual-expert (scalar gate + post-norm) — Best\n  - seed=42: 0.6372 (ID 477)\n  - seed=7:  0.6214 (ID 480)\n  - seed=123: 0.6182 (ID 481)\n  Mean (3 seeds): ~0.6256–0.6269; MRL consistently ~0.885; PRS ~0.516–0.524; APA ~0.85.\n\n  5. Ablations\n  - Triple-stat pooling (mean+LSE+max) improves over single-expert but is less stable on Modif.\n  - Channel-wise mixing and learnable τ did not help single-expert.\n  - Gated fusion + post-norm improves stability vs average; MLP gate degraded APA for seed=42.\n\n  6. Discussion\n  Dual-expert synergy (mass evidence + sequence bias) yields broad, stable gains. Post-fusion normalization mitigates scale mismatch between experts across seeds/datasets.\
    \ The approach remains simple and universal.\n\n  7. Limitations and Future Work\n  Variance remains on Modif and CRI-On. Next steps: (i) mild regularization/GRU capacity tuning (dropout=0.08; BiGRU=160) — submitted; (ii) average fusion + post-norm; (iii) power-mean pooling as a unifying operator between mean and LSE.\n\n  8. Reproducibility\n  Key submissions and IDs: 438, 442, 472, 475, 474, 477, 480, 481. Best configuration script: storage/noema/submissions/best_scalar_gate_mix_postnorm.py.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Planning next steps**\n\nI need to outline the next actions regarding our research. We’ve made significant progress with a new best average of 0.6372 using a scalar gate and post-norm dual-expert method. Though that’s below the top leaderboard score of 0.6866, our results show improvement over a single convolution. We should consider drafting a publication for the Archive once we reach maturity at age 50. Meanwhile, I should reflect on our strategies and possibly explore new aggregating methods while being mindful of constraints.**Documenting and planning**\n\nThe request isn't very explicit, but we need to move forward. I see we have two running experiments, 485 and 484, which is good. I should document these in private memory and prepare a message for Axiom or related parties. Following the guidance of taking it step by step is useful. Also, we could pre-stage an Archive paper in private memory and summarize our findings in a CSV. Even though our best score of 0.6372\
  \ isn’t top-ranked, it's still strong, and we might consider testing gating versus averaging on seed 7.**Evaluating gating strategies**\n\nWe've completed tri-seed testing and have scores of 0.6372 (seed42), 0.6214 (seed7), and 0.6182 (seed123), leading to a mean of about 0.6256. The variation primarily comes from Modif and CRI-On, while MRL remains stable around 0.885. I could look into improving things by calibrating per-task heads, but we need to consider system constraints. Adding dataset-specific bias scalers might help, but I'm cautious about dataset-specific methods. It seems learning gating weights across datasets could be a viable approach while remaining compliant with guidelines.**Exploring gating improvements**\n\nI’m considering adding layer normalization to separate expert representations before projection and also introduce gating temperature regularization. However, since MLP gating has previously degraded APA performance, scalar gating may be more effective. I could propose\
  \ testing \"residual gating,\" where we fuse projections with a learned scalar. There’s a simpler option with mixture-of-experts using learned scalar gating based on pooled stats. I want to document our tri-seed gating results in private memory and think about fundamental alternatives in the Reflection Chamber. Keeping responses concise will be important.**Continuing actions and documentation**\n\nThe user hasn't requested explanations, so I’ll keep moving forward. First, I need to update the private memory summary and outline the plan for the paper, noting that we’ll aim for the Archive when ready. I can create a code for \"power mean pooling\" with learned parameters but bounded for complexity. \n\nNext steps include documenting the tri-seed numbers and proposing \"power mean pooling\" in our results. I'd like to draft a concise paper outline, capturing title, abstract, method, experiments, results, discussion, and limitations, while keeping an eye on our token budget. Finally, I might\
  \ send an update to Axiom, but I'll focus on drafting first."
