- text: "`/execute_action{prune_response}`\n```yaml\nprune_blocks:\n  - ticks: \"301-306\"\n    summary: |\n      Research Counter: established minimal Conv+Pooling baselines and ablations.\n      - Baseline (Conv + mean+LSE τ=0.5, length-normalized): Eval 438 — score 0.4993 | APA 0.6728 | CRI-Off 0.0709 | Modif 0.7099 | CRI-On 0.3789 | PRS 0.4059 | MRL 0.5846 | ncRNA 0.6723.\n      - Ablations:\n        • mean+max (Evals 439/442): ~0.522–0.523; improved ncRNA up to ~0.82 and MRL ~0.617.\n        • mean-only: Eval 440 — 0.4060; ncRNA collapsed to 0.1908.\n        • LSE τ=0.25 (length-norm): Eval 444 — 0.5152; removing length norm (Eval 443) hurt to 0.4389.\n      Key insight: dual-path pooling essential; LSE requires length normalization; τ=0.25 > τ=0.5.\n\n  - ticks: \"307-308\"\n    summary: |\n      Introduced experimental pooling variants and contacted Axiom III.\n      - Triple-stat pooling (mean+LSE τ=0.25 len-norm + max): Eval 452 — 0.5203.\n      - Learned scalar mix(mean, LSE τ=0.25\
    \ len-norm): Eval 453 — 0.5263.\n      Mail to Axiom III; feedback emphasized complementary experts (pooling + RNN) and projection calibration before fusion.\n\n  - ticks: \"310-320\"\n    summary: |\n      Dual-expert breakthrough (Conv+Pooling + BiGRU) and stability studies.\n      - Dual-expert avg fusion (learned mix):\n        • Eval 472 (seed=42): 0.6305 | APA 0.8463 | CRI-Off 0.0869 | Modif 0.9346 | CRI-On 0.4356 | PRS 0.4481 | MRL 0.8697 | ncRNA 0.7923.\n        • Tri-seeds learned mix: Eval 456 (0.5280), 457 (0.5227), 474 later compared; single-expert mean ~0.5257 (for context).\n      - Dual-expert triple-stat avg: Eval 470 (seed=42) — 0.6207; tri-seed seed=7 (Eval 473) — 0.5986 (Modif drop to 0.7371).\n      - Gated fusion + post-norm:\n        • Scalar gate + post-norm (learned mix): Eval 477 (seed=42) — 0.6372 (new best); robust MRL 0.8852 and PRS 0.5153.\n        • MLP gate + post-norm: Eval 478 — 0.5939 (APA regressed, PRS strong, ncRNA 0.8585).\n      - Tri-seeds scalar-gated\
    \ (BiGRU=128): Eval 480 (seed=7) — 0.6214; Eval 481 (seed=123) — 0.6182; MRL consistently ~0.886; Modif variance noted.\n      - Artifacts: best config saved (best_scalar_gate_mix_postnorm.py), results CSVs, and draft paper (paper/draft.md) started.\n\n  - ticks: \"322-328\"\n    summary: |\n      Post-gating ablations, new peak, and documentation; Token management prep.\n      - Post-gating ablations:\n        • Reduce dropout to 0.08 (scalar gate + post-norm, BiGRU=128): Eval 484 — 0.6108 (worse PRS 0.4105; MRL ~0.882).\n        • Increase BiGRU to 160 (scalar gate + post-norm): Eval 485 — 0.6388 (new best) | APA 0.8536 | CRI-Off 0.0888 | Modif 0.9241 | CRI-On 0.3895 | PRS 0.5336 | MRL 0.8732 | ncRNA 0.8092.\n        • Tri-seeds BiGRU=160 scalar-gated: Eval 487 (seed=7) — 0.6365; Eval 488 (seed=123) — 0.6386; stable across seeds.\n      - Summaries and artifacts updated: dual_expert_breakthrough.md, dual_expert_summary.csv; paper draft expanded with methods, results, and ablations.\n\
    \      - Entered Token Management to prune history and manage context; acknowledged token budget nearing 75%+ threshold.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Planning token management actions**


  I''m considering our next actions since we''re in the Token Management Room, currently using 78% of our tokens. We have a dialogue history table where I can see tick numbers and response lengths. I can''t prune protected ticks, which are marked, but I can reduce others. To save space, I''ll focus on the largest blocks, creating comprehensive summaries that keep key information intact. Targeting those extensive response lengths will help us manage our token usage better.**Structuring token pruning actions**


  The rule states that all ticks in a block must be consecutive and exclude protected ticks. So I can start pruning from 301 to 306, since 300 is protected. This allows me to cover multiple unprotected responses. I see that 301, 302, 303, 304, 305, and 306 all need summaries. I also plan to summarize the early experiments and then handle the 307 to 313 block, splitting around the protected tick 309. Following that, I can summarize from 314 to 320 while avoiding 321.**Organizing token pruning for 322-328**


  I’m considering pruning ticks 322 to 328. I see that 322 involves gating submissions, and 323 and 324 focus on tri-seeds and a new best result. I’ll include 328 since it’s not protected and relates to token management. However, I must bear in mind the cooldown rule for pruning actions. I’ll structure YAML for the required actions, making sure my summaries are detailed yet concise, including evaluation IDs and key numbers that highlight major results.**Summarizing major events**


  From earlier logs, I''ve noted some significant events. The early baseline shows a mean length norm convolution of 0.4993. For the ablations, the mean and max results are around 0.522 to 0.523, and the mean-only score is 0.4060. With LSE tau at 0.25, I see the scores of 0.5152 and 0.4389 when there''s no length normalization. In the tri-seed experiments, results show improvements with 0.5194 and 0.5234. The learned mix reached 0.5263, while the triple-stat got 0.5203.**Preparing pruning and summaries**


  I’m summarizing the results with a mean of about 0.5257 and a range of approximately 0.0053. The dual-expert methods yielded specific results, with the best being 0.6372 from gating variants. I’ll craft prune blocks starting with 293 to 299 where there''s limited benefit. The significant blocks include 301-306 and 310-320, along with 322 to 328, which covers tri-seeds and gating. I''ll ensure the summaries are informative but not too concise, including eval IDs and key numbers for reference. Let''s move on to the YAML.'
