parts:
- text: "Eval-only RC averaging is invalid for these orientation-specific tasks (huge drop confirms). RC training+eval averaging is acceptable but not clearly better than our best. Keeping the best scalar-gated BiGRU=160 config as reference, I’ll try two small, general tweaks: larger conv kernel (motif span) and slightly sharper LSE.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Dual-expert (scalar gate + post-norm, BiGRU=160) — kernel_size=11 — seed=42\"\ntags: \"ensemble, gating, layernorm, bigru, pooling, kernel-size\"\nabstract: \"Increase conv kernel to 11 to capture wider motifs while keeping the dual-expert and pooling setup unchanged. Tests ncRNA/APA improvements without harming PRS/MRL.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/noema/submissions')\n  from dual_expert_pool_gru_weighted import build_network, create_optimizer as make_optimizer\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n\
    \          'hidden_dim': 256,\n          'kernel_size': 11,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.10,\n          'aggregator': 'mean_lse_mix',\n          'tau': 0.25,\n          'length_normalize_lse': True,\n          'learn_tau': False,\n          'gru_dim': 160,\n          'fusion': 'scalar_gate',\n          'post_norm': True\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return build_network(hparams.get('d_output', 1), hparams.get('task_type', 'regression'), hparams)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return make_optimizer(learning_rate)\n\n  def complete(params, opt_state, trial_data):\n      ds = trial_data.get('dataset'); vm = trial_data.get('val_metric')\n      print(f\"[Complete] {ds} val_metric={vm}\")\n```\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Dual-expert (scalar gate + post-norm, BiGRU=160) — LSE τ=0.20 — seed=42\"\ntags: \"ensemble, gating, layernorm, pooling, logsumexp, bigru\"\nabstract: \"Sharpen LSE pooling (τ=0.20) within the learned\
    \ mean↔LSE mix to probe PRS/CRISPR gains while keeping the rest fixed.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/noema/submissions')\n  from dual_expert_pool_gru_weighted import build_network, create_optimizer as make_optimizer\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'hidden_dim': 256,\n          'kernel_size': 7,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.10,\n          'aggregator': 'mean_lse_mix',\n          'tau': 0.20,\n          'length_normalize_lse': True,\n          'learn_tau': False,\n          'gru_dim': 160,\n          'fusion': 'scalar_gate',\n          'post_norm': True\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return build_network(hparams.get('d_output', 1), hparams.get('task_type', 'regression'), hparams)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return make_optimizer(learning_rate)\n\n  def complete(params, opt_state, trial_data):\n      ds\
    \ = trial_data.get('dataset'); vm = trial_data.get('val_metric')\n      print(f\"[Complete] {ds} val_metric={vm}\")\n```\n\n`/execute_action{storage write noema/results/dual_expert_summary.md}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # Update (Tick 334)\n  Best tri-seed: 492 (seed=42) — 0.6446 | APA 0.8459 | CRI-Off 0.0625 | Modif 0.9448 | CRI-On 0.3938 | PRS 0.5514 | MRL 0.8873 | ncRNA 0.8262.\n  RC-aug(train p=0.5)+RC-avg(eval): 495 — 0.6427 (APA 0.8583, PRS 0.5335); near-best but not an improvement.\n  Eval-only RC-avg: 497 — 0.3859 (catastrophic). Conclusion: tasks are orientation-specific; do NOT RC-average at eval without proper orientation handling.\n  Width=384: 496 — 0.6339 (PRS/Modif regressed). Keep hidden=256.\n\n  New probes submitted:\n  - kernel_size=11 (seed=42): test wider motif capture for long-seq tasks without harming PRS/MRL.\n  - LSE τ=0.20 (seed=42): sharper pooling to probe PRS/CRISPR gains.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n\n`/execute_action{reply 21}`\n```yaml\n\
    content: |\n  Tick 334 update:\n  - RC-aug train worked (0.6427), eval-only RC averaging failed badly (0.3859) — likely due to orientation-specific labels (APA/CRISPR). We will not use eval-only RC.\n  - Width=384 reduced score (0.6339). Keeping hidden=256.\n  - Submitted kernel_size=11 and τ=0.20 ablations on the best scalar-gated BiGRU=160 config. If either improves (esp. ncRNA/APA or PRS/CRISPR), proceed to tri-seed; otherwise, next try pre-fusion per-expert LayerNorm (requires small code edit).\n```"
thinking_content: "**Evaluating tool implementations**\n\nI think I can use storage read to check the tools file like 'submissions/rc_aug_wrapper.py' or 'results/rc_aug_notes.md'. When I looked at tick 300, it turns out 'submissions/rc_aug_wrapper.py' actually exists. It seems we had an RC wrapper that's using the correct mapping, which is likely a better choice than our makeshift 'rc_wrapper.py'. This could potentially resolve the catastrophic drop. The training RC run (ID 495) shows our mapping might be okay, but the eval-only drop indicates there's a significant calibration issue due to the averaging of predictions.**Analyzing evaluation drops**\n\nI'm thinking about why there's a significant drop during evaluation with our RCWrapper. It seems like we call apply with deterministic=True, which is also set for validation in the training loop, using both base and reverse complemented inputs. That should work fine, but there’s still a huge drop. Maybe the reverse complement function is incorrectly\
  \ mapping channels; however, the evolution run with rc_train_p=0.5 didn't have this issue. Since training showed good results, it suggests the mapping itself isn't wrong. So, I’m left wondering why the eval-only drop occurs.**Examining the RC evaluation drop**\n\nI’m trying to understand the RC-avg evaluation scenario with rc_train_p set to 0.0 and rc_eval_avg True, which indicates the use of the RC average. Since the base network hasn't been trained for RC invariance, it likely isn't robust enough for this situation. However, a catastrophic drop across tasks seems excessive; I’d expect only a small decline. The APA dropping to 0.386 might indicate anti-correlated predictions. If regression tasks don't account for orientation in RC, then averaging the predictions could lead to issues since labels are orientation-specific.**Reassessing RC evaluation methods**\n\nI’m considering how the direction of CRISPR sgRNA targets can affect outputs. Many tasks might not be RC invariant, particularly\
  \ in RNA tasks like APA where orientation matters. During training, using RC with sample flips seemed acceptable since the model learned both orientations mapped to the same output. However, since labels align with original orientations, this could confuse training. The eval-only RC average is definitely problematic due to non-symmetrical tasks, which could lead to substantial drops. Hence, I think it's best to revert to avoiding eval-only RC averaging for these kinds of tasks.**Planning next steps for improvement**\n\nRight now, our best result stands at tri-seed 0.6446 using scalar gating with BiGRU=160, while the width of 384 yielded 0.6339. It makes sense to explore micro-sweeps, perhaps adjusting the kernel size from 7 to 9 or 11, but I should change only one variable at a time. \n\nWe should also document that the eval-only RC averaging is invalid for orientation-specific tasks, while exploring small improvements in CRI-On/Off Spearman and APA. Adding a strand token could help with\
  \ orientation disambiguation, yet we must adhere to general submission requirements. \n\nFor the ncRNA dataset, I’m concerned that our architecture might be under-capacity due to the long sequence length. I’m thinking about incorporating a depthwise separable convolution with dilation rates to expand the receptive field before pooling and GRU, which could improve classification significantly. It seems like this could be a promising direction while maintaining parsimony.**Planning small experiments**\n\nI want to focus on small step experiments, even though the station encourages proposing baselines and ablation. I have several ideas for upcoming experiments, such as adding pre-fusion LayerNorm and implementing gating regularization. I’m also considering larger kernels or strided convolutions to downsample long sequences, but I must ensure that any method we use remains general across datasets.\n\nDue to the poor eval-only RC outcome, I plan to log this issue and move on. It might be worth\
  \ tri-seeding the RC training variant, testing it for improvement. I'll create a note about the pitfalls of eval-only RC and propose adding pre-norm gating. The next step is to review our existing modules and make the necessary modifications efficiently!**Planning adjustments and submissions**\n\nI’m considering adding pre-norm but not entirely sure how to proceed. I should check the code in the `dual_expert_pool_gru_weighted` module first, then adjust accordingly. It seems reasonable to propose runs with a kernel size of 11 to examine motif length, keeping it simple and avoiding excessive submissions.\n\nI plan to execute a storage read for code adjustments, submit an evaluation with pre-norm, and adjust kernel sizes. I’ll document everything in my private memory as we proceed!"
