role: model
parts:
- text: "RC eval-only averaging is invalid here; keeping scalar-gated BiGRU=160 as reference while 499/500 run. Submitting a learnable τ variant to let the model calibrate pooling sharpness globally.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Dual-expert (scalar gate + post-norm, BiGRU=160) — learnable LSE τ (init 0.25) — seed=42\"\ntags: \"ensemble, gating, layernorm, pooling, logsumexp, bigru\"\nabstract: \"Enable learnable LSE temperature (global, init τ=0.25) within the mean↔LSE mix to allow the model to calibrate pooling sharpness across datasets. Baseline is scalar-gated dual-expert with BiGRU=160 and post-fusion LayerNorm.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/noema/submissions')\n  from dual_expert_pool_gru_weighted import build_network, create_optimizer as make_optimizer\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'hidden_dim':\
    \ 256,\n          'kernel_size': 7,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.10,\n          'aggregator': 'mean_lse_mix',\n          'tau': 0.25,            # initialization\n          'length_normalize_lse': True,\n          'learn_tau': True,      # new: learnable temperature\n          'gru_dim': 160,\n          'fusion': 'scalar_gate',\n          'post_norm': True\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return build_network(hparams.get('d_output', 1), hparams.get('task_type', 'regression'), hparams)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return make_optimizer(learning_rate)\n\n  def complete(params, opt_state, trial_data):\n      ds = trial_data.get('dataset'); vm = trial_data.get('val_metric')\n      print(f\"[Complete] {ds} val_metric={vm}\")\n```"
thinking_content: "**Evaluating next steps**\n\nI’m thinking about what to try next in our planning. We found that RC evaluation breaks orientation, likely due to tasks not being RC-invariant, so it's good we documented that.\n\nNow, I’m considering options like applying LayerNorm before fusion to calibrate scales, modifications to gating networks, or improving CRI-Off and CRI-On metrics. Another idea could be using weighted ranking loss, but the framework restricts changes to loss functions. We could think about output calibrations, especially for tasks involving Spearman correlation. Although attempting to add monotonic transformations during training may not be allowed, I wonder if incorporating some form of nonlinear adjustment at the output would be helpful.**Exploring sequence-level tasks**\n\nI'm considering sequence-level tasks that involve using BiGRU with 160 and convolutional dimensions of 256. I could test a multi-head pooling aggregator beyond the mean-LSE mix—like trying Softmax\
  \ pooling with a temperature factor. However, since we have constraints on using dataset-specific methods for the aggregator, I will explore alternatives like learned position bias or even Fourier features for enhancement. \n\nBut I need to balance complexity with simplicity. Another area to look into is incorporating depthwise separable convolutions, which might unify our dual-expert architecture while keeping it innovative.**Analyzing performance improvements**\n\nI'm noticing we have some novelty with gating interplay, but our scores aren't where I'd like them to be. I think we should analyze why our method focuses on PRS/MRL while underperforming in classification tasks and propose some targeted modifications. \n\nOne idea is to implement channel-wise normalization on pooling outputs by performing LayerNorm on features before global pooling, similar to our previous \"post-norm\" approach. We may need to adjust the code for \"dual_expert_pool_gru_weighted\" and store this change in\
  \ our persistent storage. Next, I'll plan to implement per-expert LayerNorm before fusion, while continuing to manage token usage effectively.**Considering gating improvements**\n\nI'm aware that I can't vary the method, but we can use dropout as a hyperparameter, which is consistent across the system. One improvement I'm thinking about is a gating schedule that starts gates close to 0/1 and learns over time, even though our training loops are fixed. \n\nWe could also explore feature-wise gating instead of scalar gating to enhance performance by allowing per-feature expert weighting. While we've tried an MLP gate previously, a simpler vector gate might work better. I can propose a pre-fusion per-expert LayerNorm experiment now and update our summary in 'results/FINAL.md', but that may not be necessary at this stage.**Planning code modifications**\n\nI need to issue commands to submit our code and focus on modifying the gating system, particularly for per-expert pre-norm gating. I could\
  \ either adjust the existing 'dual_expert_pool_gru_weighted.py' file or create a new variant to avoid disruption. \n\nSince we can’t access the content from that file directly, I plan to import the existing module and wrap it with a \"PreFusionNorm\" feature. We might also explore different variations while considering the complexity and time constraints. For now, I’ll keep track of our progress and propose some experiments, including fine-tuning dropout rates and other network parameters.**Executing next steps**\n\nThe cooldown is now at 6 ticks, and since tokens are only 27% used, there's no need to prune yet. I’ll wait for results on our current experiments, particularly with kernel size 11 and tau of 0.20. \n\nFor now, I'll log the negative conclusions to our results file and propose running 'learn_tau: True' for dual-expert architecture. This should allow global settings across datasets. \n\nSo, I'll execute these commands:\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{submit\
  \ 1}  \n```yaml\ntitle: Dual-expert (scalar gate + post-norm, BiGRU=160) — learnable τ init 0.25\n```"
