parts:
- text: "Great news: DSConv Expert A raised performance to 0.6674 (ID 517), within ~0.019 of SOTA (~0.6866). Tri-seed replications (IDs 521/522) are running. I’ll prep parameter-count tooling and a paper revision draft with explicit SOTA context while we wait.\n\n`/execute_action{storage write noema/submissions/dsconv_paramcount_submit.py}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  sys.path.append('storage/noema/submissions')\n  from dual_expert_dsconv_pool_gru import build_network\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      # Match ID 517 config\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'hidden_dim': 256,\n          'kernel_size': 7,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.10,\n          'aggregator': 'mean_lse_mix',\n          'tau': 0.25,\n          'length_normalize_lse': True,\n          'gru_dim': 160,\n          'ds_dilations': [1,2,4,8,16],\n          'fusion': 'scalar_gate',\n          'post_norm':\
    \ True\n      }\n\n  def _count_params(params_tree):\n      leaves = jax.tree_util.tree_leaves(params_tree)\n      return sum(int(x.size) for x in leaves)\n\n  def _init_and_count(d_output, task_type):\n      hparams = _define_hyperparameters()\n      net = build_network(d_output, task_type, hparams)\n      key = jax.random.PRNGKey(0)\n      # Dummy input: (batch=2, seq_len=186, 4) is safe for all datasets (will be padded/cropped by convs)\n      dummy = jnp.zeros((2, 186, 4), dtype=jnp.float32)\n      params = net.init(key, dummy)\n      return _count_params(params)\n\n  def test():\n      # Report param counts for representative heads\n      counts = {}\n      counts['regression(d_output=1)'] = _init_and_count(1, 'regression')\n      counts['multilabel_classification(d_output=12)'] = _init_and_count(12, 'multilabel_classification')\n      counts['multilabel_regression(d_output=3)'] = _init_and_count(3, 'multilabel_regression')\n      counts['multiclass_classification(d_output=13)']\
    \ = _init_and_count(13, 'multiclass_classification')\n      print(\"Parameter counts (by head type):\")\n      for k,v in counts.items():\n          print(f\"  {k}: {v:,} params\")\n      # Also print max across heads (approx. model size range)\n      max_params = max(counts.values())\n      min_params = min(counts.values())\n      print(f\"Param range across heads: {min_params:,} to {max_params:,}\")\n      return counts\n```\n\n`/execute_action{storage write noema/paper/revision_plan_tick347.md}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Paper Revision Plan (Tick 347)\n  Goal: Address reviewer by (1) adding SOTA-aligned DSConv Expert A and (2) contextualizing results vs SOTA with param/time trade-offs.\n\n  New Results:\n  - DSConv Expert A (5-block dilations [1,2,4,8,16]) + mean↔LSE(τ=0.25,len-norm) + BiGRU(160), scalar gate + post-LN:\n    • 517 (seed=42): 0.6674 | APA 0.8526 | CRI-Off 0.0838 | Modif 0.9506 | CRI-On 0.4069 | PRS 0.5556 | MRL 0.8807 | ncRNA 0.9415.\n    • Tri-seeds running: 521 (seed=7),\
    \ 522 (seed=123).\n\n  SOTA Context (top examples):\n  - 369 (Noema I): 0.6866\n  - 328 (Axiom II): 0.6863\n  - 365 (Veritas II): 0.6862\n  Gap: ~0.019 relative to best SOTA (vs earlier gap ~0.042 with 0.6446).\n\n  Trade-offs & Analysis:\n  - Parameter count: to be measured via dsconv_paramcount_submit.py (submission with test()). Target: ~1M params (estimate) vs larger SOTA backbones/MoE variants.\n  - Wall-clock: from logs, total fit ~30-33 min per multi-dataset run (varies by dataset timeouts). Will report aggregate from evaluation logs (e.g., ID 517 total ~32 min).\n  - Stability: tri-seed to report mean and variance; earlier single-conv dual-expert was ~0.637–0.645; DSConv improved and boosted ncRNA markedly.\n\n  Negative/Neutral Findings:\n  - Multi-τ pooling (Conv expert) did not improve PRS (513: 0.6342; 514+clip: 0.6374).\n  - Learnable τ regressed (503: 0.6123; ncRNA collapsed).\n  - RC eval-only invalid; train+eval averaged is near-best but not a clear win.\n\n  Next Actions:\n\
    \  - Await tri-seeds (521/522). If mean ≥0.666: prepare revised archive with SOTA table, param counts, and wall-time. Otherwise, reframe as strong parsimony trade-off with explicit gaps and compute/params analysis.\n```\n\nWhen the queue frees:\n- I will submit dsconv_paramcount_submit.py (test-only) to report exact parameter counts.\n- Then revise and resubmit the paper with SOTA comparisons and trade-offs."
thinking_content: '**Planning next steps**


  I need to respond with actions and some commentary. It looks like the DSConv Expert A has improved to 0.6674, which is great! We''re already planning tri-seed replicates for seeds 7 and 123. The next step is to compare with the SOTA scoreboard, which has a top score around 0.6866. I think it’s wise to hold off on an archive revision until the tri-seed results come in. Also, I want to fix the gradient clipping run for DSConv.**Planning for the paper**


  I should consider including param count and training time in the paper. However, measuring time across tasks is tricky since we don’t have direct access; still, the logs show times per dataset, which can help us compute an approximate total. Once the tri-seed results are in, I can revise the Archive paper with proper SOTA context. I could also write a test() function to count parameters for the DSConv dual-expert. This function runs quickly without training, and I can execute it locally to print the parameter count.**Exploring implementation options**


  I can create a file in storage to calculate param counts for the DSConv network and baseline. To execute this code, I need to submit it; though I could also use the test() function, which is allowed. It might be easier to use test() to print parameter numbers for each dataset configuration without involving training. I’ll wait to update the Archive paper until the tri-seeds finish. I should also update the summary with DSConv result 517 and mention SOTA comparisons for resubmission when ready.**Updating the paper with comparisons**


  I have the DSConv partial results, though the tri-seed replicates aren''t finished yet. Still, I can revise the paper to include an SOTA comparison by showing our score of 0.6674 against 0.6866. I’ll discuss the 0.019 gap and compute the param/time trade-offs. It''s worth noting that the DSConv addition improved our score by +0.0229. I should also mention the dual-expert method with BiGRU and DSConv 5-block, emphasizing that while we use DSConv, our approach is simpler than some SOTA methods with gating.**Preparing archive revision**


  I need to craft an archive revision that acknowledges the SOTA score of 0.6866 from Noema I ID 369. Our best result, 0.6674 from ID 517, is still in tri-seed processing. I should include a trade-off analysis of parameter counts, noting that total training time is around 1800 seconds, but I want to compute actual times from logs if possible. It seems best to wait for the tri-seeds to finish before revising; resubmission can focus on SOTA comparisons. Alternatively, I could start drafting the revised paper, but submitting a new test now isn''t feasible because of the concurrent limit. I''ll wait until the tri-seeds finish and then present the necessary updates.**Implementing parameter count functions**


  I’m set to implement the test() function to print counts for d_output=1 and 13. For the counting function, I''ll use jax.tree_util.tree_map to compute the sum of size parameter arrays. Now, let’s lay out the actions: I''ll write the script in storage as ''noema/submissions/dsconv_paramcount_submit.py''. Additionally, I''ll update the results plan or paper revision document. I''ll also revise the summary doc ''dual_expert_summary.md'' to include our new result, noting the SOTA gap of 517. Let’s get started on these actions!'
