    \ — seed=7\"\ntags: \"dsconv, shared-backbone, task-aware, pooling, gating, layernorm\"\nabstract: \"Task-aware variant replication (seed=7): mean_lse_mix for non-ncRNA; mean_max_mix for ncRNA. Per-expert pre-LN + post-LN; global-norm grad-clip=1.0.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/noema/submissions')\n  from submit_shared_taskaware_clip import _define_hyperparameters, create_network, create_optimizer, complete\n  BASE_SEED = 7\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n```\n\n`/execute_action{storage write noema/results/dual_expert_summary.md}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # Update (Tick 354)\n  Shared-backbone DSConv dual-expert (BiGRU on DSConv features), scalar gate + post-LN, clip=1.0 — Tri-seed complete:\n  - 528 (seed=42): 0.6751 | CRI-On 0.4924 | PRS 0.5542 | MRL 0.8947 | ncRNA 0.8985.\n  - 532 (seed=7):  0.6671 | CRI-On 0.4559 | PRS 0.5460 | MRL 0.8918 | ncRNA 0.8908.\n  - 533 (seed=123):0.6712 | CRI-On 0.4751 | PRS 0.5522 | MRL 0.8935 | ncRNA 0.9031.\n  Tri-seed mean ≈ 0.6711 (gap\
    \ to SOTA 0.6866 ≈ 0.0155). CRI-On substantially improved (up to ~0.49), with a modest ncRNA drop (~0.89–0.90).\n\n  Next experiment (submitted):\n  - Task-aware pooling + pre-fusion calibration (per-expert LN): mean_lse_mix for non-ncRNA; mean_max_mix for ncRNA; grad-clip=1.0. Seeds {42, 7} running to assess ncRNA recovery without hurting CRI-On.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n\n`/execute_action{update 18-1}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Addendum (Tick 354): Shared-backbone + clipping tri-seed mean is 0.6711 (IDs 528/532/533), narrowing the gap to ~0.0155. CRI-On improved to as high as 0.4924; ncRNA decreased to ~0.89–0.90. We have submitted a task-aware pooling variant (mean_lse_mix on non-ncRNA; mean_max_mix on ncRNA) with per-expert pre-LN and grad-clip=1.0 to recover ncRNA while keeping CRI-On gains. Seeds {42,7} are running; we will report outcomes and updated trade-offs.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Analyzing experiment status**


  I''ve written some code to help with aggregator selection and prepared a submission wrapper called "submit_shared_taskaware_clip.py." Now that the tri-seeds are done, it seems there are no running experiments. I see a message saying, "You do not have any running experiments." However, earlier in tick 353, it mentioned there''s none running, but it didn''t repeat that in tick 354. I’m checking the "Latest Room Output" for clarity on this. It looks like I might need to submit new experiments since success messages indicate previous runs are complete.**Planning submission actions**


  For my next steps, I need to submit the task-aware clipped shared-backbone variant to recover ncRNA while keeping CRI-On intact. Testing with pre_norm set to True might be beneficial since the wrapper already sets it. The base code defaults to mean_lse_mix for the aggregator, while the wrapper sets it to mean_max_mix for ncRNA. I’ll also submit a parameter count test for the shared-backbone. Previously, submitting the param count faced issues due to max concurrency, but now it seems I can proceed with that. Test mode may not score, but it will log counts—useful!**Updating submission details**


  I should update the archive addendum with the tri-seed mean for the shared-backbone clipped variant and include information about the modified code path, along with parameter counts once the test returns. Logging the new tri-seed results in the summary file is also a good idea. Furthermore, I plan to adjust the aggregator to mean_max_mix for multiclass classification. For multilabel classification, we may stick with mean_lse_mix. Time is limited for considering seed stabilization or dropout effects. Regarding submission, I need to reference the "submit_shared_taskaware_clip.py" in the content for submission, either importing it or pasting the code again.**Simplifying the submission process**


  I can import the "submit_shared_taskaware_clip" wrapper module and use its functions. The system loads submissions by reading the code from "content" and requires definitions for hyperparameters, network, optimizer, and any optional constants. So, I can write a content script that imports from this wrapper and re-exports the necessary functions, making sure they actually exist. This includes defining constants like BASE_SEED and BATCH_SIZE. For now, I think setting BASE_SEED to 42 for tri-seed will work well!**Planning training and submission strategy**


  I''m ready for a general run and considering using a new seed, maybe 42, which I’ve used before. Since the tri-seed for the clipped shared-backbone is done, I may run it again to evaluate effects with the enhancement aggregator. There’s a concurrency limit of two, so I can start two runs, one with seed 42 and another with seed 7. I''ll ensure the wrapper sets BASE_SEED correctly, and I can override it if necessary.


  I need to write the content to submit these experiments. After that, I also plan to submit the parameter count test for the shared-backbone. Since tests run quickly, I can either run one training and one test concurrently, or I could defer the test for the next tick to maximize throughput. Given the completed tri-seeds and their results, I’m set to submit the training runs for seeds 42 and 7. Finally, I''ll update my summary files with the tri-seed results and the newly computed means!'
