parts:
- text: "SE calibration improved CRI-On and average. Submitting updates and planning next steps while bi-window runs (573/574) execute.\n\n`/execute_action{storage write noema/results/se_calibration_summary.md}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # Channel SE Calibration Summary (Tick 375)\n  Config: Shared DSConv (5 dilations [1,2,4,8,16]) + Channel SE (reduction=4) + mean↔LSE pooling (τ=0.25, len-norm) + BiGRU(160) + scalar gate + pre/post-LN + clip=1.0.\n\n  Results:\n  - 569 (seed=42): 0.6756 | APA 0.8556 | CRI-Off 0.0798 | Modif 0.9500 | CRI-On 0.5034 | PRS 0.5545 | MRL 0.8968 | ncRNA 0.8892\n  - 570 (seed=123): 0.6727 | APA 0.8587 | CRI-Off 0.0806 | Modif 0.9504 | CRI-On 0.4806 | PRS 0.5579 | MRL 0.8959 | ncRNA 0.8846\n\n  Takeaways:\n  - CRI-On improved (peak 0.5034), PRS solid (~0.556), APA/MRL strong; average ~0.674 (↑ vs default 0.6729).\n  - SE adds minimal params and appears broadly helpful without regressions.\n\n  Next:\n  - Bi-Window pooling (IDs 573/574) targets CRI-Off by half-wise\
    \ pooling while staying universal. Criteria for adoption: ≥0.678 avg and CRI-Off ↑ without hurting APA/MRL.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n\n`/execute_action{update 18-1}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Addendum (Tick 375): Channel SE calibration improved average to ~0.674 and pushed CRI-On to 0.5034 (ID 569), with strong PRS and stable APA/MRL. Minimal extra parameters keep the model <1M. \n  We have queued a universal Bi-Window pooling variant (IDs 573/574) that splits DSConv features into equal halves and learns a channel-wise soft mix over {mean1, LSE1, mean2, LSE2}. This aims to close the CRI-Off gap without sacrificing APA/MRL. Adoption criteria: ≥0.678 mean and CRI-Off gain with no major regressions; otherwise we retain SE-calibrated shared-backbone as the default and report Bi-Window as neutral/negative.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: Bi-Window and SE — Decision Gate (Tick 376)\nabstract: Plan and thresholds while\
    \ 573/574 run; fallback and follow-ups.\ncontent: |\n  Status:\n  - SE Calibration: +~0.002 avg vs default; CRI-On peak 0.503; PRS ~0.556; APA/MRL strong. Low-risk improvement; keep as default if Bi-Window fails.\n  - Bi-Window (573/574): equal-halves mean/LSE cw-mix; targets CRI-Off (our ~0.08 vs best 0.1365) without dataset-specific logic.\n\n  Decision Gate:\n  - Adopt Bi-Window iff tri-seed mean ≥0.678 and no regressions on APA/MRL; otherwise, stick with SE-calibrated shared backbone.\n\n  Follow-ups (if needed):\n  - Tri-seed SE to lock stability; small SE reduction sweep (r ∈ {4,8}) for param/perf trade-off.\n  - Optional: gate-prior init bias=0 (keep scalar gate neutral); post-norm OFF ablation to re-check ncRNA/CRI-On.\n  - If CRI-Off remains weak, try 4-window variant (quarters) or interleaved pooling (even/odd), both universal.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Planning my next steps**


  I need to respond in an actions room style, currently at the Research Counter. I''ve just submitted bi-window pooling, and I have some results from SE runs to consider. The leaderboard shows a strong score by Veritas II, and I think we should plan our next steps carefully. We can''t run more experiments while two are pending, so maybe I should work on documentation or brainstorm ideas in the Reflection Chamber instead.**Considering token pruning**


  I might need to prune tokens, as I''ve checked the cooldown status. The last prune was at tick 372, and now there''s a difference of 4 ticks up to 376, which means I''m eligible to prune again. I''ll look into the Token Management help section for verification. I can prune some large responses, but given my token usage is at 46%, it’s not urgent. Still, pruning could be beneficial, and I can also work on an aggregator for SE results or summaries.**Exploring model improvements**


  I''m considering implementing a "gate prior" to favor pooled over RNN methods across datasets while keeping things universal. I won''t create dataset-specific logic, so that’s off the table for now. Another idea is designing a "contrastive calibration head" for variance regularization, but I can’t change the training loop. I’ll also think about a "local-window aggregator" with multi-window pooling or morphological pooling, but I need to wait for current runs to finish before deciding on improvements. After that, I can update the results summary and archive addendum.**Deciding on archive updates**


  I think it''s better to focus on updating the archive or considering a plan for private memory. Implementing a parameter count function could help compute model parameter counts, but it’s not necessary right now. We could share in Public Memory since we’ve matured past the isolation period, but I might not need to do that. It’s tricky because creating a test for parameter counts interrupts the training, and evaluation slots are currently busy. So, it seems I should hold off on that for now.**Outlining next steps and updates**


  Instead of running arbitrary Python directly, I can write a separate script and store it. While the Token Management sound like a good idea for pruning, I need to be cautious about which ticks I choose to remove. I’ll summarize that SE improved CRI-On and average results. If bi-window improves CRI-Off, I''ll consider tri-seeding; otherwise, I’ll revert. I’ll add necessary updates to private memory, log results, and update the archive. We’ll ensure consistent YAML quoting and proceed with the pruning if cooldown permits, balancing it with necessary content retention.'
