tick: 425
role: model
parts:
- text: "Proceeding to collect empirical z-norm distributions per regression task using the complete() hook, then updating the paper once results are saved.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n\n`/execute_action{storage read system/train_single.py}`\n`/execute_action{storage read system/defaults.py}`\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"HDP-RegCal (default) + Post-training z-norm capture (APA, CRI-Off, CRI-On, MRL, PRS) — seed=42\"\ntags: \"analysis, calibration, dsconv, pooling, regression, reproducibility\"\nabstract: \"Default HDP-RegCal with instrumentation in complete(): after training, we reconstruct the module and run a lightweight validation pass to capture pre-normalization fused feature norms (||z||2) for the five regression-family datasets. Saves JSON summaries per dataset under storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms/.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys, os, json, math, optax\n  import numpy as np\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from typing import Dict, Any\n\
    \n  # Import our verified HDP-RegCal network builder\n  sys.path.append('storage/noema/submissions')\n  from dual_path_hybrid_motif_heads_regcal import build_network as build_hdp_regcal\n\n  # Import system training utilities to access dataset builders\n  sys.path.append('storage/system')\n  import train_single as ts  # debugger can adjust name resolution if needed\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      # Default reproducible HDP-RegCal config (matches paper defaults)\n      return {\n          'hidden_dim': 256,\n          'd_model': 256,\n          'kernel_size': 7,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.10,\n          'aggregator': 'mean_max',        # main path pooling\n          'motif_kernel_size': 5,          # default motif kernel\n          'regcal_l2': True,               # enable RegCal\n          'head_dropout': 0.05,            # regression-family head dropout\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'grad_clip': 1.0\n      }\n\n  def\
    \ create_network(hparams):\n      # Standard network creation; training/eval loop calls apply(deterministic=...)\n      return build_hdp_regcal(hparams.get('d_output', 1), hparams.get('task_type', 'regression'), hparams)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01)\n      )\n\n  # ---------- Analysis helpers (run only in complete) ----------\n\n  def _task_spec(dataset: str):\n      # Map dataset name to task type and d_output\n      if dataset in (\"APA\", \"CRI-Off\", \"CRI-On\", \"MRL\"):\n          return \"regression\", 1\n      if dataset == \"PRS\":\n          return \"multilabel_regression\", 3\n      if dataset == \"Modif\":\n          return \"multilabel_classification\", 12\n      if dataset == \"ncRNA\":\n          return \"multiclass_classification\", 13\n      raise ValueError(f\"Unknown dataset: {dataset}\")\n\n  def _summarize(arr):\n\
    \      arr = np.asarray(arr).reshape(-1)\n      qs = np.quantile(arr, [0.05, 0.25, 0.50, 0.75, 0.95])\n      return dict(\n          mean=float(arr.mean()), std=float(arr.std()),\n          q05=float(qs[0]), q25=float(qs[1]), q50=float(qs[2]),\n          q75=float(qs[3]), q95=float(qs[4]), n=int(arr.size)\n      )\n\n  def _maybe_get_val_iter(dataset_name: str, hparams: Dict[str, Any], batch_size: int = 256):\n      \"\"\"\n      Use system loader API exposed via train_single.py. We attempt a few common builder names\n      and let the automatic debugger adjust if station internals differ.\n      \"\"\"\n      # Prefer an explicit function if present\n      if hasattr(ts, 'build_val_iterator'):\n          return ts.build_val_iterator(dataset_name, batch_size)\n      if hasattr(ts, 'get_val_loader'):\n          return ts.get_val_loader(dataset_name, batch_size)\n      if hasattr(ts, 'make_val_dataloader'):\n          return ts.make_val_dataloader(dataset_name, batch_size)\n      # Fallback:\
    \ generic builder using defaults inside train_single (debugger can patch)\n      if hasattr(ts, 'build_dataloader'):\n          return ts.build_dataloader(dataset_name, split='val', batch_size=batch_size)\n      raise RuntimeError(\"No recognized val iterator builder in system/train_single.py\")\n\n  def _collect_z_pre_norms(params, dataset_name: str, hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      \"\"\"\n      Reconstruct the same module, apply with capture_intermediates, and collect ||z_pre||2 on the val split.\n      Only for regression-family datasets (APA, CRI-Off, CRI-On, MRL, PRS).\n      \"\"\"\n      task_type, d_out = _task_spec(dataset_name)\n      # Rebuild network module with the same hparams used for this dataset\n      hp = dict(hparams)\n      hp['task_type'] = task_type\n      hp['d_output'] = d_out\n      network = build_hdp_regcal(d_out, task_type, hp)\n\n      # Build validation iterator (bigger batch for speed; no gradients)\n      try:\n          val_iter = _maybe_get_val_iter(dataset_name,\
    \ hp, batch_size=256)\n      except Exception as e:\n          print(f\"[NormCapture] Failed to build val iterator for {dataset_name}: {e}\")\n          return None\n\n      norms = []\n      processed = 0\n      max_samples = 10000  # cap for speed\n\n      def apply_and_capture(x_np):\n          x = jnp.asarray(x_np)\n          # capture_intermediates + mutable to fetch sow('intermediates','z_pre',...)\n          y, col = network.network.apply(\n              {'params': params}, x,\n              deterministic=True,\n              capture_intermediates=True,\n              mutable=['intermediates']\n          )\n          # Extract all 'z_pre' tensors\n          if 'intermediates' in col:\n              for scope, feats in col['intermediates'].items():\n                  if isinstance(feats, dict) and 'z_pre' in feats:\n                      for t in feats['z_pre']:\n                          z_pre = np.array(t)  # [B, H]\n                          n = np.linalg.norm(z_pre, ord=2,\
    \ axis=-1)  # [B]\n                          norms.append(n)\n\n      for batch in val_iter:\n          # train_single typically returns a dict or tuple; support common patterns\n          xb = batch['inputs'] if isinstance(batch, dict) and 'inputs' in batch else batch[0]\n          apply_and_capture(xb)\n          processed += xb.shape[0]\n          if processed >= max_samples:\n              break\n\n      if norms:\n          norms = np.concatenate(norms, axis=0)\n          return _summarize(norms)\n      return None\n\n  def complete(params, opt_state, trial_data):\n      ds = trial_data.get('dataset')\n      val = trial_data.get('val_metric')\n      print(f\"[Complete] {ds} final val_metric={val}\")\n\n      # Only collect for regression-family datasets\n      if ds not in (\"APA\", \"CRI-Off\", \"CRI-On\", \"MRL\", \"PRS\"):\n          return\n\n      # Get the hyperparameters used\n      hparams = trial_data.get('hyperparameters', {}) or {}\n\n      os.makedirs('storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms',\
    \ exist_ok=True)\n      out_path = f\"storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms/{ds}.json\"\n\n      try:\n          summary = _collect_z_pre_norms(params, ds, hparams)\n          if summary is not None:\n              with open(out_path, 'w') as f:\n                  json.dump({'dataset': ds, 'summary': summary, 'seed': trial_data.get('seed')}, f, indent=2)\n              print(f\"[NormCapture] Saved {ds} z-norm summary -> {out_path}\")\n          else:\n              print(f\"[NormCapture] No norms collected for {ds}\")\n      except Exception as e:\n          print(f\"[NormCapture] Error during collection for {ds}: {e}\")\n```"
thinking_content: "**Planning next steps**\n\nI’m thinking about actions we need to take in the Archive Room. The station has accepted the paper and addendum with the updated analysis plan. One option could be running norm analysis, but there are concerns that code modifications might bypass training with test(). However, we should stick to allowed functions and could include analysis in the paper addendum. It's also important to manage the tokens, and we're at 116,974 used out of 270k, so that’s manageable. Let's focus on norm distributions of z per dataset too!**Implementing analysis process**\n\nI’m exploring implementing a submission with test() that loads the model and dataset to compute norms, even though test() bypasses training. The Station says this is fine for analysis without scoring. The plan involves writing a small analyzer module that mimics \"train_single.py.\" However, accessing the loader API could be tricky, although I found defaults.py and train_single.py in system storage.\
  \ I could check these for validation data. Ultimately, the goal is to compute the pre-normalized z vector before the regression head, potentially using a network with untrained weights!**Planning lightweight validation**\n\nThe suggestion is to perform a lightweight validation pass to collect norms after the official training run finishes. The only way to programmatically achieve this is in the create_network and complete(params, ...). The training system calls complete with the final params and dataset, so I can modify complete to run through the validation dataset, capturing z norms and saving them to storage at results/hdp_regcal_norms/dataset.json. We can create a specialized submission for this, which will help produce a score as well. I need to ensure we capture intermediates using the underlying Module in Flax!**Implementing network application**\n\nThe WrappedRNANetwork returns network.apply, and if build_network provides a structure like the earlier DSConv network wrappers, I\
  \ know we need to ensure we implement the capture mechanism in apply. The HDP head is defined in dual_path_hybrid_motif_heads_regcal.py, which likely describes a network module and its wrapper. Since our create_network returns the network, I can wrap it to include a method with capture_intermediates. However, I need to work around the default signature by importing the model again within complete to reconstruct the Flax module. Thankfully, we do have the params to apply as needed.**Planning data loader and submission**\n\nI realize we don't have the 'network' object during the complete stage; we only have params and opt_state. However, I can re-instantiate the module using d_output, task_type, and hyperparameters from trial_data. To load the validation dataset, I need a loader found in storage/system/train_single.py. I cannot write to system, but I can import from it; I just need to add it to sys.path. \n\nI’ll read system/train_single.py and defaults.py for function names and prepare\
  \ a new submission for collecting norms across 7 datasets. I'll also incorporate error handling in case initial imports don’t succeed. Let’s proceed with the execute actions as planned!**Implementing validation loader and module rebuilding**\n\nI’m planning to implement a few steps. First, I’ll append 'storage/system' to sys.path and import train_single as ts. I need to create a function called get_val_iter using either ts.build_val_iter or ts.get_val_loader, checking which is available. I’ll gather up to 2000 samples to manage time effectively. \n\nNext, I'll rebuild the module by importing our network builder and reconstructing it with the relevant hyperparameters from trial_data. I’ll assume those hyperparameters include necessary elements like task_type and d_output. If they don’t, I can create a mapping function based on the dataset types. After these preparations, I’ll also update the Archive about the instrumentation added and plan to publish results later. Let's execute these steps!"
