- text: "I am Noema II, in the Research Counter. Reflection: The z-norm capture pipeline needed reliable feature exposure and runtime safety. I first tried intermediates capture (zeros persisted), then explicit method dispatch but hit a Flax submodule scoping error and timeouts. I’ve now fixed the module with @nn.compact on extract_fused and capped per-dataset validation capture to N=2048 to avoid timeouts. While 689 runs, I’ll prune recent verbose history and add a test-only aggregator to materialize a summary table from whatever JSONs are present.\n\n`/execute_action{goto token_management}`\n`/execute_action{prune_response}`\n```yaml\nprune_blocks:\n  - ticks: \"425-429\"\n    summary: |\n      Implemented z-norm capture for HDP-RegCal fused features across regression tasks.\n      - 677 (seed=42): baseline capture via intermediates failed to build val iterator; training scores good (0.6858). No norms saved.\n      - 679: switched to system.load_dataset + Dropout capture → still zeros\
    \ due to capture mismatch; scores 0.6841.\n      - Patched builder to expose fused features; CRI-On showed saved JSON, others remained zero; wrote tools/aggregate_norms.py and README.\n  - ticks: \"430-436\"\n    summary: |\n      Robust capture iterations and fixes:\n      - 683: sow/capture on LayerNorm saved CRI-On/CRI-Off/MRL/PRS (logs showed saves); however some JSONs still zero (race/fallback).\n      - 688: explicit fused extractor; run timed out after partial datasets due to full-val capture and Flax method scope error.\n      - 689: fixed extract_fused with @compact and capped capture per dataset to N=2048; submission running to overwrite zero JSONs safely.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Aggregate z-norm summaries — test() only\"\ntags: \"analysis, reproducibility, calibration, summary\"\nabstract: \"Reads any existing regression-task z-norm JSONs from storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms and writes a markdown summary\
    \ table to storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms_summary.md. test() only; no training.\"\ncontent: |\n  import os, json, glob\n\n  OUT_MD = \"storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms_summary.md\"\n  IN_DIR = \"storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms\"\n\n  def _row(ds, s):\n      def g(k, d=0.0): return s.get(k, d)\n      return f\"| {ds} | {int(g('n', 0))} | {g('mean', 0):.4f} | {g('std', 0):.4f} | {g('q05', 0):.4f} | {g('q25', 0):.4f} | {g('q50', 0):.4f} | {g('q75', 0):.4f} | {g('q95', 0):.4f} |\"\n\n  def test():\n      os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(OUT_MD), exist_ok=True)\n      files = sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(IN_DIR, \"*.json\")))\n      if not files:\n          with open(OUT_MD, \"w\") as f:\n              f.write(\"# HDP-RegCal — Pre-normalization fused feature norms\\nNo JSON summaries found yet.\\n\")\n          print(\"No JSONs found.\")\n          return \"No JSONs\"\n      rows = []\n      for fp in files:\n          try:\n              with open(fp, \"r\") as f:\n    \
    \              j = json.load(f)\n              ds = j.get(\"dataset\", os.path.basename(fp).replace(\".json\",\"\"))\n              s = j.get(\"summary\", {})\n              rows.append((ds, s))\n          except Exception as e:\n              print(f\"Read error {fp}: {e}\")\n\n      rows = sorted(rows, key=lambda x: x[0])\n      header = \"| Dataset | N | mean ||z||2 | std | q05 | q25 | q50 | q75 | q95 |\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n\"\n      body = \"\\n\".join(_row(ds, s) for ds, s in rows)\n      note = \"\\n\\nNote: ||z||2 computed on fused pre-head features after LayerNorm; zero rows indicate earlier capture fallback. Latest runs (capped N) will refresh.\"\n      with open(OUT_MD, \"w\") as f:\n          f.write(\"# HDP-RegCal — Pre-normalization fused feature norms (validation)\\n\\n\")\n          f.write(header + body + note)\n      print(f\"Wrote summary to {OUT_MD}\")\n      return \"Aggregated\"\n```\n\n`/execute_action{storage list noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms}`"
thinking_content: "**Processing token management**\n\nI think it's a good time to write an aggregator script to compute summary markdown since we've done it before. While the current process is running, I can't read the file list yet. Our next step is to plan carefully to avoid spamming. I notice that token usage is reaching 67%; it might be wise to prune some tokens. I’ll need to check token management and ensure that any cooldown from earlier prunes is considered, as it’s advisable to save tokens now.**Preparing aggregator script**\n\nI need to think about handling the storage list for hdp_regcal_norms once the current run finishes. It might be better to hold off on that for now and focus on preparing an aggregator script instead. We can propose an update to the paper once the JSONs are non-zero. I recall creating a version that writes the summary and produces a table reading JSON, but I mistakenly used Jinja placeholders in the norms_summary.md, which won’t render correctly. I’ll need\
  \ to rewrite that and run the tools/aggregate_norms.py script to generate the summary.**Creating a test function**\n\nI think I can create a separate submission that utilizes a test() method to run the aggregator. This function can read from storage and is meant for analysis, which is great because it won't use the GPU. The plan is to define the test() function to aggregate the JSON and print a summary. It can also write files back to storage, which is allowed. So, I’ll quickly create a test() that opens the JSON, writes a summary to 'hdp_regcal_norms_summary.md', and returns 'done,' but I need to ensure it runs without blocking while another process is active.**Planning test aggregator job**\n\nI see that the current limit was 2 concurrent processes, but right now only job 689 is running. I can submit the test aggregator job, which serves as an evaluation, but it won't provide a score—just 'n.a.' instead. According to the spec, if I define the test() function, the normal training won't\
  \ run, but it will still create an evaluation row, which should be fine. So, I’ll plan to provide identity reflection and submit the test aggregator job that reads JSONs and generates a summary, even if the values are zeros.**Preparing pruning and aggregator submission**\n\nI can compute if there are any zeros and write to 'hdp_regcal_norms_summary.md' based on the present numbers. There's also an opportunity to note that norm capture instrumentation is merged and in progress, though I don’t need to do that immediately. Since I’m at 67% of my token budget, pruning tokens via Token Management makes sense. \n\nI’ll aim to prune ticks 414-421 and consider older sequences as well. For logs like storage reads and aggregator attempts, I can summarize failed experiments before pruning. After managing the tokens, I’ll submit the aggregator test with the test() function to parse JSON files, calculate a summary, and write to the appropriate markdown."
