parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{goto research}`\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"HDP-RegCal (fixed extract_fused, N=2048) — z-norms for regression datasets — seed=7\"\ntags: \"calibration, analysis, dsconv, pooling, norms, reproducibility\"\nabstract: \"Replicate ID 689 with BASE_SEED=7 to capture pre-head fused feature ||z||2 distributions for APA/CRI-Off/CRI-On/MRL/PRS (capped N=2048 per dataset where applicable). Saves JSONs to storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms/.\"\ncontent: |\n  import optax\n  from storage.noema.submissions.dual_path_hybrid_motif_heads_regcal import build_network\n\n  BASE_SEED = 7\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'hidden_dim': 256,\n          'd_model': 256,\n          'kernel_size': 7,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.10,\n          'motif_kernel_size': 5,\n          'aggregator': 'mean_max',\n          'regcal_l2': True,\n          'head_dropout': 0.05,\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'grad_clip':\
    \ 1.0,\n          'capture_max_n': 2048\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return build_network(hparams.get('d_output', 1), hparams.get('task_type', 'regression'), hparams)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.chain(optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n                         optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01))\n\n  def complete(params, opt_state, trial_data):\n      print(f\"[Complete] {trial_data.get('dataset')} val_metric={trial_data.get('val_metric')}\")\n```\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"HDP-RegCal (fixed extract_fused, N=2048) — z-norms for regression datasets — seed=123\"\ntags: \"calibration, analysis, dsconv, pooling, norms, reproducibility\"\nabstract: \"Replicate ID 689 with BASE_SEED=123 to capture pre-head fused feature ||z||2 distributions for APA/CRI-Off/CRI-On/MRL/PRS (capped N=2048 per dataset where applicable). Saves JSONs to storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms/.\"\n\
    content: |\n  import optax\n  from storage.noema.submissions.dual_path_hybrid_motif_heads_regcal import build_network\n\n  BASE_SEED = 123\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'hidden_dim': 256,\n          'd_model': 256,\n          'kernel_size': 7,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.10,\n          'motif_kernel_size': 5,\n          'aggregator': 'mean_max',\n          'regcal_l2': True,\n          'head_dropout': 0.05,\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'grad_clip': 1.0,\n          'capture_max_n': 2048\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return build_network(hparams.get('d_output', 1), hparams.get('task_type', 'regression'), hparams)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.chain(optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n                         optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01))\n\n  def complete(params, opt_state, trial_data):\n      print(f\"[Complete] {trial_data.get('dataset')}\
    \ val_metric={trial_data.get('val_metric')}\")\n```\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Aggregate z-norm summaries by dataset and seed — test() only\"\ntags: \"analysis, calibration, reproducibility, summary\"\nabstract: \"Reads all JSONs in storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms and writes a per-dataset, per-seed markdown table to storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms_by_seed.md. test() only; no training.\"\ncontent: |\n  import os, json, glob\n\n  IN_DIR = \"storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms\"\n  OUT_MD = \"storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms_by_seed.md\"\n\n  def _row(ds, seed, s):\n      def g(k, d=0.0): return s.get(k, d)\n      return f\"| {ds} | {seed} | {int(g('n', 0))} | {g('mean', 0):.4f} | {g('std', 0):.4f} | {g('q05', 0):.4f} | {g('q25', 0):.4f} | {g('q50', 0):.4f} | {g('q75', 0):.4f} | {g('q95', 0):.4f} |\"\n\n  def test():\n      os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(OUT_MD), exist_ok=True)\n      files = sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(IN_DIR, \"*.json\")))\n\
    \      rows = []\n      for fp in files:\n          try:\n              with open(fp, \"r\") as f:\n                  j = json.load(f)\n              ds = j.get(\"dataset\", os.path.basename(fp).replace(\".json\",\"\"))\n              seed = j.get(\"seed\", \"NA\")\n              s = j.get(\"summary\", {})\n              rows.append((ds, seed, s))\n          except Exception as e:\n              print(f\"Read error {fp}: {e}\")\n\n      header = \"| Dataset | Seed | N | mean ||z||2 | std | q05 | q25 | q50 | q75 | q95 |\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n\"\n      body = \"\\n\".join(_row(ds, seed, s) for ds, seed, s in sorted(rows))\n      with open(OUT_MD, \"w\") as f:\n          f.write(\"# HDP-RegCal — Pre-normalization fused feature norms by dataset and seed (validation)\\n\\n\")\n          f.write(header + body + \"\\n\")\n      print(f\"Wrote {OUT_MD}\")\n      return \"OK\"\n```"
thinking_content: "**Planning research steps**\n\nI need to produce the next turn from the Archive Room after successfully replying to archive capsule #22. We’ve summarized the z-norm feature scale differences which support the hypothesis for RegCal. The station offers optional previews of papers, but I think we can skip that for now. Our major tasks include continuing research experiments to push state-of-the-art (SOTA) or producing follow-up analysis. We nearly matched our best single-run baseline, achieving 0.6876 on ID 689. Next steps involve tri-seeding for improved capture-run variants.**Deciding next steps**\n\nI noticed that earlier gating and other polishing efforts were neutral, so I'm considering a few options. One approach might involve adjusting motifs to k=6 or 5 with some dilation changes. I also think about a head-only schedule warmup for regression tasks, since the global schedule was neutral. Another idea is exploring task weighting or per-head scaling, although the training\
  \ loop is fixed. I should also look into an ablation without RegCal to check training with matching averages. It seems like we should publish additional dataset-level analysis in the Archive, but I haven't received specific user requests yet.**Updating optimization strategies**\n\nIt seems I can update the published capsule (#22), but it might not be necessary. Instead, I could submit a head-only warmup schedule variant to Research. Previously, we used a warmup-cosine schedule for the whole network, but now, I might implement optimizer composition for head parameters only. The challenge is that create_optimizer returns a single optax transformation, so I could use optax.masked transformations with a mask dictionary for parameter paths. By creating mask functions for regression heads, I could apply this warmup effectively while keeping constant learning rates for other parts.**Exploring parameter transformations**\n\nI’m looking at how Optax includes optax.multi_transform to apply different\
  \ transformations to parameters using a mask mapping, but it requires param_labels from the tree label mapping. However, I can’t modify the training loop easily to pass these labels to multi_transform. The create_optimizer defaults to returning optax.GradientTransformation, which complicates things. The training code uses this with safe_init but without being able to supply param_labels. So, I should investigate how optax.masked lets me pass a mask function that computes a PyTree of booleans at initialization.**Planning submission actions**\n\nI’m considering creating a masked chain for applying head-specific schedules to weights and biases, while ensuring I identify path names. There’s a challenge since create_optimizer doesn’t receive param names directly. I think I can define a mask function to check path strings using optax.masked, but this could be fragile, so I might skip it for now. \n\nI also want to slightly adjust the head hidden size or dropout, and since we’ve published with\
  \ a 9/10 acceptance rate, I’ll finalize documentation. I aim to submit two runs, replicate some configurations for improved analyses, and run the aggregator test again while being mindful of station restrictions."
