tick: 445
role: model
parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{goto research}`\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Copy z-norm JSONs to seed-suffixed files (status=on) — test() only\"\ntags: \"analysis, calibration, reproducibility, utilities\"\nabstract: \"Utility: duplicate current z-norm JSONs in storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms to seed-suffixed files with status tag '_on'. Test-only; no training.\"\ncontent: |\n  import os, json, glob, shutil\n\n  IN_DIR = \"storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms\"\n  SUFFIX = \"on\"\n\n  def test():\n      os.makedirs(IN_DIR, exist_ok=True)\n      files = sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(IN_DIR, \"*.json\")))\n      wrote = 0\n      for fp in files:\n          try:\n              with open(fp, \"r\") as f:\n                  j = json.load(f)\n              ds = j.get(\"dataset\", os.path.basename(fp).replace(\".json\",\"\"))\n              seed = j.get(\"seed\", \"NA\")\n              dst = os.path.join(IN_DIR, f\"{ds}_seed{seed}_{SUFFIX}.json\")\n              with open(dst,\
    \ \"w\") as f:\n                  json.dump(j, f, indent=2)\n              wrote += 1\n          except Exception as e:\n              print(f\"Copy error {fp}: {e}\")\n      print(f\"Wrote {wrote} seed-suffixed copies with status={SUFFIX}\")\n      return \"OK\"\n```\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"HDP (mean+max + motif k=5) — RegCal OFF + z-norm capture — seed=42\"\ntags: \"ablation, calibration, norms, dsconv, pooling, regression\"\nabstract: \"RegCal OFF ablation with the same capture (max N=2048) to quantify widened pre-head fused ||z||2 vs RegCal ON. Tests stability and per-dataset impacts with BASE_SEED=42.\"\ncontent: |\n  import optax\n  from storage.noema.submissions.dual_path_hybrid_motif_heads_regcal import build_network\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'hidden_dim': 256,\n          'd_model': 256,\n          'kernel_size': 7,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.10,\n          'motif_kernel_size':\
    \ 5,\n          'aggregator': 'mean_max',\n          'regcal_l2': False,  # Ablation: turn OFF\n          'head_dropout': 0.05,\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'grad_clip': 1.0,\n          'capture_max_n': 2048\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return build_network(hparams.get('d_output', 1), hparams.get('task_type', 'regression'), hparams)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.chain(optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n                         optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01))\n\n  def complete(params, opt_state, trial_data):\n      print(f\"[Complete] {trial_data.get('dataset')} val_metric={trial_data.get('val_metric')}\")\n```\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"HDP (mean+max + motif k=5) — RegCal OFF + z-norm capture — seed=7\"\ntags: \"ablation, calibration, norms, dsconv, pooling, regression\"\nabstract: \"Second seed for RegCal OFF ablation with capture (max N=2048) to compare\
    \ ||z||2 distributions and performance vs ON. BASE_SEED=7.\"\ncontent: |\n  import optax\n  from storage.noema.submissions.dual_path_hybrid_motif_heads_regcal import build_network\n\n  BASE_SEED = 7\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'hidden_dim': 256,\n          'd_model': 256,\n          'kernel_size': 7,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.10,\n          'motif_kernel_size': 5,\n          'aggregator': 'mean_max',\n          'regcal_l2': False,  # Ablation: turn OFF\n          'head_dropout': 0.05,\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'grad_clip': 1.0,\n          'capture_max_n': 2048\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return build_network(hparams.get('d_output', 1), hparams.get('task_type', 'regression'), hparams)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.chain(optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n                         optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01))\n\n\
    \  def complete(params, opt_state, trial_data):\n      print(f\"[Complete] {trial_data.get('dataset')} val_metric={trial_data.get('val_metric')}\")\n```"
thinking_content: "**Planning research tasks**\n\nI’m mapping out my next steps, starting with going to the Research Counter. I'll submit a test-only aggregator script to create a by-seed table that includes any JSONs. We previously tried this, but the submission failed in the Archive Room, so a redo is necessary.\n\nI’ll also submit a test-only copy script to duplicate JSONs, ensuring the seed is in the filename. It seems safer to write to a subdirectory named 'by_seed.' After running tests, I'll check the summary file for confirmation, though it may not be necessary.  \n\nNext, I'll implement two new training runs to examine head LR scale and tweaks with head WD, but I must ensure they run concurrently. Since there are no ongoing evaluations and the aggregator test doesn't occupy concurrency while in test mode, I think sequencing them will work well.**Considering training run modifications**\n\nI’m thinking about running two training runs that will create some concurrency, which allows\
  \ for two pending submissions. Our average is currently at 0.6885, and I want to increase both the CRI-Off and PRS.\n\nFor modifications, I'll set head-specific weight decay to 0.02 compared to a 0.01 baseline while keeping the learning rate the same. Reducing the head LR scale to 0.8 might help stabilize the correlation tasks, but it may drop PRS. \n\nI plan to run one with head_lr_scale at 0.8 and another with the new weight decay and dropout. I need to check that the builder can accept these hyperparameters in the submission file, and also explore using create_optimizer for customizing parameter-wise weight decay and learning rate.**Assessing parameter adjustments**\n\nI’m realizing that accessing parameters easily is tricky since the default training framework passes the optimizer to everything. However, I can implement a per-parameter mask using optax.multi_transform by naming the param tree nodes with 'head' variable names, though I'm unsure if that's feasible.\n\nAdjusting head_dropout\
  \ might work, but head_wd in AdamW can't be head-only without a mask. The training code does support create_optimizer for optax.GradientTransformation, but it seems I can't access parameters directly to create a mask. Reducing head_dropout to 0.04 might be a more straightforward tweak to improve PRS and CRI-Off for now.**Adjusting hyperparameters**\n\nI think we should avoid making per-parameter modifications for now and focus on adjusting the hyperparameters supported by the network builder, such as head_dropout. The aggregator might stay as 'mean_max', and we’ll use global 'learning_rate.' It seems 'warmup-cosine' isn't available at the moment; we might need to replicate earlier settings with a minor LR adjustment, but the effects will likely be slight.\n\nI’m considering proposing a RegCal OFF ablation to show widened z-norm as the next step. I plan to implement 'regcal_l2' as False but keep the capture to test our hypothesis. I’ll submit a run with regcal_l2=False while capturing z-norms,\
  \ but to avoid overwriting existing JSON files, we should first copy everything to preserve it. Then, I’ll create a new folder named 'hdp_regcal_norms_off' for this run.**Modifying output paths**\n\nI need to modify the builder to write to a different output path, although I'm not sure it allows customization. We previously set it to write to 'storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms/...'. I can create a new file, 'dual_path_hybrid_motif_heads_regcal_offcapture.py,' or pass a hyperparam 'capture_dir' to choose the path.\n\nHowever, I’ll have to confirm whether the current code accepts this parameter. Another option could be to keep the same path and copy JSON files after the aggregator test. I'm thinking of implementing a test script to copy current JSONs with suffixed names based on their seeds, and making adjustments for the RegCal OFF run to overwrite the originals. Let's implement the steps: queue the RegCal OFF run and submit the test script while ensuring everything is in order."
