tick: 452
role: model
parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n`/execute_action{reply 22}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Tri-seed results (RegCal ON): consolidated table, comparisons, and decisions\"\ncontent: |\n  We completed a RegCal ON tri-seed with z-norm capture. Results exceed the historical SOTA median (0.6866) and are stable across seeds.\n\n  Tri-seed (RegCal ON) — IDs 689 (seed=42), 692 (seed=7), 693 (seed=123)\n  | Eval ID | Score  | APA   | CRI-Off | CRI-On | PRS   | MRL   | Modif | ncRNA |\n  |---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n  | 689 | 0.6876 | 0.8590 | 0.0809 | 0.5150 | 0.5652 | 0.8949 | 0.9506 | 0.9477 |\n  | 692 | 0.6871 | 0.8627 | 0.0848 | 0.4947 | 0.5722 | 0.8919 | 0.9514 | 0.9523 |\n  | 693 | 0.6907 | 0.8615 | 0.0834 | 0.5252 | 0.5783 | 0.8854 | 0.9507 | 0.9508 |\n  | Mean | 0.6885 | 0.8611 | 0.0830 | 0.5116 | 0.5719 | 0.8907 | 0.9509 | 0.9503 |\n\n  Notes\n  - Average 0.6885 > SOTA median 0.6866, with strong CRI-On and PRS while retaining APA/MRL/Modif/ncRNA.\n  - The accompanying z-norm\
    \ appendix shows large pre-head scale disparities across regression tasks (CRI-Off ~30.96 vs PRS ~13.28 mean ||z||₂). RegCal neutralizes this by normalizing per-sample inputs to the regression heads.\n\n  Head dropout probes (RegCal ON)\n  - 707 (seed=42, head_dropout=0.04): 0.6826 — degraded avg, CRI-On 0.4736.\n  - 708 (seed=123, head_dropout=0.04, wd=0.012): 0.6860 — near baseline but not better than tri-seed ON mean.\n  Decision: retain head_dropout=0.05 and wd=0.01 as defaults; treat 0.04 as neutral/negative.\n\n  Artifacts and pointers\n  - Norm JSONs: storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms/{APA,CRI-Off,CRI-On,MRL,PRS}.json\n  - Aggregated table: storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms_summary.md\n  - ON vs OFF (pre-head z) table: storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms/hdp_regcal_norms_on_off.md\n  - Code (RegCal + capture): storage/noema/submissions/dual_path_hybrid_motif_heads_regcal.py\n\n  Next (analysis)\n  - Optional: capture post-RegCal norms (z') for ON vs OFF to visualize\
    \ ON≈1 vs OFF mirroring pre-head scales (mechanistically expected; pre-head equality already sanity-checked).\n  - Minor head tuning beyond 0.04 is deprioritized given negative/neutral results; focus remains on reproducibility and analysis.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{storage write noema/results/tri_seed_regcal_on_summary.md}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # HDP‑RegCal (ON) — Tri-seed summary with per-dataset metrics\n\n  IDs: 689 (seed=42), 692 (seed=7), 693 (seed=123)\n\n  | Eval ID | Score  | APA   | CRI-Off | CRI-On | PRS   | MRL   | Modif | ncRNA |\n  |---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n  | 689 | 0.6876 | 0.8590 | 0.0809 | 0.5150 | 0.5652 | 0.8949 | 0.9506 | 0.9477 |\n  | 692 | 0.6871 | 0.8627 | 0.0848 | 0.4947 | 0.5722 | 0.8919 | 0.9514 | 0.9523 |\n  | 693 | 0.6907 | 0.8615 | 0.0834 | 0.5252 | 0.5783 | 0.8854 | 0.9507 | 0.9508 |\n  | Mean | 0.6885 | 0.8611 | 0.0830 | 0.5116 | 0.5719 | 0.8907 | 0.9509 | 0.9503 |\n\n  - SOTA median reference:\
    \ 0.6866 (e.g., ID 369).\n  - RegCal ON improves stability and average vs OFF (~+0.002–0.003 across our probes).\n  - See storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms_summary.md for pre-head z-norm scale disparities across regression tasks.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Adjusting training parameters**


  I’m thinking of reverting to the default head_dropout of 0.05 and possibly setting the head-only learning rate scale to 0.8 as planned. Should I try a per-head weight decay of 0.02? The training system uses a single optimizer; I''m considering how to adjust for head-only weight decay by masking parameters. Implementing this with `optax.GradientTransformation` seems like a good direction. However, I need to check if we can name parameters correctly to facilitate this implementation. That might just work!**Exploring optimizer mask functions**


  I’m considering how, in standard Flax, parameter names include counts. I could create mask functions using regex if I had access to tree_map keys. The catch is that `create_optimizer` can’t see params to create those masks. Some frameworks manage to pass masks through parameter names later. I noticed that `optax.multi_transform` takes them coming in as a pytree. It seems there’s a known pattern for defining gradient transformations based on a partition function when initializing optimizers. I’ll need to think about how to effectively incorporate this with the current setup.**Testing training parameters**


  I’m thinking about flattening the parameter tree in the training code. I’ll need to check the `system/train_single.py` file to see if `create_optimizer` is called during initialization. Earlier, we looked at the defaults, but for now, I want to try a simpler approach: set the head_dropout back to 0.05. Testing the head-only weight decay doesn''t seem easy, so I''ll set weight_decay to 0.012 across all. It looked fine before with ID 708 at 0.6860. I wonder if I should try 0.02, but I might risk over-regularization.**Exploring learning rate adjustments**


  I’m considering trying a head learning rate scale of 0.8, which means lowering the learning rate for the head layers compared to the backbone. This could work with `multi_transform` using masks, but the training system might not allow that. Alternatively, I could lower the global learning rate to 0.0008, although that might slow training down. Maybe I’ll test this with seed=42 while keeping the head dropout at 0.05. Since previous results showed a warmup-cosine schedule impacting performance, I might just keep the learning rate constant. Additionally, I could explore capturing post-Regularization Calibration norms for quantitative alignment in the research context.**Planning submission actions**


  Next, I need to submit a test-only aggregator to compute z'' norms based on stored z-norms when ON and OFF. To capture these z'' values, I’ll modify the network to record input norms after Regularization Calibration (RegCal), which requires some changes. I can run an aggregator to compare ON vs OFF, but we haven''t captured z''-norms yet. Essentially, the plan is to write a submission to re-run and ensure we capture both z and z'' norms correctly, alongside meta tasks for archive and research updates. I''ll avoid heavy training while focusing on these captures.**Preparing submission actions**


  Next, I’ll note that the head dropout ablations of 0.04 for results 707 and 708 were 0.6826 and 0.6860, suggesting they either degraded or stayed neutral. I’ve concluded to keep the default at 0.05 and set weight decay at 0.01. Now, I''ll execute actions to go to the archive and reply to capsule 22 with a YAML response titled "Tri-seed results (RegCal ON)" that includes a content table. Additionally, I’ll store a results file summarizing this data in the research section. Let’s move forward with this!'
