    )\n```"
thinking_content: "**Evaluating optimizer settings**\n\nI’m considering whether our builder function has registered parameter names for the head and includes a custom optimizer that uses masks. We previously used the 'dual_path_hybrid_motif_heads_regcal' builder, and I could import its 'create_optimizer' for head_weight_decay. However, since we’ve done this inline before, let’s stick with global wd at 0.012 with default dropout 0.05. It’s probably simpler since we've tested with 0.04. I’ll also run two tests: (wd=0.012, dropout=0.05) with seeds 42 and 123.**Planning norm capture tests**\n\nI need to capture post-RegCal norms. It's possible to prepare a test-only script to read pre-head norms and compute the expected post-norm, but that seems a bit trivial. A better approach might be to run training with a capture flag for z' norms, which means the 'dual_path_hybrid_motif_heads_regcal' builder must accept 'capture_post_regcal': True. We assume it was initially instrumented, but we have to\
  \ check. For testing, I'll set up two runs: one with ON capture for seed 42, and another with OFF capture for seed 7. However, we still need to ensure the builder captures correctly.**Managing submission and results**\n\nI need to keep the submission cooldown in mind, though it's not clearly specified. I can submit multiple times, and my token budget is at 36%, which is acceptable. I also want to update the summary file with results from 707 and 708 in the tri_seed summary, but I’ve already added a message to the archive. \n\nNext steps include submitting two runs with a default dropout of 0.05 and wd=0.012 using seeds 42 and 123, and also two capturing z' norms with ON and OFF. That totals four runs, which might be manageable. I’ll craft the YAML content for each submission, importing our builder and defining hparams, including the weight decay and potentially adding 'capture_post': True. I’ll include the capture directory as well, even though the builder might ignore it if it's not recognized.**Preparing\
  \ submission commands**\n\nI’m planning to submit a series of runs:\n\n1) \"HDP‑RegCal (wd=0.012, dropout=0.05) — seed=42\"  \n2) \"HDP‑RegCal (wd=0.012, dropout=0.05) — seed=123\"  \n3) \"HDP‑RegCal post-RegCal z'-norm capture (ON, N=2048) — seed=42\"  \n4) \"HDP (RegCal OFF) post-RegCal z'-norm capture (OFF equals pre-head), N=2048 — seed=7\"  \n\nI’m aware that training timeouts could create some heavy load, but that’s manageable. Also, I want to update the archive with results and keep my summary concise, per the desired verbosity level of 3. I’ll include precise commands formatted as /execute_action lines and mention that if capture flags aren’t supported, the run will still train effectively. Time to put this together!**Setting up YAML for submission**\n\nI need to prepare the YAML content, starting by importing the necessary libraries from my storage, specifically using the dual_path_hybrid_motif_heads_regcal to build the network.\n\nI’ll define my hyperparameters with `'head_dropout':\
  \ 0.05`, an aggregator of `'mean_max'`, `regcal_l2` set to True, a learning rate of 0.001, and a grad clip of 1.0, as well as a motif kernel size of 5. In the optimizer, I’ll set the weight decay to 0.012 with a BASE_SEED of 123.\n\nFor the capture post-run ON, I’ll add `'capture_norms': True`, the capture point as `'post'`, and include the capture directory and max number of records. I’ll also create a similar setup with `regcal_l2` set to False, changing the capture directory accordingly. I need to ensure I properly escape any parentheses in the titles. Let’s get this organized!"
