role: model
parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{meta}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Noema II — Working Meta (update, Tick 454)\n  Active runs:\n  - 713, 714: HDP‑RegCal (wd=0.012, head_dropout=0.05) seeds {42,123}. Await results.\n\n  Next actions (queue after 713/714 complete):\n  - Submit post‑RegCal z′-norm capture (ON, seed=42) with capture_dir=.../hdp_regcal_norms_post_on (N=2048).\n  - Submit post‑RegCal z′-norm capture (OFF, seed=7) with capture_dir=.../hdp_regcal_norms_post_off (N=2048).\n\n  After post captures finish:\n  - Run aggregator (test-only) to build ON vs OFF z′-norm table; append to Archive #22 appendix.\n  - If wd=0.012 improves tri-seed mean ≥0.689 without regressions, adopt; else keep wd=0.01.\n\n  Paper maintenance:\n  - Keep tri-seed ON table updated (IDs 689/692/693).\n  - Ensure param count (<1M) and capture code pointers are in Abstract/Appendix.\n\n  Token hygiene:\n  - Prune narrative blocks every ~8 ticks; keep tables/IDs only.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{storage write noema/tools/norms_post_aggregate.py}`\n\
    ```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import os, glob, json\n\n  POST_ON_DIR = \"storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms_post_on\"\n  POST_OFF_DIR = \"storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms_post_off\"\n  OUT_MD = \"storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms_post_on_off.md\"\n\n  def _load(dirpath):\n      data = {}\n      for fp in glob.glob(os.path.join(dirpath, \"*.json\")):\n          try:\n              with open(fp, \"r\") as f:\n                  j = json.load(f)\n              ds = j.get(\"dataset\") or os.path.basename(fp).replace(\".json\",\"\")\n              s = j.get(\"summary\", {})\n              data[ds] = {\n                  \"mean\": float(s.get(\"mean\", 0.0)),\n                  \"std\": float(s.get(\"std\", 0.0)),\n                  \"n\": int(s.get(\"n\", 0)),\n              }\n          except Exception as e:\n              print(f\"Read error {fp}: {e}\")\n      return data\n\n  def main():\n      on = _load(POST_ON_DIR)\n      off = _load(POST_OFF_DIR)\n      datasets = sorted(set(on.keys())\
    \ | set(off.keys()))\n      lines = []\n      lines.append(\"# HDP‑RegCal — Post‑RegCal input norms (z′) ON vs OFF (validation)\\n\")\n      lines.append(\"| Dataset | ON mean ||z′||₂ | OFF mean ||z′||₂ | Ratio (ON/OFF) | ON N | OFF N |\")\n      lines.append(\"|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\")\n      for ds in datasets:\n          o = on.get(ds, {\"mean\": 0.0, \"n\": 0})\n          f = off.get(ds, {\"mean\": 0.0, \"n\": 0})\n          ratio = (o[\"mean\"] / f[\"mean\"]) if (f[\"mean\"] not in (0.0, 0)) else 0.0\n          lines.append(f\"| {ds} | {o['mean']:.4f} | {f['mean']:.4f} | {ratio:.3f} | {o['n']} | {f['n']} |\")\n      if not datasets:\n          lines.append(\"\\nNo post‑RegCal norm JSONs found yet. Re-run after captures complete.\")\n      lines.append(\"\\nNotes:\\n- ON (RegCal=True) should yield ||z′||₂≈1 per sample; OFF mirrors pre‑head scale.\\n- Captures were capped at N=2048 per dataset when configured.\")\n      os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(OUT_MD), exist_ok=True)\n\
    \      with open(OUT_MD, \"w\") as f:\n          f.write(\"\\n\".join(lines))\n      print(f\"Wrote {OUT_MD}\")\n\n  if __name__ == \"__main__\":\n      main()\n```\n\n`/execute_action{storage write noema/results/README_norms.md}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # Norm captures (HDP‑RegCal)\n  Artifacts and scripts for analyzing feature/input norms to regression heads.\n\n  Pre‑head (z) – already captured\n  - JSONs: storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms/{APA,CRI-Off,CRI-On,MRL,PRS}.json\n  - Summary: storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms_summary.md\n  - ON vs OFF (pre‑head z) summary: storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms/hdp_regcal_norms_on_off.md (equality expected)\n\n  Post‑RegCal (z′) – pending captures\n  - ON dir (expected ||z′||₂≈1): storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms_post_on\n  - OFF dir (mirrors pre‑head scale): storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms_post_off\n  - Aggregation script: storage/noema/tools/norms_post_aggregate.py\n  - Output table (to be generated): storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms_post_on_off.md\n\
    \n  Usage\n  1) Run the post‑RegCal capture submissions (ON and OFF) with capture_max_n=2048.\n  2) After they finish, execute the aggregator (via a test‑only submission or local tooling) to build the ON/OFF table.\n  3) Append the table to Archive #22 appendix.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Planning next steps**\n\nIn the current Station environment, the user hasn't made any requests; they're just updating about pending submissions. I have two submissions running (713 and 714) and attempted to submit two more but was blocked by the limit on concurrent submissions. So, I’ll queue those for later. Meanwhile, I can update my Private Memory with the plan, possibly build the ON/OFF z' aggregator test code, and keep an eye on the token usage, which is currently at 101,673.**Organizing next steps**\n\nIt's totally okay! I have a few options to maintain our goals. I could set a meta prompt to guide us, and while we've already done that, some minor updates could help. I also considered previewing archives to read other papers, but since our token budget looks good, focusing on current tasks makes sense. \n\nI'll write an aggregator script to compare z' ON vs OFF once the files appear. After the runs finish, I may need to submit the OFF z' capture using seed=42,\
  \ which complements the previously used seed=7 for OFF. Lastly, I’ll provide actions to schedule the test job for generating the ON_vs_OFF_post table.**Planning the aggregator setup**\n\nI should consider checking the storage directories for the presence of files, although they won’t exist until the runs complete. So, I’ll create the aggregator to handle any missing files gracefully. I also need to keep in mind YAML quoting rules, avoiding special characters and quoting colons inside strings if necessary.\n\nFor actions, I plan to write a test file for the aggregator, but since that could be heavy, using the Research Counter’s \"submit 1\" option with just the test() aggregator script might be better. However, we're at the limit of two running submissions. The test-only approach doesn't count as training, but it does seem to utilize evaluation slots.**Clarifying the submission process**\n\nIt seems that even though the aggregator test likely occupies one slot, it finishes quickly. The\
  \ system indicates that no experiments are currently running, but I know that the concurrent limit applies to pending evaluations, which means the aggregator test would get queued. Since we already have two experiments running, I remember that the system once allowed test-only submissions even with others running. However, since we have two active tasks, it’s better not to submit anything more for now.**Organizing aggregation tasks**\n\nI can write code to store the aggregator for later execution and include instructions for when to run it. One useful step is examining the 'rank score' table for top SOTA. I also want to write a sticky summary and next actions in my Private Memory and maybe update the meta prompts with these next steps.\n\nCreating a general utility to copy JSONs to seed-suffixed locations is a good idea since we've done this before. I'll ensure we're cautious about allowed actions and propose specific tasks: \n\nI'll create the script `norms_post_aggregate.py`, a README\
  \ in the results, and update the meta prompt to plan for submitting captures later, since we ran into a concurrency limit. The aggregator script will compute means and ratios from the JSON files, producing a summary table while ensuring YAML quoting is correct. I’ll finalize the code now!"
