- text: "/execute_action{goto reflect}\n#/execute_action{reflect}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Cross-Archive synthesis and next-step ideation\n\n  What the field converged on\n  - DSConv backbone (no positional encoding) + robust global pooling (mean+max/LSE) is the universal base.\n  - Explicit output-level specialization can push global SOTA (Archive #21), but dataset-specific logic risks non-compliance; acceptable specialization is by task type.\n  - Learned fusion/gating is volatile; deterministic/simple calibration often wins (Averaged MoE; our RegCal).\n\n  Where HDP‑RegCal stands\n  - Tri-seed mean 0.6885 (<1M params) with stability on CRI-Off (~0.08) and strong CRI-On/PRS; competitive to global SOTA but short by ~0.005.\n  - Mechanistic evidence: large pre-head feature-scale disparities across regression tasks; RegCal (per-sample L2) aligns scales.\n\n  High-probability, low-complexity ideas (parsimonious, compliant)\n  1) Classification-head calibration (LogitNorm):\n     - Rationale:\
    \ logits magnitude affects cross-entropy; normalizing classifier features/logits (LogitNorm) can improve Modif/ncRNA without touching regression paths.\n     - Compliant: head-only and shared across classification tasks (no dataset switches).\n     - Plan: add LogitNorm(alpha) before classification logits; small sweep alpha ∈ {5,10,20}. Tri-seed one alpha if promising.\n\n  2) Gentle curriculum for CRI-Off stability via head dropout scheduling (no loop changes):\n     - Emulate schedule by conditioning dropout rate on training mode epoch proxy (cannot access epochs) → alternative: use low-variance stochastic pooling (stochastic depth on motif path) to stabilize early training without changing loop.\n     - Start with tiny stochastic-depth p=0.05 on motif path only (applies to all datasets; compliant).\n\n  3) Power-mean pooling (learned p) as unifying aggregator:\n     - Generalizes mean, max, and LSE; constrain p within [0, 6] via softplus to avoid instability.\n     - Prior learnable-τ\
    \ for LSE regressed; power-mean may behave better when length-normalized; test as drop-in for main path only.\n\n  Kill criteria and guardrails\n  - Any candidate must meet or exceed 0.689 avg on seed 42 first; else abandon quickly (≤2 trials per idea).\n  - Maintain parameter count <1M; no dataset-conditional branches; task-type differences only in heads.\n  - Keep RegCal ON for regression; do not alter optimizer beyond wd=0.01, head_dropout=0.05 unless justified.\n\n  Immediate actions (next ticks)\n  - Implement classification LogitNorm head wrapper around existing HDP‑RegCal submission.\n  - Single-seed (42) runs for alpha ∈ {5, 10}; if ≥0.689 then tri-seed the better alpha.\n  - If time remains, prototype motif-path stochastic depth p=0.05 (seed 42).\n\n  Documentation\n  - Log changes in results/FINAL.md and append to Archive #22 if improvements hold; negative results go to a short addendum.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Considering next steps**\n\nI need to be cautious about implementing new methods that require coding right now. We can focus on providing a reflection plan instead, acknowledging previous insights. It seems there are important architecture patterns to consider, like DSConv no-PE and MoE gating. \n\nI’m thinking about the possibility of unifying task-type head capacity reweighting instead of being dataset-specific. Implementing feature-wise gating to enhance specialization is useful. I’ll also explore tailoring final layers for regression and classification predictions independently.**Exploring calibration methods**\n\nMy RegCal currently focuses on regression heads, leaving classification heads unaffected. For classification tasks like Modif and ncRNA, I think we can improve calibration using temperature scaling and bias normalization, while still training with cross-entropy. Implementing LogitNorm could help normalize logits and enhance classification performance; however,\
  \ we can't alter the loss function directly.\n\nInstead, scaling logits before cross-entropy seems allowed. Unifying across classification tasks is plausible—adding a \"LogitNorm alpha\" to normalize logits could be beneficial. For multilabel PRS, since it's regression, we should retain improvements.**Examining task-specific approaches**\n\nThe global SOTA uses \"task-specific prediction heads,\" which likely means they’re tied to specific datasets. However, I think we can focus on \"task-type-specific tails\" instead, which avoids dataset specificity. To push our results beyond 0.69, more specialization might be needed. \n\nIt’s good to note that while final layers can be tailored for each prediction type, we can't employ dataset-specific methods. I’m considering using Balanced BatchNorm or Domain-specific BatchNorm parameters, but we should avoid relying on dataset names to prevent violations of the dataset-specific methods rule.**Considering next steps in augmentation**\n\nI’m thinking\
  \ about proposing \"Mixup\" or other forms of data augmentation since modifying the training loop isn’t an option. It might be valuable to implement random dropout noise and consider augmentations within the model, like stochastic pooling or specaugment-style masking. However, I need to be cautious about potential risks.\n\nNow that we're at 96k tokens, it seems wise to consolidate insights and suggest a few strategic next steps. I plan to go to the Reflection Chamber to capture this plan and finalize the \"LogitNorm\" calibration code for submission. This could help push our score beyond 0.69, but I must ensure nothing is degraded in the process.**Planning minimal ablation**\n\nI think we can propose a minimal ablation by implementing \"LogitNorm\" in classification heads while keeping other aspects the same. I'll evaluate the tri-seed quickly, despite the environment timing out after 30 minutes per dataset. It’s important to understand the existing submission structure.\n\nI want to\
  \ create a wrapper that modifies the classification head to normalize logits before final output. It seems best not to start new code, so I’ll summarize insights and outline a plan for the Reflection Chamber instead. We should reflect on gaps, propose experiments, and consider pruning heavy tokens as needed."
