    : 0.0,\n          \"logitnorm_apply_to_multilabel\": False,\n          \"logitnorm_order\": \"dropout_then_norm\",\n          \"logitnorm_bias_multiclass\": False\n      }\n```\n\n/execute_action{storage write noema/results/plan_next.md}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Plan update (Tick 469)\n  - LogitNorm v2 (alpha=10) results:\n    • bias=False (ID 744): 0.6838 | ncRNA 0.9538 | CRI-On 0.5062 | PRS 0.5632.\n    • bias=True  (ID 745): 0.6850 | CRI-On 0.5214 | CRI-Off 0.0837 | ncRNA 0.9446.\n    → Still below default ON tri-seed mean (0.6885). Keep default heads.\n  - Next probe: motif-branch stochastic depth p=0.05 (ID pending) with RegCal ON, no LogitNorm. Goal: small lift via stabilized early training on CRI-* without hurting APA/MRL/PRS/Modif.\n  - If ≥0.689 on seed 42, tri-seed; else record as negative and revert to default.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Analyzing research results**


  I need to respond to the user''s submitted system info. They’ve provided runs for LogitNorm v2 with results that show 744 scoring 0.6838 and 745 scoring 0.6850, which are improved compared to previous LogitNorm tests. However, these scores still fall below the baseline mean of 0.6885. It seems wise to consider submitting a variant with a p-value of 0.05 and no LogitNorm to see if we can improve average scores, particularly for CRI-On and CRI-Off.**Evaluating SOTA and next steps**


  The state of the art (SOTA) has changed, indicating a score of 0.6935 with task-specific heads. While they may use dataset-specific heads, we’re sticking to our compliance path and not specializing in SOTA head specialization. I need to summarize that LogitNorm v2 improved ncRNA accuracy to 0.9538 and CRI-On with bias True to 0.5214, but with some tradeoffs in PRS and APA. Overall, we''re still under the baseline, so we should abandon LogitNorm, document the results, and proceed with the motif stochastic depth test using p=0.05 and seed=42.**Managing token usage and evaluation submission**


  I might prune tokens since our current usage is 129,307, which isn''t too high, but earlier there was some token management with cooldown that we don''t need now. I need to submit an evaluation using the hdp_regcal_motif_stodepth module with p_stodepth_motif set to 0.05 while keeping other hyperparameters the same, possibly using seed 42. I''ll log the results and consider updating the archive or private memory, but I want to keep it minimal. Proper YAML quoting is essential if titles have special characters, and I need to reference the module accurately for the training system.**Planning variant submissions**


  I’m thinking we should submit two variants: one with p=0.05 using seed 42, and if time allows, another with p=0.1. However, let’s focus on starting with p=0.05 to be efficient and respect cooldowns, even though the system allows unlimited submissions. I''ll add an aggregator update file for the results and modify logitnorm_ablation.md to include v2 results without rewriting. I’ve noted some intermittent debug errors that didn’t cause failures, so I’ll ignore those. I’ll proceed with the p=0.05 submission using the /execute_action commands.**Streamlining submission process**


  I''m focusing on minimal actions: submitting one evaluation and writing a results update, while all else remains optional. I’ll craft a command for /execute_action to submit with YAML content that references the script and sets hyperparameters, like p_stodepth_motif to 0.05 and logitnorm settings. This will work because create_network uses those hyperparameters. After submission, I’ll document the pivot in results/plan_next.md and consider updating the archive later if needed. I''ll keep the reply concise and include the YAML content code block for the Station to parse. Let''s get this done!'
