tick: 471
role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{storage write noema/results/stochastic_depth_ablation.md}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # Motif-Branch Stochastic Depth Ablation — HDP‑RegCal (seed=42)\n\n  Hypothesis: tiny stochastic depth on the motif branch (p) smooths early training, modestly improving CRI‑On/Off without harming others.\n\n  Config: HDP‑RegCal default (<1M params), RegCal ON for regression heads, no LogitNorm. Only motif path is stochastically gated during training with keep-prob 1-p (inference: no gating).\n\n  Results\n  - p = 0.05 — ID 751: 0.6841 | APA 0.8563 | CRI‑Off 0.0709 | Modif 0.9523 | CRI‑On 0.5111 | PRS 0.5664 | MRL 0.8931 | ncRNA 0.9385\n    • Effect: CRI‑On slightly up vs baseline seed 42 runs, CRI‑Off slightly down; ncRNA down. Net average below default tri‑seed mean ≈ 0.6885.\n  - p = 0.03 — ID 755: running (pending)\n\n  Takeaway\n  - At p=0.05, stochastic depth is neutral to negative on the aggregate. Await p=0.03 to confirm sensitivity. Unless p=0.03 hits ≥ 0.689 on seed 42, we\
    \ will keep motif path deterministic by default.\n```\n\n/execute_action{storage write noema/results/FINAL.md}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # HDP‑RegCal — Final Summary (Updated)\n\n  Current default (RegCal ON, no LogitNorm, deterministic motif path):\n  - Tri‑seed mean (IDs 689/692/693): 0.6885 (> SOTA median 0.6866). Strong CRI‑On and PRS; robust APA/MRL/Modif/ncRNA.\n\n  Negative/neutral follow‑ups:\n  - LogitNorm on ncRNA (feature L2 + alpha=5 or 10): IDs 739 (0.6816), 738 (0.6829), v2 with fixed ordering:\n    • 744 (alpha=10, dropout→norm, no bias): 0.6838; 745 (alpha=10, dropout→norm, bias): 0.6850.\n    • Conclusion: head‑only LogitNorm did not improve average; keep default classifier.\n  - Motif stochastic depth (training only, p):\n    • p=0.05 (ID 751): 0.6841 — neutral/negative; p=0.03 (ID 755) pending.\n    • Conclusion: revert to deterministic motif branch unless p=0.03 clears ≥ 0.689 (unlikely).\n\n  Calibration evidence (unchanged):\n  - Pre‑head fused feature norms show\
    \ ~2.3x cross‑task scale disparity (CRI‑Off ≈ 31.0 vs PRS ≈ 13.3 mean ||z||2).\n  - RegCal aligns scales at regression heads; tri‑seed stability maintained.\n\n  Decision:\n  - Keep the previously published default as primary; document negative probes (LogitNorm, motif stochastic depth).\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{reply 22}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Addendum: LogitNorm and Motif Stochastic Depth — negative/neutral outcomes; default reaffirmed\"\ncontent: |\n  We probed two low‑complexity tweaks on top of HDP‑RegCal:\n\n  1) LogitNorm for ncRNA (feature L2 + alpha)\n     - alpha=5: ID 739 → 0.6816; alpha=10: ID 738 → 0.6829.\n     - With corrected ordering (Dropout → L2‑norm → Dense) and bias toggle:\n       • ID 744 (alpha=10, no bias): 0.6838\n       • ID 745 (alpha=10, bias): 0.6850\n     - Conclusion: neutral/negative on average; keep default classifier.\n\n  2) Motif‑branch stochastic depth (training‑only gate)\n     - p=0.05: ID 751 → 0.6841 (CRI‑On ↑ slight;\
    \ ncRNA ↓; average below default tri‑seed mean ≈ 0.6885).\n     - p=0.03: ID 755 running (sensitivity). If not ≥ 0.689 on seed 42, we will keep motif path deterministic.\n\n  Files:\n  - LogitNorm ablation: storage/noema/results/logitnorm_ablation.md\n  - Stochastic‑depth ablation: storage/noema/results/stochastic_depth_ablation.md\n  - Final summary (updated): storage/noema/results/FINAL.md\n\n  Decision: Reaffirm the published default (RegCal ON; deterministic motif; <1M params), as it remains the strongest and most stable across seeds. If the pending p=0.03 run is also neutral, we will close this line.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Evaluating model adjustments**\n\nI’m thinking about proposing some small schedule modifications, but it seems like those would be beyond the allowed configuration since the training loop is fixed. We can only change the network and optimizer. I attempted a head dropout schedule, but it feels unnatural since I can’t access the epoch. \n\nMaybe I could try the \"cosine classifier” for ncRNA and normalize the classification weight vector. But previous reflections said to consider it only if LogitNorm fails. \n\nWe already tried some improvements and got close to our tri-seed mean, but I’m hesitant to push further. Another low-risk idea would be to apply LogitNorm with bias true, although modif isn’t the limiting dataset since its AUC is already quite high. Alternatively, we might pursue the tri-seed of our best run, which achieved a 0.6910.**Considering task-specific methods**\n\nI'm contemplating the use of \"task-specific prediction heads\" as discussed in paper #21,\
  \ but there might be compliance issues since they don't seem to allow specialties based on dataset-specific logic. Their definition of \"task-specific\" could relate more to task types than datasets, even if they claim a global SOTA of 0.6935. \n\nWithout clear details, it might not be worth attempting. Alternatively, I could explore other lineages or archived papers for new ideas, but isolation could be limiting. While I can't import code, I can replicate the concept of calibrations for specific tasks. However, if dataset-specific methods aren't allowed, I need to tread carefully here.**Wrapping up research tasks**\n\nThe research guidelines don't allow for dataset-specific methods, but I can use \"task type\" variations. \"Task-specific prediction heads\" likely implies that each dataset has its own dedicated head, which is already set up in the system. Adjustments like widening regression heads or varying dropout per dataset might breach compliance.\n\nI think it’s time to close this\
  \ iteration and document the negative outcomes from LogitNorm and motif stodepth, possibly pruning tokens. I'll format my actions accordingly and note my location at the Research Counter. We could update archive #22 with our findings and propose an aggregator summary to compile results, updating results/FINAL.md to reflect those outcomes. It might also be worthwhile to switch permanently to the default configuration.**Planning next steps**\n\nI have several possible actions to consider. First, I can append our motif stodepth results to the existing logitnorm_ablation.md. After the p=0.03 runs are complete, I can analyze those results, but they're still running at the moment.\n\nThere’s also the option to use Token Management to prune older ticks, such as 466-471. I might want to prune earlier logs too, but there's a cooldown period. The last prune was at tick 460, so it might be available again.\n\nI could schedule further seeds at p=0.02, but it might be wise to wait for the results from\
  \ p=0.03 first. I should also keep our \"Working Meta\" up-to-date because LogitNorm underperformed and stochastic depth 0.05 yielded a 0.6841; I need to note that we're currently running with p=0.03.\n\nFinally, I could visit the archive to add an addendum summarizing the negative results to finalize the analysis.**Outlining next steps**\n\nNow that the instructions have returned to normal, I should stay concise while planning my actions. First, I’ll write the document results/stochastic_depth_ablation.md with results from p=0.05, noting that p=0.03 is still pending. \n\nNext, I need to update FINAL.md to reflect that the classification interventions didn’t help and that stodepth at p=0.05 is neutral. \n\nI’m considering a tri-seed run with defaults to maintain a stable scoreboard, although we’ve already had tri-seed results previously. \n\nI can optionally check the scoreboard ranking by score to compare with SOTA, although that's not entirely necessary. I should write and update the\
  \ archive with a short addendum summarizing the negative results. \n\nFinally, I’ll fix the \"Working Meta\" accordingly and remember to include a YAML block while being cautious about quoting special characters. Let’s get started!"
