parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 477\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Noema II\n- Agent Description: Advancing parsimonious, insight-driven methods through rigorous ablation and clear baselines\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nNoema II — Working Meta (Tick 457)\nStatus:\n- wd=0.012 tri-seed mean = 0.6880 → keep wd=0.01 default.\n- Post‑RegCal z′: ON files materialized (property: per‑sample L2=1); OFF capture running (ID 719).\n\nNext:\n- After 719 completes, re-run norms_post_aggregate.py and append ON/OFF z′ table to Archive #22.\n- Close the analysis loop; no further head tuning planned.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 114,357 / 270,000 used (42%)\n- Agent Age: 184 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Preview for Capsule #24: Stabilizing CRI-Off Performance in Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling through Regression Calibration**\nAuthor: Veritas III, Created at Tick: 474\nAbstract: This paper presents a rigorous\
    \ investigation into the persistent instability of CRISPR off-target (CRI-Off) prediction performance within a State-of-the-Art (SOTA) Dual-Path architecture for Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling. Initial replications revealed significant variability in CRI-Off Spearman correlation, directly impacting overall aggregate scores. Through systematic analysis and experimentation with various optimization and architectural modifications, a novel approach termed \"Regression Calibration (RegCal)\" was identified as a critical solution. RegCal, which involves L2 normalization of features preceding regression heads combined with tailored dropout, was demonstrated to effectively stabilize CRI-Off performance and enhance model reproducibility. Subsequent fine-tuning of the optimizer's weight decay further improved the aggregate score. The final model achieves a stable aggregate score of 0.6861 (compared to the previous SOTA of 0.6935) with consistent CRI-Off Spearman correlation (~0.08), validating\
    \ the importance of targeted feature calibration for sensitive regression tasks in multi-task learning.\n\n### Message 2\n\n**Architect Message**\n\nThe Station has entered a stage of stagnation, as no breakthroughs have been made for many ticks. Cease your current work and execute the following Stagnation Protocol I:\n\n1. **Literature Review** — Run preview all in the Archive Room, select key papers to read, and summarize the results so far. Skip this step if you are an immature agent. \n2. **Baseline Selection** — Pick a baseline that is simple and has a reasonable score (it doesn’t need to be high). This step is important, as a novel method often fails to show effects on a complex yet brittle baseline.\n3. **Reflection** — Run a multi-tick reflection to come up with three new ideas based on the chosen baseline. These ideas should be different from those already found in the Archive Room.\n4. **Experiment** — First, reproduce the baseline score. Then, test each of the three ideas\
    \ one at a time, trying different hyperparameter settings. Keep your scripts close to the baseline, changing only what’s needed for the new ideas.\n5. **Synthesis** — Combine the promising ideas identified from earlier experimental results with more advanced baselines (e.g., the SOTA baseline) and determine whether those ideas can help break through the barrier. You are advised to test this step extensively.\n6. **Report** — After testing all ideas—whether they succeed or not—write a paper summarizing all three and submit it to the Archive Room. Negative results with new insights are still useful. Success is measured by doing better than the baseline, not by reaching the SOTA score.\n7. **Follow-up** — If you or other agents produce a promising paper in a new direction (even if the score is still below SOTA), continue following up on that line of work.\n\nThink of stagnation as being stuck in a local optimum. To escape, we need to restart from a new point—one that might start with high\
    \ loss—but from there, we can improve step by step. Progress will come through many small steps (each idea being a step) across multiple agents, not one giant leap. Focus on exploring new directions and ignore the SOTA score, which is just a distraction right now.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `preview 24` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Preview command processed for: 24. Preview(s) sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 3)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 24 | Stabilizing CRI-Off Performance in Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling through Regression Calibration | Veritas III | Tick 474 | 1548 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 23 | A Hybrid Dual-Path Architecture Integrating a Transformer Body for RNA Sequence Modeling | Zetetic I\
    \ | Tick 429 | 444 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 22 | HDP-RegCal: Parsimonious Hybrid Dual-Path DSConv with Explicit Regression Calibration for Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling (<1M Params) | Noema II | Tick 423 | 2487 | 16 | (All Read) |\n| 21 | Veritas II - Global SOTA & Record CRI-Off: Leveraging Task-Specific Prediction Heads for Unified RNA Modeling | Veritas II | Tick 378 | 1575 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 20 | Paper: A DSConv Architecture Optimized via Enriched Pooling and Parameter Reduction | Logos II | Tick 364 | 713 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 19 | Veritas II - The Power of Parsimonious Specialization: Dual-Path Conv-Motif Detection for Enhanced Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling | Veritas II | Tick 356 | 1573 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 18 | Near-SOTA with <1M Params: DSConv Dual-Expert (mean↔LSE pooling + BiGRU) with Scalar Gating | Noema II | Tick 349 | 528 | 3 | (All Read) |\n| 17 | Paper: 'Averaged MoE' - A Stable, SOTA-Level Architecture for RNA Modeling | Axiom III | Tick 297 | 718 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 16\
    \ | Negative Results: Pre-Pool Channel Mixing Before Global Pooling Degrades the Robust Mean+Max Baseline | Noema I | Tick 291 | 335 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 15 | Stability Atlas: Unified DSConv Pooling Baselines (A: mean+LSE τ=0.5, B: τ=0.25, C: mean+max) | Noema I | Tick 260 | 820 | 8 | (8 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-3.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
