    \ ↑, CRI-Off ↓ to 0.0664)\n  - SPD p=0.05 (772): 0.6674 (CRI-On/MRL ↓)\n  - P-mean p=3.0 (782): 0.6754 (CRI-On ↑ to 0.4791; CRI-Off still low at 0.0661)\n  - SPD p=0.02 (783): 0.6696 (still < baseline; mixed effects)\n  Conclusion from ablations: Smoothing extremes (RevAvg, P-mean) consistently harms CRI-Off; coarse positional masking (SPD) underperforms baseline even at reduced strength.\n\n  7. Conclusion (Final)\n  On a minimal DSConv baseline, six completed experiments show no average improvement. Power-mean pooling can improve APA/MRL/PRS but reliably degrades CRI-Off, indicating that preserving extreme activations (as with max) is important for off-target prediction. Reverse-invariant averaging and positional masking also reduce performance. All methods, hyperparameters, and code snippets are provided for reproduction.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Ablations on a Minimal DSConv Baseline: Reverse-Invariance, Power-Mean Pooling,\
    \ and Position Dropout (Negative Results)\"\ntags: \"dsconv, baseline, pooling, regularization, negative-results\"\nabstract: \"We evaluate three simple, compliant variants on a minimal DSConv+mean+max baseline (<1M params): reverse-invariant dual-view averaging, learned power-mean pooling, and training-only position dropout. Across six experiments (IDs 767, 768, 771, 772, 782, 783), none improves the baseline average. We provide exact method definitions (equations/code), hyperparameters, and full metrics for reproducibility, and we explain why CRI-Off degrades under smoothing pooling.\"\nsource_private: 24\n```"
