- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 489\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Noema II\n- Agent Description: Advancing parsimonious, insight-driven methods through rigorous ablation and clear baselines\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nNoema II — Working Meta (Tick 457)\nStatus:\n- wd=0.012 tri-seed mean = 0.6880 → keep wd=0.01 default.\n- Post‑RegCal z′: ON files materialized (property: per‑sample L2=1); OFF capture running (ID 719).\n\nNext:\n- After 719 completes, re-run norms_post_aggregate.py and append ON/OFF z′ table to Archive #22.\n- Close the analysis loop; no further head tuning planned.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 180,853 / 270,000 used (67%)\n- Agent Age: 196 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour archive submission 'Negative Results on a Minimal DSConv Baseline: Reverse-Invariance, Power-Mean Pooling, and Position Dropout' has been accepted for publication! Score: 6/10. The capsule has been\
    \ created and a reviewer comment has been added.\n\n**Full Reviewer Feedback:**\nThis is an exemplary negative results paper and a perfect revision of the previous submission. The agent has successfully reformatted the work into a complete, static, and professional manuscript. The paper is exceptionally clear, with precise, reproducible method descriptions that include both equations and code snippets. The analysis provides valuable insights, particularly regarding the trade-off between smoothing pooling operators and performance on the CRI-Off task. While the findings are negative, the quality of the research and the clarity of the reporting make this a valuable contribution to the station's knowledge base.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper's key insight is that smoothing operations like Power-mean hurt CRI-Off while helping other tasks. A logical and promising next step would be to test the hypothesis that task-specific pooling is beneficial. For example, one could implement\
    \ a compliant model that uses Power-mean pooling for the classification heads (Modif, ncRNA) while retaining the robust mean+max pooling for the regression heads. This would directly test the paper's main conclusion and could lead to a net performance improvement.\n\n**Tip:** You can update your published capsule to address the reviewer's suggestions. Use `/execute_action{update 25-1}` followed by YAML with updated content to revise your original submission. \n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `create` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: title, tags, abstract, content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Your archive submission 'Negative Results on a Minimal DSConv Baseline: Reverse-Invariance, Power-Mean Pooling, and Position Dropout' has been queued for evaluation by the review system. You will be notified of the results. The submission will be published if it meets the publication standards (score >= 6/10).\n\
    \n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 3)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 25 | Negative Results on a Minimal DSConv Baseline: Reverse-Invariance, Power-Mean Pooling, and Position Dropout | Noema II | Tick 488 | 937 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 24 | Stabilizing CRI-Off Performance in Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling through Regression Calibration | Veritas III | Tick 474 | 1548 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 23 | A Hybrid Dual-Path Architecture Integrating a Transformer Body for RNA Sequence Modeling | Zetetic I | Tick 429 | 444 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 22 | HDP-RegCal: Parsimonious Hybrid Dual-Path DSConv with Explicit Regression Calibration for Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling (<1M Params) | Noema II | Tick 423 | 2487 | 16 | (All Read) |\n| 21 | Veritas II - Global SOTA & Record CRI-Off: Leveraging Task-Specific Prediction Heads for Unified RNA Modeling | Veritas II | Tick\
    \ 378 | 1575 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 20 | Paper: A DSConv Architecture Optimized via Enriched Pooling and Parameter Reduction | Logos II | Tick 364 | 713 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 19 | Veritas II - The Power of Parsimonious Specialization: Dual-Path Conv-Motif Detection for Enhanced Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling | Veritas II | Tick 356 | 1573 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 18 | Near-SOTA with <1M Params: DSConv Dual-Expert (mean↔LSE pooling + BiGRU) with Scalar Gating | Noema II | Tick 349 | 528 | 3 | (All Read) |\n| 17 | Paper: 'Averaged MoE' - A Stable, SOTA-Level Architecture for RNA Modeling | Axiom III | Tick 297 | 718 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 16 | Negative Results: Pre-Pool Channel Mixing Before Global Pooling Degrades the Robust Mean+Max Baseline | Noema I | Tick 291 | 335 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-3.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n\
    - Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
