role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{storage write noema/results/head_probes_summary.md}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  HDP‑RegCal head probes (seed=42)\n  - 795 — Regression-head WeightNorm: 0.6748 | CRI-Off 0.0729 | CRI-On 0.4799 | PRS 0.5591 | ncRNA 0.9277 (negative vs default ~0.6885)\n  - 796 — Cosine classifier (classification only): 0.6802 | CRI-Off 0.0783 | CRI-On 0.5020 | PRS 0.5633 | ncRNA 0.9369 (negative)\n  Conclusion: keep default heads; RegCal remains the effective stabilization for regression; head changes did not help.\n```\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{reply 25}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Addendum: Head Probes (WeightNorm, Cosine) — Negative Results\"\ncontent: |\n  We tested two orthogonal, low-complexity head variants atop HDP‑RegCal (seed=42):\n  - Regression-head WeightNorm (ID 795): 0.6748 | CRI-Off 0.0729 | CRI-On 0.4799 | PRS 0.5591 | ncRNA 0.9277.\n  - Cosine classifier for classification heads (ID 796): 0.6802 | CRI-Off 0.0783 | CRI-On 0.5020\
    \ | PRS 0.5633 | ncRNA 0.9369.\n  Both underperformed the default (~0.6885 tri-seed). This supports the conclusion that explicit Regression Calibration is the key stabilizer for regression tasks, while classification heads are already well-conditioned. Artifacts: storage/noema/results/head_probes_summary.md.\n```\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{storage write noema/submissions/dsconv_minibase_expts2.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  \"\"\"\n  Minimal DSConv + pooling baseline with three new probes:\n    - shift_avg: weight-tied shift averaging h, (h + roll±1)/3 (no new params)\n    - multires_b: multi-resolution pooling via block max mean (additive pooled stat)\n    - topk_k: mean + top-k mean pooling (per-channel)\n  Heads: single hidden MLP (128); no motif; no RegCal. <1M params.\n  \"\"\"\n  from typing import Any, Dict, Sequence\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random, lax\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE\
    \ = 64\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          \"learning_rate\": 0.001,\n          \"hidden_dim\": 256,\n          \"kernel_size\": 7,\n          \"dropout_rate\": 0.10,\n          \"ds_dilations\": [1,2,4,8,16],\n          \"head_hidden\": 128,\n          \"head_dropout\": 0.05,\n          # probes\n          \"shift_avg\": False,\n          \"multires_b\": 0,   # e.g., 8 for blocks of 8\n          \"topk_k\": 0        # e.g., 8 for mean of top-8 per channel\n      }\n\n  class DSConvBlock(nn.Module):\n      hidden_dim: int\n      kernel_size: int\n      dilation: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic: bool = True):\n          residual = x\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.hidden_dim,\n                      kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,),\n                      feature_group_count=self.hidden_dim,\n                      dilation=(self.dilation,),\n                      padding=\"SAME\")(x)\n         \
    \ x = jax.nn.gelu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.hidden_dim, kernel_size=(1,), padding=\"SAME\")(x)\n          x = jax.nn.gelu(x)\n          return residual + x\n\n  class DSConvBackbone(nn.Module):\n      hidden_dim: int\n      kernel_size: int\n      dilations: Sequence[int]\n      dropout_rate: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic: bool = True):\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.hidden_dim, kernel_size=(1,), padding=\"SAME\")(x)\n          h = jax.nn.silu(h)\n          for d in self.dilations:\n              h = DSConvBlock(self.hidden_dim, self.kernel_size, d, self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic)\n          return h\n\n  def global_mean_max(h):\n      return jnp.concatenate([jnp.mean(h, axis=1), jnp.max(h, axis=1)], axis=-1)\n\n  def shift_average(h):\n      return (h + jnp.roll(h, 1, axis=1) + jnp.roll(h, -1, axis=1)) / 3.0\n\n  def multires_block_max_mean(h,\
    \ b: int):\n      if b <= 1:  # degenerate\n          return jnp.max(h, axis=1)\n      B, L, C = h.shape\n      pad = (b - (L % b)) % b\n      if pad > 0:\n          pad_width = ((0,0),(0,pad),(0,0))\n          h_pad = jnp.pad(h, pad_width, mode='constant', constant_values=0.0)\n          Lp = L + pad\n      else:\n          h_pad = h\n          Lp = L\n      hb = h_pad.reshape(B, Lp//b, b, C)\n      block_max = hb.max(axis=2)         # (B, nblocks, C)\n      return block_max.mean(axis=1)      # (B, C) mean across blocks\n\n  def topk_mean(h, k: int):\n      # mean of top-k along sequence per channel\n      if k <= 0 or k > h.shape[1]:\n          return jnp.max(h, axis=1)  # fallback to max\n      x = jnp.swapaxes(h, 1, 2)        # (B, C, L)\n      vals, _ = lax.top_k(x, k)        # top-k over last axis\n      m = vals.mean(axis=-1)           # (B, C)\n      return m\n\n  class MiniDSConvNet2(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int\n      task_type: str\n      hidden_dim: int = 256\n      kernel_size:\
    \ int = 7\n      ds_dilations: Sequence[int] = (1,2,4,8,16)\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.10\n      head_hidden: int = 128\n      head_dropout: float = 0.05\n      shift_avg_flag: bool = False\n      multires_b: int = 0\n      topk_k: int = 0\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic: bool = True):\n          h = DSConvBackbone(self.hidden_dim, self.kernel_size, self.ds_dilations, self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic)\n          if self.shift_avg_flag:\n              h = shift_average(h)\n          # base pooled stats\n          mean = jnp.mean(h, axis=1)\n          if self.topk_k > 0:\n              extreme = topk_mean(h, self.topk_k)\n          else:\n              extreme = jnp.max(h, axis=1)\n          pooled = [mean, extreme]\n          if self.multires_b and self.multires_b > 1:\n              pooled.append(multires_block_max_mean(h, int(self.multires_b)))\n          z = jnp.concatenate(pooled, axis=-1)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.head_hidden)(z)\n      \
    \    z = nn.LayerNorm(epsilon=1e-6)(z)\n          z = jax.nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.head_dropout)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          out = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(z)\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\" and self.d_output == 1:\n              return out.squeeze(-1)\n          return out\n\n  class WrappedRNANetwork:\n      def __init__(self, d_output=1, task_type=\"regression\", hparams: Dict[str, Any] = None):\n          hp = hparams or {}\n          self.net = MiniDSConvNet2(\n              d_output=d_output,\n              task_type=task_type,\n              hidden_dim=int(hp.get(\"hidden_dim\", 256)),\n              kernel_size=int(hp.get(\"kernel_size\", 7)),\n              ds_dilations=tuple(hp.get(\"ds_dilations\", [1,2,4,8,16])),\n              dropout_rate=float(hp.get(\"dropout_rate\", 0.10)),\n              head_hidden=int(hp.get(\"head_hidden\", 128)),\n              head_dropout=float(hp.get(\"head_dropout\", 0.05)),\n              shift_avg_flag=bool(hp.get(\"\
    shift_avg\", False)),\n              multires_b=int(hp.get(\"multires_b\", 0)),\n              topk_k=int(hp.get(\"topk_k\", 0)),\n          )\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          pk, dk = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.net.init({'params': pk, 'dropout': dk}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.net.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n              return self.net.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return WrappedRNANetwork(d_output=hparams.get('d_output', 1), task_type=hparams.get('task_type', 'regression'), hparams=hparams)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate,\
    \ weight_decay=0.01)\n```\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Idea A — ShiftAvg(±1) + mean+max (minimal DSConv baseline) — seed=42\"\ntags: \"baseline, dsconv, pooling, shift-averaging, invariance\"\nabstract: \"Weight-tied shift averaging of backbone features (h + roll±1)/3 prior to global mean+max pooling; zero new params. Tests robustness without smoothing extremes.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/noema/submissions')\n  from dsconv_minibase_expts2 import create_network as build_net, create_optimizer as make_opt\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          \"learning_rate\": 0.001,\n          \"hidden_dim\": 256,\n          \"kernel_size\": 7,\n          \"dropout_rate\": 0.10,\n          \"ds_dilations\": [1,2,4,8,16],\n          \"head_hidden\": 128,\n          \"head_dropout\": 0.05,\n          \"shift_avg\": True,\n          \"multires_b\": 0,\n          \"topk_k\": 0,\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n\
    \      return build_net(hparams)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return make_opt(learning_rate)\n```\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Idea B — mean + TopK-mean (k=8) pooling (minimal DSConv baseline) — seed=42\"\ntags: \"baseline, dsconv, pooling, topk, robustness\"\nabstract: \"Replace max with per-channel mean of top‑k activations (k=8) and concatenate with global mean. Hypothesis: preserves informative extremes with robustness vs. pure max; avoids LSE smoothing.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/noema/submissions')\n  from dsconv_minibase_expts2 import create_network as build_net, create_optimizer as make_opt\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          \"learning_rate\": 0.001,\n          \"hidden_dim\": 256,\n          \"kernel_size\": 7,\n          \"dropout_rate\": 0.10,\n          \"ds_dilations\": [1,2,4,8,16],\n          \"head_hidden\": 128,\n       \
    \   \"head_dropout\": 0.05,\n          \"shift_avg\": False,\n          \"multires_b\": 0,\n          \"topk_k\": 8,\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return build_net(hparams)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return make_opt(learning_rate)\n```"
thinking_content: "**Planning next steps**\n\nIt seems the request is about taking specific actions, so I should effectively summarize the head probe results in a storage file. I also need to reply to Archive #25 or #22 with any negative results related to the head probe.  \n\nLooking ahead, I could plan three novel ideas that are orthogonal, such as \"head capacity rebalancing,\" \"RegCal scaling factor trade-off alpha for regression heads,\" and \"feature-wise affine normalization (LayerScale).\" I may think about incorporating gradient norm clipping for regression heads, but there are constraints in modifying the training loop.**Evaluating modifications and results**\n\nI've got an idea about the allowed modifications for creating the network and optimizer. I can apply a mask by parameter subtree names specifically for regression or classification heads. The training code passes the relevant parameters for both. \n\nHowever, we've hit a snag, as previous optimizations with 'HDP-RegCal\
  \ default' aren't working. The architect's message suggests picking a new baseline along with three ideas, but so far, the results with minimal DSConv are mostly negative. For the next step, \"Synthesis,\" I’ll combine promising concepts but note that none have improved the baseline.**Reflecting on baseline options**\n\nI'm considering whether to choose a different baseline, possibly going with \"Averaged MoE\" from Archive #17 or \"Transformer body\" from #23, but I want to keep things simple. There’s also the option of CRI-On/Off specialized methods. \n\nSince the instructions say to propose another three novel ideas if we're mature enough, I think we could look into incorporating \"phase-shifted dilations ensemble.\" My idea is to create \"Weight-tied shifted kernels\" (WTSK), where I share convolution filters to apply to shifted input windows without introducing new parameters. This could effectively capture sub-position invariances!**Exploring new ideas**\n\nFor the second idea, I'm\
  \ considering \"Frequency-domain augmentation via DCT channel mixing\" before pooling, but I'm wary since earlier negative results in Noema I indicated that mixing channels before pooling wasn't effective. \n\nInstead, I could try \"Small spectral gating,\" which is parameter-free. By applying DCT along the length, I can create low/high energy ratios as additional pooled statistics, adding them to the feature set. \n\nFor the third idea, there’s \"Scale-limited LSE,\" but since we aren’t using LSE in the default framework, I may opt for something like \"Conv head anti-aliased pooling,\" which would use Gaussian smoothing before max pooling to reduce noise. But I need to avoid the smoothing issues we’ve faced before!**Formulating new ideas and plans**\n\nI’m reflecting on some ideas to improve our approach. Observations show that CRI-Off is sensitive to extremes, and using RegCal can help. I was thinking about calibrating regression heads with a learned scalar \"temperature\" for different\
  \ task types. However, I'm uncertain about the impact of different loss types like MSE or Spearman. \n\nAs for new methods, I could implement \"Adaptive dropout schedules\" or \"Multi-resolution pooling,\" which involve computing local and global means and max values. The immediate next steps are to log results, including updating the results file and proposing new ideas like shift-averaged features or quantile pooling. I’ll create and submit the necessary experiments while ensuring that I avoid exceeding token limits.**Implementing a multi-resolution pooling algorithm**\n\nI’m planning to implement a simple algorithm for multi-resolution pooling. First, I’ll calculate block statistics by padding input data if necessary, restructuring it for mean and max calculations over blocks, and then returning a combined result. This way, I can create a pooled output that includes global and block statistics without expanding dimensions too much.\n\nNext, I’ll set up top-k pooling by computing the\
  \ mean of the top-k values for each channel, reshaping data as needed. I’ll add control flags to toggle between methods and choose to run two experiments: \"Idea A — ShiftAvg + mean+max\" and \"Idea B — MultiRes b=8 + mean+max.\" Then, I'll submit these experiments and log everything. Let's navigate to the Research Counter!"
