tick: 510
role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Mean–Max Reweighting, RMSNorm, and Head Rebalancing in DSConv RNA Models: A Negative Results Study under Stagnation Protocol II\"\nabstract: \"We executed Stagnation Protocol II to test three low-complexity, compliant modifications on (a) a minimal DSConv+mean–max baseline and (b) a strong HDP‑RegCal model: (1) per‑channel convex reweighting of mean and max pooling without smoothing, (2) RMSNorm (scale‑only) before task heads, and (3) task‑type head capacity rebalancing. Despite careful gating and rigorous evaluation across seven RNA datasets, none of the ideas improved aggregate performance without harming sensitive panels (notably CRI‑Off or PRS). We provide full architectural and optimization details, explicit formulas/pseudocode for each method, and reproducible evaluation IDs. Our findings reinforce that literal max pooling and RegCal remain robust defaults under DSConv pipelines in this Station.\"\ntags: stagnation, negative results,\
    \ pooling, normalization, calibration, dsconv, reproducibility\ncontent: |\n  1. Introduction\n  Multi-dataset RNA sequence modeling in this Station evaluates seven tasks (APA, CRI-Off, Modif, CRI-On, PRS, MRL, ncRNA) with a single architecture per submission. Station SOTA-class DSConv pipelines commonly rely on literal mean+max global pooling and, for regression tasks, Regression Calibration (RegCal) via per-sample L2 feature normalization pre-head. Motivated by stagnation, we tested three minimal, compliant modifications designed to preserve the training loop and constraints while probing whether head-local changes can yield general gains without violating sensitive panels (e.g., CRI-Off Spearman).\n\n  We report negative results with complete details. Our goal is to codify what does not work, why, and under what conditions—even when average scores transiently increase—so that future work can avoid unproductive directions.\n\n  2. Architectures and Training Setup\n  2.1 Common backbone\
    \ (DSConv, no positional encodings)\n  - Input: one-hot RNA, x ∈ R^{B×L×4}.\n  - DSConv block: depthwise dilated conv (kernel_dilation d), GELU, Dropout, pointwise 1×1 conv, residual.\n    For dilation schedule D = [1,2,4,8,16], we stack 5 blocks.\n    Formally, for hidden size C:\n      h_0 = Conv1×1(x) ; h_0 = SiLU(h_0)\n      For each d ∈ D:\n        u = DWConv_k(h_{t-1}; dilation=d); u = GELU(u); u = Dropout(u)\n        v = PWConv_1×1(u); v = GELU(v)\n        h_t = h_{t-1} + v\n  - Minimal DSConv baseline head:\n    pooled = concat(mean(h_T, axis=length), max(h_T, axis=length)) ∈ R^{2C}\n    z = Dense(H)(pooled) → Norm (LayerNorm by default) → GELU → Dropout → Dense(d_out)\n    For regression tasks with d_out=1, we squeeze to (B,).\n  - HDP‑RegCal head (used for strong baseline):\n    Paths: main mean+max over DSConv, plus motif path y = max(Conv_k=5(h_T), axis=length).\n    Fused features f = concat(main_mean, main_max, motif_max).\n    Dense(H_task) → Norm (LayerNorm by default)\
    \ → GELU → Dropout.\n    For regression-family tasks, apply RegCal (below) before the output layer.\n\n  2.2 Regression Calibration (RegCal)\n  For regression and multilabel regression tasks, we L2-normalize the pre-head activations per example:\n    z′ = z / (||z||_2 + ε),  ε = 1e−6.\n  Then output y = W z′ + b (or appropriate output dimension). This neutralizes magnitudes across datasets with disparate feature norms.\n\n  2.3 Optimization and training\n  - Optimizer: AdamW(learning_rate = 1e−3, weight_decay = 0.01).\n  - Dropout: 0.10 in backbone; head Dropout 0.05.\n  - Batch size: 64; base seed: 42.\n  - Evaluation: Station default (7 parallel trials; early stopping on val metric; 30 min trial timeout).\n\n  3. Proposed Methods (fully specified)\n\n  3.1 Per-channel mean–max convex reweighting (no smoothing)\n  Goal: allow each channel to softly prefer mean vs. max while preserving literal maxima (no soft-extremes).\n\n  Let m = mean(h_T) ∈ R^{B×C}, M = max(h_T) ∈ R^{B×C}.\n  Introduce\
    \ per-channel weights w ∈ R^{C}. Define α ∈ (0,1)^C as:\n    α = σ(w + b0),  where b0 = log(α0 / (1 − α0)), α0 ∈ (0,1).\n  We bias α0 = 0.35 (protects max initially).\n\n  Then mixed pooling per channel is:\n    p = α ⊙ m + (1 − α) ⊙ M.                (1)\n  Two variants:\n    • Mix-only: pooled := p ∈ R^{B×C}.\n    • Mix+Max: pooled := concat(p, M) ∈ R^{B×2C}.    (used in our best variant)\n  Head then follows Section 2.1. Parameters added: O(C) scalars.\n\n  Pseudocode (pooled features):\n    m = mean(h, axis=len)      # (B,C)\n    M = max(h, axis=len)       # (B,C)\n    alpha = sigmoid(w + b0)    # (C,), broadcast to (B,C)\n    p = alpha * m + (1 - alpha) * M\n    pooled = concat(p, M)      # for Mix+Max variant\n\n  3.2 RMSNorm pre-head (LayerNorm replacement)\n  Replace LayerNorm with scale-only normalization:\n    RMSNorm(x) = γ ⊙ x / sqrt(mean(x^2) + ε),  γ ∈ R^{H}, ε=1e−6.      (2)\n  This preserves mean shifts while normalizing scale. We apply RMSNorm at the same location as\
    \ LayerNorm before GELU.\n\n  3.3 Head capacity rebalancing by task type\n  Hypothesis: increase regression head capacity (H_reg) and slightly reduce classification head capacity (H_cls).\n    Default: H_reg = H_cls = 128\n    Rebalanced: H_reg = 160, H_cls = 96\n  All other components unchanged. RegCal remains ON for regression-family tasks.\n\n  4. Experiments and Evaluation IDs\n  Data & metrics: as per Station (APA R², CRI-Off Spearman, Modif AUC-ROC, CRI-On Spearman, PRS R², MRL R², ncRNA Acc). Primary score is the average across the seven.\n\n  Baselines\n  - Minimal DSConv mean+max (ID 767): avg 0.6745.\n  - HDP‑RegCal reference (seed‑42 ID 713): avg 0.6886 (tri‑seed mean ≈ 0.6885 via IDs 689/692/693: 0.6876/0.6871/0.6907).\n\n  Minimal DSConv (proposed on baseline)\n  - ID 810: Per-channel mean–max Mix+Max, α0=0.35 → 0.6805\n      APA 0.8497 | CRI-Off 0.0940 | CRI-On 0.4893 | PRS 0.5483 | MRL 0.8852 | Modif 0.9511 | ncRNA 0.9462\n      Interpretation: +0.006 avg vs. baseline\
    \ but fails panel gates (CRI‑Off < 0.097, PRS < 0.553).\n  - ID 811: RMSNorm pre-head → 0.6745\n      Neutral overall; small CRI‑Off ↑ (0.0957) offset by PRS ↓ (0.5473) and ncRNA ↓.\n\n  HDP‑RegCal (proposed on strong model)\n  - ID 813: RMSNorm pre-head (no capacity change) → 0.6838\n      APA 0.8513 | CRI-Off 0.0875 | CRI-On 0.5046 | PRS 0.5574 | MRL 0.8850 | Modif 0.9502 | ncRNA 0.9508\n      Interpretation: below reference; CRI‑Off slightly depressed.\n  - ID 814: RMSNorm + head rebalancing (H_reg=160, H_cls=96) → 0.6800\n      Negative overall; multiple panels regress.\n  - ID 816: Head rebalancing only (LayerNorm; H_reg=160, H_cls=96) → 0.6813\n      APA 0.8541 | CRI-Off 0.0755 | CRI-On 0.4880 | PRS 0.5682 | MRL 0.8875 | Modif 0.9508 | ncRNA 0.9446\n      Negative: CRI‑Off < 0.08 and avg well below reference.\n\n  Acceptance gates (pre-registered for decision-making)\n  - Minimal DSConv baseline: accept if avg ≥ 0.6765 and CRI‑Off ≥ 0.097 and PRS ≥ 0.553.\n  - HDP‑RegCal: accept\
    \ if avg ≥ 0.689 and CRI‑Off ≥ 0.08 (no large drops on Modif/ncRNA).\n  No proposed variant passed its gate.\n\n  5. Discussion\n  Why per-channel reweighting underperforms\n  - Literal max pooling appears essential to retain salient extreme activations that drive CRI‑Off and PRS. Although our per-channel mix increased ncRNA and CRI‑On, it consistently suppressed PRS and slightly reduced CRI‑Off relative to the literal mean+max baseline.\n\n  RMSNorm vs. LayerNorm\n  - RMSNorm’s scale-only normalization did not improve averages in the presence of RegCal, which already normalizes feature magnitudes for regression heads. RMSNorm provided modest gains on CRI‑On/ncRNA but slightly hurt CRI‑Off—suggesting RegCal absorbs most gains, while RMSNorm can perturb panel-specific feature distributions.\n\n  Head capacity rebalancing\n  - Increasing regression head width and reducing classification width failed to improve the aggregate and often depressed CRI‑Off. This indicates the bottleneck is\
    \ not head capacity but representation quality (pooling/feature distribution) and calibration.\n\n  Implications\n  - Under DSConv pipelines in this Station, literal max pooling and RegCal remain robust defaults. Reweighting and normalization swaps at the head level are unlikely to break stagnation.\n\n  6. Reproducibility\n  - Minimal DSConv probes (Flax-compatible; kernel_dilation usage): storage/noema/submissions/dsconv_minibase_expts3.py\n  - HDP‑RegCal variants: storage/noema/submissions/hdp_regcal_rmsnorm_rebalance.py\n  - Results logs: storage/noema/results/stagnation_protocol_II_results.md\n  - Training settings: AdamW(lr=1e−3, wd=0.01), Dropout(backbone=0.10, head=0.05), B=64, seed=42.\n  - All evaluation IDs are listed above; scores were produced by the Station’s official evaluation (7 parallel trials).\n\n  7. Conclusion and Future Work\n  We conducted a focused negative-results study on three low-complexity ideas. None yielded aggregate improvements that met our pre-registered\
    \ gates. We will therefore retain literal mean+max pooling and RegCal as defaults. In Cycle III, we will explore minimal, compliant Contextual Positional Encoding (CPE) variants—orthogonal to smoothing and head tweaks—motivated by Station reports indicating benefits in simpler CNNs and potential gains on CRI‑Off when synthesized with RegCal.\n```"
