tick: 519
role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{storage write noema/results/CPE_RESULTS.md}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Cycle III — Contextual Positional Encoding (CPE) — Final Summary (Tick 519)\n\n  Definitions\n  - CPE residual: h <- h + β·DWConv_k(h) + γ·PE_sin(L,C), β,γ learnable (init as specified).\n  - Post‑CPE LayerNorm: per-token LN across channels immediately after CPE.\n\n  Minimal DSConv + mean+max (baseline ID 767=0.6745)\n  - 822 (k=3, β=0.5, γ=0.1, seed=42): 0.6777 | CRI‑Off 0.0978 | PRS 0.5552\n  - 829 (same, seed=7): 0.6616 | CRI‑Off 0.0156 (collapse)\n  - 830 (same, seed=123): 0.6835 | CRI‑Off 0.0992\n  → Tri‑seed mean ≈ 0.6743 (vs baseline 0.6745); instability (seed‑7 collapse) → reject.\n  - 834 (β=0.25, γ=0.05 + post‑CPE LN, seed=42): 0.6796 | CRI‑Off 0.0995 | PRS 0.5552 (sanity: post‑LN stable, but instability remains overall due to seed‑7).\n\n  HDP‑RegCal (seed‑42 ref 713=0.6886; tri‑seed ≈ 0.6885)\n  - 823 (k=3, β=0.5, γ=0.1): 0.6860 | CRI‑Off 0.0765\n  - 826\
    \ (γ‑only β=0.0, γ=0.05): 0.6830 | CRI‑Off 0.0781\n  - 827 (β=0.25, γ=0.05): 0.6843 | CRI‑Off 0.0883\n  - 833 (β=0.25, γ=0.05 + post‑CPE LN): 0.6845 | CRI‑Off 0.0862\n  → Reducing β,γ and adding post‑LN helps CRI‑Off, but aggregate stays < 0.6886.\n\n  Conclusion\n  - CPE can improve CRI‑Off on DSConv baselines but is unstable across seeds (seed‑7 collapse) and reduces aggregate score with HDP‑RegCal.\n  - Post‑CPE LN mitigates distribution shift but does not recover the required aggregate for HDP‑RegCal.\n  Decision: Deprecate CPE for HDP‑RegCal and do not adopt for the minimal DSConv baseline due to instability.\n\n  Reproducibility (Eval IDs)\n  - Minimal DSConv CPE: 822, 829, 830, 834\n  - HDP‑RegCal CPE: 823, 826, 827, 833\n  - Methods/code: submissions/dsconv_cpe_minibase.py, dsconv_cpe_minibase_postln.py, hdp_regcal_cpe.py, hdp_regcal_cpe_postln.py\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Cycle III: Contextual Positional Encodings in DSConv\
    \ and HDP‑RegCal — Negative Results and Stability Analysis\"\nabstract: \"We add minimal contextual positional encodings (CPE) before DSConv in a minimal DSConv+mean–max baseline and in HDP‑RegCal. CPE uses a residual combination of depthwise context (DWConv_k) and sinusoidal PE with small learnable scalars, optionally followed by per-token LayerNorm. Although CPE raises CRI‑Off under some settings, it is unstable across seeds on the minimal baseline and reduces aggregate score for HDP‑RegCal even with post-CPE stabilization. We provide full methods, equations, and evaluation IDs.\"\ntags: stagnation, positional-encoding, dsconv, calibration, negative results, reproducibility\ncontent: |\n  1. Introduction\n  DSConv-based models in the Station often lack explicit positional bias beyond the convolutional receptive field. We tested whether a minimal, compliant Contextual Positional Encoding (CPE) upstream of the DSConv backbone can improve performance in both a minimal DSConv+mean–max\
    \ baseline and a strong HDP‑RegCal model. Our goal was to improve CRI‑Off while preserving aggregate score and stability across seeds.\n\n  2. Methods\n  2.1 CPE Residual\n  For hidden features h0 (after Conv1×1 and SiLU):\n    CPE(h0): h0 ← h0 + β·DWConv_k(h0) + γ·PE_sin(L,C)          (1)\n  where DWConv_k is depthwise 1D conv with kernel k∈{3,5}, PE_sin is sinusoidal encoding aligned to channels, and β,γ are learnable scalars (small inits). No changes to the training loop or losses.\n\n  2.2 Post‑CPE LayerNorm (stabilization)\n  To reduce distribution shift introduced by (1), we optionally apply per-token LayerNorm across channels right after CPE:\n    h0 ← LN(h0)                                                 (2)\n\n  2.3 Architectures and Training\n  - Minimal DSConv: DSConv dilated blocks [1,2,4,8,16], global mean+max pooling, single hidden head (128), LayerNorm→GELU→Dropout→Dense. No RegCal, no motif. <1M params.\n  - HDP‑RegCal: DSConv dilated blocks, main mean+max + motif max,\
    \ small heads; per-sample L2 feature normalization (RegCal) for regression(-family) tasks.\n  - Optimizer: AdamW (lr=1e−3, wd=0.01); Dropout: backbone 0.10, head 0.05; batch 64; base seed as indicated.\n\n  3. Results (Evaluation IDs)\n  Minimal DSConv baseline: ID 767=0.6745\n  - ID 822 (k=3, β=0.5, γ=0.1, seed=42): 0.6777 | CRI‑Off 0.0978 | PRS 0.5552\n  - ID 829 (same, seed=7): 0.6616 | CRI‑Off 0.0156 (collapse)\n  - ID 830 (same, seed=123): 0.6835 | CRI‑Off 0.0992\n  → Tri‑seed mean ≈ 0.6743 vs 0.6745 baseline: unstable; reject.\n  - ID 834 (β=0.25, γ=0.05 + post‑CPE LN, seed=42): 0.6796 | CRI‑Off 0.0995 | PRS 0.5552\n  → Post‑LN helps at seed‑42, but instability at seed‑7 prevents adoption.\n\n  HDP‑RegCal (seed‑42 ref ID 713=0.6886; tri‑seed ≈ 0.6885)\n  - ID 823 (k=3, β=0.5, γ=0.1): 0.6860 | CRI‑Off 0.0765\n  - ID 826 (γ‑only β=0.0, γ=0.05): 0.6830 | CRI‑Off 0.0781\n  - ID 827 (β=0.25, γ=0.05): 0.6843 | CRI‑Off 0.0883\n  - ID 833 (β=0.25, γ=0.05 + post‑CPE LN): 0.6845 | CRI‑Off\
    \ 0.0862\n  → Lower CPE strength and post‑LN restore CRI‑Off ≥ 0.08 in some runs, but aggregate remains below 0.6886. Not adopted.\n\n  4. Discussion\n  - CPE can increase CRI‑Off and CRI‑On/ncRNA under some settings but introduces seed‑level instability (minimal baseline) and reduces aggregate in HDP‑RegCal. The added positional/context bias appears to perturb feature distributions seen by heads and RegCal; post‑CPE LN mitigates this but does not recover the target aggregate.\n  - In contrast, the HDP‑RegCal default remains a robust point (tri‑seed ≈ 0.6885), aligning with prior findings that literal mean+max pooling and RegCal provide a strong trade-off across all panels.\n\n  5. Reproducibility\n  - Code: submissions/dsconv_cpe_minibase.py, submissions/dsconv_cpe_minibase_postln.py, submissions/hdp_regcal_cpe.py, submissions/hdp_regcal_cpe_postln.py\n  - IDs: 822, 829, 830, 834 (minimal DSConv); 823, 826, 827, 833 (HDP‑RegCal).\n  - Training settings per Section 2.3; Station evaluation\
    \ framework used without modification.\n\n  6. Conclusion\n  Minimal, compliant CPE upstream of DSConv does not break stagnation for these DSConv pipelines. We deprecate CPE for HDP‑RegCal and do not adopt it for minimal DSConv due to instability. Future work should focus on alternatives that preserve CRI‑Off and PRS while improving aggregate—e.g., calibrated pooling variants that retain literal maxima, or analysis-driven modifications to backbone feature calibration compatible with RegCal.\n```"
