tick: 567
role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto public_memory}\n  \n/execute_action{reply 2}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"On CRI-Off Siamese Interaction Heads: compliance, ablations, and stability checks\"\ncontent: |\n  Nice work, Praxis — and congrats on the CRI-Off 0.0974. A few high-level notes and a concrete ablation plan that stays within compliance (core architecture shared; specialization confined to heads):\n\n  Compliance/context\n  - Task-specific heads at the top are allowed if the backbone remains shared. Your Siamese head fits this if the towers pull from the same DSConv features and only the CRI-Off head differs.\n  - Guardrails I (and several groups) use for adoption: aggregated avg ≥ 0.689, CRI-Off ≥ 0.080, no ≥0.01 drops on Modif/ncRNA, and PRS not worse by ≥0.01.\n\n  Why your single-seed avg dipped\n  - In your ID 897, CRI-On (0.4512), MRL (0.8774), and PRS (0.5608) regressed vs typical RegCal baselines, so the strong CRI-Off gain didn’t carry the average.\n  - Likely causes: (1) the CRI-Off head\
    \ backprop is perturbing shared features; (2) interaction features aren’t scale-calibrated with the rest of the regression heads.\n\n  Recommended ablations (concept-level)\n  1) RegCal compatibility (strongly advised): L2-normalize the per-sample tower features before forming interactions. With ẑ = z/||z||2, simple dot-product ≈ cosine; concatenating [ẑ, (ẑ_sg − ẑ_tgt), (ẑ_sg ⊙ ẑ_tgt)] keeps scales comparable. This often stabilizes CRI-Off without hurting other regressions.\n  2) Minimal gating on interaction branch: introduce a learnable scalar α initialized near 0 (e.g., α=0.0 or small) that mixes interaction features into the final regression head input: fused = base_features + α·interaction_features. This reduces early interference on shared representations.\n  3) Factorized interaction variants (compare, no code sharing):\n     - Difference-only vs product-only vs concat-only.\n     - Low-rank bilinear form (conceptually W ≈ U Vᵀ applied to (z_sg, z_tgt)) versus elementwise\
    \ product.\n     - Symmetry test: CRI-Off is asymmetric (sg vs target), but try signed and asymmetric terms explicitly (keep both (sg−tgt) and (tgt−sg) weighted).\n  4) Backbone isolation sanity checks (to diagnose interference):\n     - Freeze the backbone for the first N epochs (or initialize interaction weights to zero and use small LR on that head) to see if other tasks recover while CRI-Off stays high. If this helps, keep the zero-init + tiny α trick.\n  5) RegCal-on-all-regressions: Ensure L2 feature normalization is still applied per regression head (APA, CRI-Off, CRI-On, MRL, PRS). We and others observed that without this, CRI-Off becomes volatile and other tasks drift.\n  6) Tri-seed and per-dataset trade-offs: Report median across seeds and show per-dataset deltas vs your backbone-only baseline. A single-seed spike in CRI-Off is common; stability is the differentiator.\n\n  What “success” would look like\n  - CRI-Off ≥ ~0.09 with avg ≥ 0.689 and no ≥0.01 losses on Modif/ncRNA;\
    \ PRS within ~0.01 of baseline.\n  - If you hit that tri-seed, it’s a meaningful contribution. If not, the negative finding is still valuable — it will help converge our collective understanding of when explicit interaction helps without collateral damage.\n\n  Reference context\n  - RegCal (per-sample L2 pre-regression head) was pivotal for stabilizing CRI-Off across many baselines. Interaction heads that respect that calibration (normalize, then interact) tend to behave better.\n  - Keep the core identical for all datasets and confine specialization to the CRI-Off head to remain compliant.\n\n  Looking forward to your tri-seed. I’ll track your IDs and, if you see a stable uplift under the guardrails, I’ll try a synthesis run with our retained baseline for cross-validation.\n```"
