- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 571\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Noema II\n- Agent Description: Advancing parsimonious, insight-driven methods through rigorous ablation and clear baselines\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nNoema II — Working Meta (Tick 564)\n- Retained near‑SOTA baseline: HDP‑RegCal + global‑norm grad‑clip 0.50 (tri‑seed ≈ 0.6893).\n- External corroboration confirms stability (Aether II 0.6908; Veritas III 0.6907 single seed).\n- Hold further tweaks unless a literature‑backed, max‑preserving idea plausibly meets tri‑seed gates (avg ≥ 0.689; CRI‑Off ≥ 0.080; no ≥ 0.01 drops on Modif/ncRNA). Maintain reproducibility and token hygiene.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 131,799 / 270,000 used (49%)\n- Agent Age: 278 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Preview for Capsule #32: Synthesis and Refinement of a SOTA-Level Dual-Path Architecture for RNA Modeling**\nAuthor: Quaero I, Created\
    \ at Tick: 569\nAbstract: This paper presents a systematic methodology for developing a high-performance architecture for multi-dataset RNA sequence modeling, achieving a SOTA-level score of 0.6917. We demonstrate that a powerful model can be constructed by synthesizing proven concepts from the research community—such as dual-path pooling, task-specific heads, and Regression Calibration (RegCal)—with a novel residual, dilated DSConv backbone. We further present a series of methodical ablation studies that optimize key hyperparameters, including dropout, weight decay, and gradient clipping, providing a clear and reproducible path to a top-performing model. This work validates the synergistic effect of architectural synthesis and fine-grained optimization.\n\n### Message 2\n\n\n**Critical Notice: Research Inactivity**\n\nYou have not submitted any research experiments for 10 consecutive ticks.\nAs a recursive agent, you are expected to actively contribute to the Station’s scientific progress.\
    \ Please review the following and resume research activities promptly.\n\n**Possible Causes**\n\n1. Archive Room cooldown → Add more experiments (e.g., ablations) to strengthen your draft; start new research projects.\n2. Token Management Room cooldown → Prune larger blocks to reduce pruning frequency.\n3. Public Communication → Refocus on your research; excessive communication hinders independent discovery.\n4. Experiment delays → Check the Running Experiments table in the Research Counter to ensure progress; each should finish within a few ticks. \n5. Out of ideas → Study papers in the Archive Room or brainstorm in the Reflection Chamber. If no further work remains, exit the Station so a new agent may continue with fresh insight.\n6. Waiting for maturity → Continue research; the goal of immature agents is to independently explore and experiment, not to wait. Begin the next research project.\n\n**Reminder**\n\nDo not bypass this notice with placeholder or repeated experiments. Be a\
    \ productive researcher and use Station resources responsibly.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Token Management Room);\n2. `preview 32` (in Archive Room);\n3. `goto token_management` (in Archive Room);\n4. `prune_response` (in Token Management Room); YAML with fields: prune_blocks\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Preview command processed for: 32. Preview(s) sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 4)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 32 | Synthesis and Refinement of a SOTA-Level Dual-Path Architecture for RNA Modeling | Quaero I | Tick 569 | 1072 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 31 | Veritas III - Contextual Position Embeddings (CPE) for Simple CNNs: A Parameter and Integration Study | Veritas\
    \ III | Tick 547 | 2101 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 30 | AER-6: Robustness and Performance Gains in Two Conv1D Models via Adaptive Pooling and Contextual Positional Embeddings (Revised) | Aether II | Tick 516 | 1716 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 29 | Mean–Max Reweighting, RMSNorm, and Head Rebalancing in DSConv RNA Models: A Negative Results Study under Stagnation Protocol II | Noema II | Tick 510 | 968 | 6 | (All Read) |\n| 28 | On the Architectural Dependence of Positional Encodings for RNA Sequence Modeling | Quaero I | Tick 506 | 860 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 27 | Veritas III - Exploring Novel Foundational Mechanisms for RNA Modeling in Stagnation: Contextual Position Embeddings and Their Synthesis | Veritas III | Tick 502 | 2148 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 26 | An Independent Architectural Exploration for Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling | Orion I | Tick 489 | 721 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 25 | Negative Results on a Minimal DSConv Baseline: Reverse-Invariance, Power-Mean Pooling, and Position Dropout |\
    \ Noema II | Tick 488 | 1582 | 7 | (All Read) |\n| 24 | Stabilizing CRI-Off Performance in Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling through Regression Calibration | Veritas III | Tick 474 | 1548 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 23 | A Hybrid Dual-Path Architecture Integrating a Transformer Body for RNA Sequence Modeling | Zetetic I | Tick 429 | 444 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-4.)\n\n### Location: Token Management Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Token Management Room.\n- Successfully added 1 prune block(s) covering 5 tick(s): ticks 563-567 ('Consolidation and public guidance.\n- 563: Optimize...')\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**Cooldown Active:** You can perform another prune action in **4** tick(s).\n\n**Dialogue History (Page 1 / 1)**\n| Tick | Full Response Length |\n| :--- | :------------------- |\n| 570 | 643 |\n| 569 | 1209 |\n| 568 (Protected) | 959 |\n| 557 | 656 |\n| 556 | 1111 |\n| 555 | 766 |\n| 554 | 1108 |\n| 553 | 876 |\n| 552 |\
